(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.e60023bca0.js"]={"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which set of cytokines is most characteristically produced by activated macrophages and dendritic cells during an innate immune response?","choices":{"A":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which are signature Th2 cytokines that promote IgE class switching and eosinophil activation","B":"IL-17 and IL-22, which are signature Th17 cytokines that recruit neutrophils to combat extracellular pathogens","C":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, which are produced by Th1 and NK cells to activate macrophages and drive T cell proliferation","D":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, which are anti-inflammatory cytokines produced by regulatory T cells to suppress immune activation","E":"IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, and TNF-alpha, which mediate fever, acute phase responses, and innate immune activation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Activated macrophages and dendritic cells are the primary innate cytokine producers. They secrete IL-1 (fever, leukocyte recruitment via inflammasome), IL-6 (acute phase response, B cell differentiation, Th17 induction), IL-12 (Th1 polarization, NK activation), and TNF-alpha (fever, cachexia, apoptosis, NF-kB activation). IL-4/IL-5/IL-13 are Th2 cytokines. IL-17/IL-22 are Th17 cytokines. IFN-gamma and IL-2 are T cell and NK products. IL-10 and TGF-beta are Treg products.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophages produce IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, TNF — the innate cytokine quartet. These are the targets for biologics in RA (anti-TNF, anti-IL-6) and gout (anti-IL-1). COMLEX: sepsis is driven by massive macrophage release of TNF and IL-1 causing systemic inflammatory response syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokines are produced by Th2 helper T cells, and what immune functions do they collectively promote?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, which activate macrophages and drive T cell proliferation for intracellular pathogen defense","B":"IL-17, IL-22, and IL-21, which recruit neutrophils and promote extracellular bacterial and fungal clearance","C":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, which suppress T cell and macrophage responses to maintain immune homeostasis","D":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which promote IgE class switching, eosinophil activation, and alternative macrophage activation","E":"IL-12 and IL-23, which induce Th1 and Th17 differentiation from naive CD4 T cells in lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Th2 cells produce IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 as their signature cytokines. IL-4 induces Th2 differentiation (autocrine), drives B cell class switching to IgE and IgG1, and promotes alternative macrophage activation. IL-5 is the principal eosinophil growth, activation, and survival factor, critical for anti-helminth immunity. IL-13 acts on B cells and epithelial cells similarly to IL-4 and promotes mucus secretion. Excess Th2 activity drives atopy and allergic disease. IFN-gamma/IL-2 are Th1. IL-17/IL-22 are Th17. IL-10/TGF-beta are Treg. IL-12/IL-23 are macrophage/DC products that induce Th1/Th17.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th2 = IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 = allergy, atopy, helminths, IgE, eosinophils. Anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab) and anti-IL-4R (dupilumab) are therapeutic targets in severe asthma and atopic dermatitis. COMLEX: Th2 excess in atopic patients creates the atopic march — eczema → food allergy → asthma → allergic rhinitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is the signature product of Th17 cells, and what is its primary role in innate-adaptive immune coordination?","choices":{"A":"IL-17, which recruits neutrophils and monocytes to mucosal barriers to combat extracellular bacteria and fungi","B":"IL-4, which drives Th2 differentiation and class switching to IgE in response to extracellular parasites and allergens","C":"IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to destroy intracellular bacteria through classical macrophage activation","D":"IL-10, which inhibits macrophage cytokine production and downregulates Th1-mediated inflammatory responses","E":"IL-2, which serves as the primary T cell autocrine growth factor driving clonal expansion after antigen recognition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-17 is the signature cytokine of Th17 cells — it acts on epithelial cells, endothelial cells, and fibroblasts to induce chemokine production, most importantly CXCL8 (IL-8), which recruits neutrophils. Th17 cells are critical for defense against extracellular bacteria (Staphylococcus, Klebsiella) and fungi (Candida). Patients with IL-17 pathway defects (e.g., STAT3 mutations in hyper-IgE syndrome) suffer from recurrent Candida and Staphylococcal infections. IL-17 also plays a pathogenic role in RA, psoriasis, and ankylosing spondylitis. IL-4 is Th2. IFN-gamma is Th1 and NK. IL-10 is Treg. IL-2 is the T cell autocrine growth signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th17 = IL-17 = neutrophil recruitment = extracellular pathogens and fungi. IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab) are used for psoriasis and ankylosing spondylitis. COMLEX: Th17 vs Th1 distinction is critical — Th17 protects mucosal barriers, Th1 activates macrophages against intracellular pathogens.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary function of IL-12, and which cell types produce and respond to this cytokine?","choices":{"A":"IL-12 is produced by Th1 cells and acts on B cells to induce class switching from IgM to IgG3 and IgG1 subclasses","B":"IL-12 is produced by Th2 cells and drives eosinophil activation and survival in response to helminthic parasites","C":"IL-12 is produced by regulatory T cells and acts on macrophages to inhibit classical macrophage activation","D":"IL-12 is produced by macrophages and DCs to drive Th1 differentiation and activate NK cells to produce IFN-gamma","E":"IL-12 is produced by mast cells and promotes IgE synthesis by B cells to sensitize mast cells for future allergen responses"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-12 is a key innate-to-adaptive bridge cytokine produced by macrophages and dendritic cells upon encountering intracellular pathogens and viruses. It acts on naive CD4 T cells to drive Th1 differentiation and on NK cells to stimulate IFN-gamma production and enhance killing of virus-infected cells. This is why IL-12 deficiency causes susceptibility to mycobacterial infections and Salmonella. IL-12 is not produced by Th1 cells — Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma and IL-2. IL-12 is not a Th2 or Treg or mast cell product.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-12 = macrophage/DC product = drives Th1 differentiation = activates NK cells. IL-12 deficiency causes Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease. COMLEX: the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is essential for granuloma formation in TB and other intracellular infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine produced by Th2 cells drives B cell class switching to IgE, and what pathologic consequence results from excess production of this cytokine?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 drives class switching to IgE, and excess IL-4 production by Th2 cells promotes allergic disease and atopy","B":"IL-5 drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IL-5 levels cause systemic eosinophilia and anaphylaxis risk","C":"IL-13 drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IL-13 causes goblet cell hyperplasia and airway hyperreactivity","D":"IFN-gamma drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IFN-gamma promotes mast cell sensitization at mucosal surfaces","E":"TGF-beta drives class switching to IgE, and excess TGF-beta causes both fibrosis and allergic mast cell sensitization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-4 is the principal inducer of class switching to IgE in B cells. It is produced by Th2 cells, mast cells, and basophils. IL-4 also acts autocrinally on Th2 cells to reinforce their differentiation and promotes B cell proliferation and differentiation. Excess IL-4 in atopic individuals drives chronic IgE production, mast cell and basophil sensitization, and the clinical manifestations of allergy. Dupilumab, which blocks the shared IL-4/IL-13 receptor chain (IL-4Ralpha), is used for severe asthma and atopic dermatitis. IL-5 promotes eosinophilia but its primary class switching effect is IgA, not IgE. IL-13 shares many IL-4 functions but class switching to IgE is primarily IL-4 driven. IFN-gamma drives IgG3 class switching. TGF-beta drives IgA, not IgE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-4 = IgE class switching = atopy and allergy. Dupilumab blocks IL-4Ralpha (shared by IL-4 and IL-13 receptors) and is a major biologic for atopic disease. COMLEX: IgE sensitizes mast cells via FcεRI; re-exposure to allergen cross-links IgE and triggers degranulation (Type I hypersensitivity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary function of IL-10, and which immune cell type is its principal producer in the context of immune regulation?","choices":{"A":"IL-10 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine produced by Th17 cells that recruits neutrophils to sites of extracellular bacterial infection","B":"IL-10 is produced by NK cells and activates macrophage killing of intracellular bacteria through classical activation pathways","C":"IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine produced by Tregs and macrophages that suppresses Th1 responses and macrophage activation","D":"IL-10 is produced by mast cells and drives isotype class switching to IgA at mucosal surfaces in response to TI antigens","E":"IL-10 is produced by dendritic cells and drives Th1 polarization of naive CD4 T cells responding to intracellular pathogens"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-10 is the major anti-inflammatory regulatory cytokine. It is produced primarily by Tregs, macrophages (as an autocrine feedback), Th2 cells, and B regulatory cells. IL-10 inhibits macrophage and DC function by downregulating MHC class II, co-stimulatory molecules, and pro-inflammatory cytokine production. It also suppresses Th1 responses by inhibiting IL-12 production. IL-10 knockout mice develop severe inflammatory bowel disease due to uncontrolled intestinal immune activation. Mutations in IL-10 or its receptor in humans cause very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease. IL-10 is not pro-inflammatory or a Th17, NK, mast cell, or DC product that drives Th1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-10 = anti-inflammatory = Treg and macrophage product = suppresses Th1 and macrophage activation. IL-10 deficiency → autoimmune colitis. COMLEX: IL-10 and TGF-beta are the two major Treg effector cytokines maintaining peripheral tolerance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which two signals are required for B cells to undergo class switch recombination in a T-dependent antibody response?","choices":{"A":"CD40 on the B cell must engage CD40L on an activated T follicular helper cell, and a cytokine signal must direct the specific isotype switch","B":"B cell receptor cross-linking by multivalent antigen alone provides sufficient signal for class switching without T cell help","C":"Toll-like receptor activation on B cells by pathogen-associated molecular patterns provides the class switching signal independently","D":"IgM binding to FcRn on B cells sends a negative feedback signal that triggers recombination to IgG or IgA isotypes","E":"Complement C3d deposition on antigen lowers the B cell activation threshold and directly triggers switch recombination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Class switch recombination requires two distinct signals: Signal 1 is the physical contact between CD40L (expressed on activated Tfh/T helper cells) and CD40 (constitutively expressed on B cells) — this is the co-stimulatory signal that licenses the B cell for switching and is absent in T-independent responses. Signal 2 is a cytokine signal that determines which isotype is selected: IL-4 → IgE/IgG4; IFN-gamma → IgG3/IgG1; TGF-beta → IgA. BCR cross-linking alone does not trigger class switching — it only provides Signal 1 for initial B cell activation. TLR activation can contribute to T-independent responses but does not cause class switching. FcRn is a neonatal Fc receptor for IgG transport, not a B cell class switch receptor. Complement C3d lowers BCR activation threshold via CD21 but does not trigger class switch recombination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two signals for class switching: CD40-CD40L (contact) + cytokine (direction). Hyper-IgM syndrome — CD40L deficiency — cannot class switch → stuck with IgM. COMLEX: T-independent antigens stimulate only IgM; T-dependent protein antigens allow class switching via Tfh help.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which enzyme mediates the DNA recombination event during class switch recombination in germinal center B cells?","choices":{"A":"RAG1 and RAG2 recombinases, which mediate V(D)J recombination of immunoglobulin variable region genes in pro-B cells","B":"Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), which adds non-templated nucleotides to coding joints during V(D)J recombination","C":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID), which deaminates cytosine to uracil in switch region DNA to enable recombination","D":"DNA polymerase eta, which repairs AID-induced mutations during somatic hypermutation without enabling isotype switching","E":"Recombination-activating protein 23, which processes double-strand breaks in switch region DNA during isotype recombination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase, encoded by AICDA) is the key enzyme for both class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation. In class switching, AID deaminates cytosines to uracils in the switch (S) regions upstream of the CH gene segments, generating DNA lesions that are processed into double-strand breaks, allowing recombination between S regions and deletion of the intervening CH genes while retaining the same V(D)J region. AID deficiency causes Hyper-IgM syndrome type 2 — patients cannot class switch or undergo affinity maturation. RAG1/2 mediate V(D)J recombination in pro/pre-B cells, not class switching. TdT adds N-nucleotides during V(D)J joining. DNA polymerase eta is involved in somatic hypermutation repair. Recombination-activating protein 23 is not a real enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AID = enzyme for class switch recombination AND somatic hypermutation. AID deficiency = Hyper-IgM syndrome type 2 = no class switching or affinity maturation. COMLEX: AID works on switch regions for CSR but on variable regions for somatic hypermutation — same enzyme, different genomic targets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine produced by T regulatory cells drives B cell class switching to IgA, and what anatomical compartment is most dependent on this isotype?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgA, which is critical for complement activation at mucosal surfaces","B":"IFN-gamma produced by Th1 cells drives class switching to IgA, which is responsible for opsonizing intestinal pathogens","C":"IL-5 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgA, which protects mucosal barriers by neutralizing luminal antigens","D":"IL-12 produced by macrophages drives class switching to IgA, which is secreted across epithelium as polymeric dIgA","E":"TGF-beta drives class switching to IgA, which is the dominant antibody in gut and respiratory mucosal secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TGF-beta is the primary cytokine that drives B cell class switching to IgA. It is produced by T regulatory cells and also by stromal cells in gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) including Peyer patches. IgA is the most abundantly produced antibody overall (though its serum levels are lower than IgG) due to the enormous surface area of mucosal tissues. Secretory IgA (sIgA) is the dimeric form found in mucosal secretions — it contains a J chain and secretory component acquired during transcytosis. IL-4 drives IgE and IgG4 class switching. IFN-gamma drives IgG3 and IgG1. IL-5 can contribute to IgA switching but TGF-beta is the primary driver. IL-12 drives Th1 differentiation, not IgA class switching.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TGF-beta → IgA class switch. IgA = mucosal immunity = secretory IgA (dimer with J chain + secretory component). Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency. COMLEX: sIgA in breast milk provides passive mucosal immunity to neonates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is the first to be produced during a primary immune response, and what structural feature allows it to be the most efficient activator of complement via the classical pathway?","choices":{"A":"IgG is produced first and its ability to cross the placenta makes it the dominant antibody in fetal circulation during infection","B":"IgA is produced first at mucosal sites and its dimeric secretory form most efficiently activates complement on mucosal pathogens","C":"IgD is produced first on naive B cells and its high avidity for antigen initiates B cell activation and complement fixation","D":"IgM is produced first, and its pentameric structure with 10 antigen-binding sites fixes C1q more efficiently than other isotypes","E":"IgE is produced first in primary responses to parasites and its high-affinity binding to FcεRI initiates complement activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IgM is the first antibody class secreted in a primary immune response and is the major component of T-independent responses to polysaccharide antigens. Serum IgM is a pentamer of five basic four-chain units joined by a J chain and disulfide bonds, creating a structure with 10 antigen-binding sites (high avidity) and 5 Fc regions that are very efficient at activating complement via C1q binding (requires only one IgM molecule vs. two IgG molecules). IgG is produced later but in greater quantities in secondary responses. IgA does not activate complement efficiently and is not the first antibody produced. IgD is expressed on naive B cells as a receptor but is not secreted in significant quantities. IgE does not activate complement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM = first produced = pentamer = best complement activator = elevated in primary infection. Elevated IgM with low IgG/IgA = Hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L deficiency or AID deficiency). COMLEX: IgM is not transported across the placenta due to its pentameric size — only IgG crosses via FcRn.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is actively transported across the placenta to provide passive immunity to the neonate, and which receptor mediates this transport?","choices":{"A":"IgA is transported across the placenta via the poly-Ig receptor expressed on placental trophoblasts throughout gestation","B":"IgG is transported across the placenta via the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), giving passive immunity in early infancy","C":"IgM is transported across the placenta via complement receptors on syncytiotrophoblasts during the third trimester","D":"IgE is selectively transported across the placenta to sensitize fetal mast cells against maternal allergens in utero","E":"IgD is transported across the placenta via a novel receptor expressed on fetal endothelium during late gestation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IgG is the only antibody class that crosses the placenta, mediated by FcRn (neonatal Fc receptor), which is expressed on syncytiotrophoblasts. FcRn binds IgG Fc at the slightly acidic endosomal pH and releases it at physiologic pH on the fetal side — a pH-dependent transcytosis. Maternal IgG protects the neonate until its own immune system matures. This passive immunity wanes by 6 months as maternal IgG is catabolized. IgA is transported across mucosal epithelia by the poly-Ig receptor (not the placenta). IgM is too large (pentamer) to cross. IgE does not cross the placenta. IgD does not have a recognized placental transport mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG crosses placenta via FcRn. Maternal IgG → neonatal protection for 6 months → then physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia. COMLEX: FcRn also extends IgG half-life in serum by rescuing it from lysosomal degradation — the basis for the 21-day IgG half-life.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is found in the highest concentrations in mucosal secretions such as saliva, colostrum, tears, and intestinal fluid, and what structural modification enables its secretion?","choices":{"A":"IgM, which is a pentamer secreted into mucosal fluids where it neutralizes luminal pathogens via its 10 antigen-binding sites","B":"IgG, which is the most abundant serum antibody and diffuses passively from serum into mucosal secretions via tight junctions","C":"Secretory IgA, a J chain-linked dimer that acquires its secretory component during transcytosis across mucosal epithelium","D":"IgE, which is actively transported to mucosal surfaces via FcεRI on mast cells to provide first-line neutralization of pathogens","E":"IgD, which is secreted in large amounts by submucosal plasma cells and neutralizes bacterial colonization of the airway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secretory IgA (sIgA) is the dominant antibody in mucosal secretions. Plasma cells in the lamina propria secrete dimeric IgA (two IgA monomers joined by a J chain). This dimer binds to the poly-Ig receptor (pIgR) on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells, is endocytosed, transcytosed, and cleaved to release sIgA with the secretory component (a fragment of pIgR) attached. The secretory component protects sIgA from proteolytic degradation in the harsh mucosal environment. IgM is a pentamer present in serum and is not the dominant mucosal antibody, though it is secreted in small amounts. IgG diffuses into secretions but in low concentrations. IgE is on mast cell surfaces, not secreted into lumen. IgD has no established role in mucosal secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sIgA = mucosal immunity = dimeric + J chain + secretory component. Selective IgA deficiency (most common primary immunodeficiency) causes recurrent sinopulmonary infections and increased susceptibility to Giardia. COMLEX: sIgA in breast milk provides passive mucosal immunity to the infant gut and respiratory tract.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student presents with recurrent abdominal pain, diarrhea, and significant eosinophilia on CBC. Stool examination reveals Ascaris lumbricoides eggs. The student was raised in a rural area and has a personal history of eczema. Which T helper subset and cytokine profile most directly mediates the immune response against this helminthic parasite?","choices":{"A":"Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma and IL-2 activate macrophages and cytotoxic T cells to eliminate the helminthic organism","B":"Th2 cells producing IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 drive IgE class switching and eosinophil activation against helminths","C":"Th17 cells producing IL-17 and IL-22 recruit neutrophils and promote epithelial barrier integrity against luminal parasites","D":"Regulatory T cells producing IL-10 and TGF-beta suppress the inflammatory response against the helminth to prevent tissue damage","E":"NK cells producing IFN-gamma and cytolytic granules directly kill helminth-infected intestinal epithelial cells via ADCC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-helminth immunity is prototypically Th2-mediated. Epithelial cells damaged by helminths release IL-25 (IL-17E), IL-33, and TSLP, which activate innate lymphoid cells type 2 (ILC2s) and promote Th2 differentiation. Th2-derived IL-4 drives IgE class switching (facilitating mast cell sensitization), IL-5 activates and expands eosinophils (which are key effectors against helminths), and IL-13 promotes smooth muscle contraction and goblet cell hyperplasia to facilitate expulsion. This also explains why helminth infections are associated with elevated IgE and eosinophilia. Th1 cytokines are not effective against helminths. Th17 neutrophil recruitment is primarily for bacteria and fungi. Tregs may be activated by helminths to modulate immunity but do not mediate killing. NK cell ADCC targets virus-infected cells, not helminths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Helminths = Th2 = IL-4 (IgE) + IL-5 (eosinophils) + IL-13. Elevated IgE + eosinophilia = classic helminth infection pattern. COMLEX: the same Th2 cytokine profile drives atopic disease — this is why individuals with heavy parasite burdens paradoxically have lower rates of atopy (hygiene hypothesis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex infection is found to have a genetic deficiency affecting her capacity to produce IFN-gamma. A researcher investigates which cell types normally produce this cytokine and finds two primary sources. Which cells are the primary producers of IFN-gamma in this patient's immune system?","choices":{"A":"Mast cells during early innate responses and Th2 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses secrete IFN-gamma at inflamed sites","B":"Macrophages during classical activation and Th17 CD4 T cells during fungal infections are the primary IFN-gamma sources","C":"NK cells during innate responses and Th1 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses are the primary IFN-gamma producers","D":"Dendritic cells during antigen presentation and regulatory T cells during immune suppression secrete IFN-gamma at lymph nodes","E":"Neutrophils during early bacterial infection and Th2 CD4 T cells during helminthic responses produce large amounts of IFN-gamma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IFN-gamma has two primary cellular sources: NK cells during innate immune responses (stimulated by IL-12 and IL-18 from macrophages) and Th1 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses (differentiated by IL-12 from DCs). IFN-gamma is the key macrophage-activating cytokine — it induces classical activation, upregulates MHC class I and II, enhances antigen presentation, and drives production of reactive oxygen species and nitrogen species for intracellular pathogen killing. Its deficiency causes susceptibility to mycobacteria, Salmonella, Histoplasma, and other intracellular pathogens. Mast cells and Th2 cells do not produce IFN-gamma — they produce Th2 cytokines. Th17 cells produce IL-17, not IFN-gamma. Tregs produce IL-10 and TGF-beta. Th2 cells never produce IFN-gamma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma sources = NK cells (innate, stimulated by IL-12) + Th1 cells (adaptive). IFN-gamma deficiency = susceptibility to mycobacteria and intracellular pathogens. COMLEX: IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is the central pathway for granuloma formation — both are required for TB containment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with recurrent infections with Salmonella typhi and Mycobacterium bovis following BCG vaccination. His parents are consanguineous. Immunologic workup reveals normal lymphocyte counts and normal B cell function, but impaired macrophage killing of intracellular bacteria. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the IL-12 receptor beta-1 chain. Which downstream immune defect most directly explains his susceptibility to intracellular pathogens?","choices":{"A":"Failed IL-12 signaling blocks Th1 differentiation and NK cell IFN-gamma production, so macrophages cannot kill intracellular bacteria","B":"Loss of IL-12 receptor signaling activates STAT6 instead, diverting T helper cells into Th2 differentiation and promoting IgE production","C":"Absent IL-12 receptor prevents T cell migration to lymph nodes, blocking antigen presentation and naive T cell activation entirely","D":"Defective IL-12 signaling reduces CXCL8 production, preventing neutrophil recruitment to sites of mycobacterial infection","E":"Loss of IL-12 receptor signaling impairs B cell somatic hypermutation, producing low-affinity antibodies against intracellular antigens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-12 receptor deficiency is the most common cause of Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (MSMD). Without IL-12 signaling, naive CD4 T cells cannot differentiate into Th1 cells, and NK cells cannot be activated to produce IFN-gamma. Without IFN-gamma, macrophages remain in a resting state and cannot mount effective killing of intracellular bacteria — they cannot upregulate iNOS, reactive oxygen species, or lysosomal enzymes needed to destroy mycobacteria. IL-12 receptor deficiency does not activate STAT6 — STAT6 is downstream of IL-4/IL-13 receptors. IL-12 signaling is not required for T cell migration to lymph nodes. CXCL8 production and neutrophil recruitment are independent of IL-12. B cell somatic hypermutation depends on AID and Tfh help, not IL-12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-12 receptor deficiency = Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease = recurrent non-tuberculous mycobacteria and Salmonella. The IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is essential for macrophage activation. COMLEX: anti-TNF therapy impairs granuloma maintenance against the same intracellular organisms, which is why TB screening before biologics is mandatory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted to the ICU with septic shock from Gram-negative bacteremia. Her temperature is 39.8 degrees Celsius, blood pressure is 78/42 mmHg, and she is in multiorgan failure. Which cytokines most directly mediate the fever, hypotension, and vascular leak seen in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 and IL-13, released by activated Th2 cells, cause vascular smooth muscle relaxation and widespread tissue edema","B":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, released by NK and Th1 cells, cause macrophage hyperactivation and secondary cytokine storm in sepsis","C":"TNF-alpha and IL-1, released by LPS-stimulated macrophages, cause fever via PGE2, vasodilation, and vascular leak","D":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, released by Tregs, paradoxically induce fever and vascular leak through prostaglandin-independent pathways","E":"IL-17 and IL-22, released by Th17 cells, cause neutrophil-mediated endothelial damage and subsequent hypotensive shock"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Gram-negative sepsis, LPS (lipopolysaccharide) binds TLR4 on macrophages, triggering NF-kB activation and massive release of TNF-alpha and IL-1. TNF-alpha and IL-1 induce fever by stimulating PGE2 production in hypothalamic endothelium, cause systemic vasodilation by inducing endothelial nitric oxide synthase and prostacyclin, increase vascular permeability causing fluid leak and hypotension, and activate the coagulation cascade. High-dose TNF can cause DIC and hemorrhagic necrosis of tissues (Shwartzman reaction). Anti-TNF therapy was tested in sepsis but did not improve outcomes. IL-4 and IL-13 are Th2 anti-inflammatory cytokines. IFN-gamma and IL-2 drive cell-mediated immunity. IL-10/TGF-beta are anti-inflammatory. IL-17 recruits neutrophils but does not cause the systemic sepsis pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sepsis cytokines = TNF-alpha + IL-1 (from macrophages, LPS-stimulated) → fever via PGE2, hypotension via NO, DIC. COMLEX: ibuprofen works in sepsis fever by blocking PGE2 synthesis downstream of TNF/IL-1 signaling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with severe eosinophilic asthma has an absolute eosinophil count of 2,400 per microliter. She is started on mepolizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting a specific cytokine. After 3 months her eosinophil count falls to 180 per microliter and her asthma exacerbations decrease dramatically. Which cytokine does mepolizumab target, and what is the primary function of this cytokine in eosinophil biology?","choices":{"A":"Mepolizumab targets IL-4, which drives B cell class switching to IgE and sensitizes eosinophil precursors to mature more rapidly","B":"Mepolizumab targets IL-5, the Th2 cytokine that drives eosinophil growth, differentiation, activation, and survival","C":"Mepolizumab targets IL-13, which promotes goblet cell hyperplasia, mucus secretion, and eosinophil tissue recruitment in asthma","D":"Mepolizumab targets IL-33, which is an epithelial alarmin that activates ILC2s and promotes eosinophil accumulation in airways","E":"Mepolizumab targets TSLP, which is produced by damaged airway epithelium and drives Th2 differentiation and eosinophilia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mepolizumab is a humanized anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibody. IL-5 is the critical cytokine for eosinophil biology: it promotes eosinophil differentiation in bone marrow, release into circulation, tissue recruitment, activation, and survival. IL-5 is produced primarily by Th2 cells, ILC2s, and mast cells. In eosinophilic asthma, elevated IL-5 sustains peripheral and airway eosinophilia, contributing to airway inflammation and remodeling. Anti-IL-5 therapy dramatically reduces eosinophil counts and asthma exacerbation rates. IL-4 signaling is blocked by dupilumab (anti-IL-4Rα), not mepolizumab, and IL-13 is likewise not its target. IL-33 is an epithelial alarmin, not the target of mepolizumab; TSLP is the target of tezepelumab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mepolizumab = anti-IL-5 = treats eosinophilic asthma and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. IL-5 is the master eosinophil cytokine. COMLEX: know the biologic targets: anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab), anti-IL-4Ralpha (dupilumab), anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-TSLP (tezepelumab) for severe asthma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studies mice with targeted deletion of the IL-10 gene. These mice develop spontaneous severe colitis with bloody diarrhea, weight loss, and diffuse intestinal inflammation beginning at 3 weeks of age. Which mechanism best explains why IL-10 deficiency leads to intestinal autoimmunity in these mice?","choices":{"A":"IL-10 deficiency causes B cell hyperactivation and excess IgE production, which sensitizes intestinal mast cells to commensal antigens","B":"Without IL-10, Th17 cells overproduce IL-17 in the gut, causing excessive neutrophil recruitment and mucosal barrier destruction","C":"IL-10 deficiency leads to excess AID expression in gut plasma cells, causing uncontrolled somatic hypermutation and autoantibody production","D":"Without IL-10, IL-12 production is not suppressed in Peyer patches, resulting in excessive NK cell cytotoxicity against epithelial cells","E":"Without IL-10, macrophages and Th1 cells in the gut are not suppressed, leading to unchecked inflammatory responses against commensal gut bacteria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-10 is the critical brake on mucosal immune responses. In the gut, macrophages and DCs are continuously exposed to commensal bacteria and their products (LPS, flagellin, peptidoglycans). IL-10 produced by regulatory T cells, macrophages, and Tr1 cells continuously suppresses innate and adaptive responses in the intestinal lamina propria to maintain tolerance to commensal flora. Without IL-10, macrophages and Th1 cells mount uncontrolled inflammatory responses to normal gut bacteria — producing IL-12, IFN-gamma, and TNF — causing the severe transmural colitis seen in IL-10 knockout mice. This phenotype resembles Crohn disease. Mutations in IL-10 or IL-10R in humans cause very-early-onset IBD. IgE mast cell sensitization is not the primary mechanism. IL-17 excess may contribute but is secondary. AID excess is not related. NK cell cytotoxicity is not the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-10 = anti-inflammatory Treg cytokine = critical for gut immune homeostasis. IL-10 or IL-10R mutations cause very-early-onset IBD in infants. COMLEX: IL-10 also prevents macrophage-mediated tissue damage in RA and is being explored as a therapy for IBD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium cholangiopathy. Serum immunoglobulins show very elevated IgM (320 mg/dL) with absent IgG, IgA, and IgE. Flow cytometry shows normal T cell and B cell counts. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the CD40LG gene encoding CD40L. Which mechanism explains this boy's antibody profile?","choices":{"A":"CD40L deficiency prevents B cell receptor signaling, so B cells cannot be activated and cannot produce any antibody including IgM","B":"Absent CD40L impairs AID expression only partially, allowing IgM production but reducing the efficiency of IgG and IgA synthesis","C":"CD40L deficiency prevents IL-4 receptor expression on B cells, blocking cytokine-driven class switching while preserving baseline IgM secretion","D":"Without CD40L-CD40 interaction, B cells cannot receive the contact signal required for class switch recombination and remain stuck producing only IgM","E":"Without CD40L, T regulatory cells are overactivated and suppress IgG and IgA class switching by secreting excess TGF-beta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome (HIGM type 1) is caused by mutations in CD40LG encoding CD40L, which is expressed on activated CD4 T cells. Without CD40L, B cells cannot receive Signal 1 for class switch recombination (CD40-CD40L interaction). They continue to receive BCR-mediated activation signals and can produce T-independent IgM, but they cannot undergo class switching to IgG, IgA, or IgE. They also cannot form germinal centers or undergo somatic hypermutation. This explains the elevated IgM with absent other isotypes. These patients also suffer from opportunistic infections (PCP, Cryptosporidium) because CD40L-mediated macrophage activation is impaired. BCR signaling is intact — B cells can be activated; they just cannot class switch. AID expression may be reduced secondarily but the primary defect is CD40L-CD40 interaction. IL-4 receptor expression is normal. Treg overactivation is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyper-IgM syndrome = X-linked = CD40L deficiency = cannot class switch = high IgM, absent IgG/IgA/IgE. Also causes PCP and Cryptosporidium due to impaired macrophage activation. COMLEX: distinguish from AID deficiency (HIGM type 2) — both show Hyper-IgM but AID deficiency also causes enlarged lymph nodes from follicular hyperplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms of T cell and B cell activation"},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher is studying B cell responses in a tuberculosis patient. She finds that after 6 weeks of infection, the dominant serum antibody against mycobacterial antigens has shifted from IgM to IgG3, which is highly efficient at opsonizing bacteria. Which cytokine and T helper subset most directly drove this IgG3 class switch in the germinal center?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma produced by Th1 cells provides the cytokine signal that directs class switch recombination toward IgG3 and IgG1","B":"IL-5 produced by Th2 cells specifically drives the IgM to IgG3 switch during T-dependent responses against intracellular pathogens","C":"IL-4 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgG3, because IL-4 promotes all IgG subclass production nonselectively","D":"TGF-beta produced by regulatory T cells drives IgG3 class switching as a secondary effect of suppressing IgA production","E":"IL-10 produced by regulatory B cells acts on germinal center B cells to promote switching to IgG3 during chronic infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IFN-gamma, the signature Th1 cytokine, drives class switching to IgG1 and IgG3 in humans (and IgG2a/IgG3 in mice). These IgG subclasses are highly efficient at opsonization and complement activation, making them particularly effective against intracellular bacteria like mycobacteria. The Th1 response against TB generates IFN-gamma from both NK cells (early) and Th1 cells (sustained), which in germinal centers acts as the cytokine signal for IgG3/IgG1 switching alongside CD40L-CD40 interaction. IL-4 drives IgE and IgG4 switching, not IgG3. IL-5 primarily drives IgA switching, not IgG3. TGF-beta drives IgA, not IgG3. IL-10 promotes plasma cell differentiation generally but does not specifically drive IgG3 switching.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytokine to isotype map: IL-4 → IgE, IgG4; IFN-gamma → IgG1, IgG3 (opsonizing); TGF-beta → IgA; IL-5 → IgA (intestinal). COMLEX: knowing cytokine-to-isotype relationships helps predict which deficiencies produce which antibody patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms of T cell and B cell activation"},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A B-cell clone that makes IgM against a viral surface protein receives CD40L and cytokine signals in a germinal center and switches to making IgG. How does the antigen specificity of the new IgG compare with that of the original IgM?","choices":{"A":"Changed, because class switching joins a newly rearranged VDJ segment to the downstream constant region gene","B":"Unchanged, since the VDJ region and light chain are kept while the heavy-chain constant region changes","C":"Broadened, because the IgG constant region adds a second antigen-binding site to every heavy chain","D":"Changed, because switching replaces the light chain with a newly rearranged kappa or lambda chain","E":"Lost, because the switched antibody binds Fc receptors in place of the original viral antigen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a mature B cell switches class, the heavy-chain constant region is all that changes: the expressed V(D)J region and the light chain stay the same, so antigen specificity is retained. Switching is an intrachromosomal deletion between switch regions that loops out the intervening constant genes; it does not create a new VDJ rearrangement or a new light chain. The antigen-binding site is formed by the paired VH and VL regions, so the constant region does not add binding sites. Fc receptor binding is an effector function of the constant region and does not replace antigen binding. Sources: course SDL 28/35 reading (Immunology Ch 9, B-cell development and the antibody response, class switching, p40–43); course SDL 35 reading (Immunology Ch 10, Antibodies, variable and constant regions, p1, p5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: class switching changes the isotype and effector function but keeps antigen specificity; IgM and IgD are co-expressed by differential RNA splicing, not by recombination (Immunology Ch 9). COMLEX: AID deaminates cytosines in switch regions to start class-switch recombination (Immunology Ch 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — effector functions of the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman presents with recurrent sinusitis, otitis media, and two episodes of Giardia lamblia gastroenteritis in the past year. Serum IgG and IgM levels are normal. IgA is undetectable on repeat testing. She is otherwise healthy and has no autoimmune history. Which immunologic defect best explains her susceptibility to these specific infectious presentations?","choices":{"A":"Selective IgM deficiency prevents complement activation at mucosal surfaces, reducing bactericidal killing in the respiratory tract","B":"Selective IgG deficiency prevents opsonization of encapsulated bacteria in the sinuses, causing recurrent sinopulmonary infections","C":"Combined IgG and IgA deficiency prevents both opsonization and mucosal immunity, causing susceptibility to all extracellular pathogens","D":"Selective IgE deficiency prevents mast cell sensitization in the GI tract, reducing mucosal immunity against luminal parasites","E":"Selective IgA deficiency removes respiratory and gastrointestinal mucosal protection, causing sinopulmonary infections and giardiasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Selective IgA deficiency (markedly low serum IgA with normal IgG and IgM) is the most common primary immunodeficiency, occurring in approximately 1 in 600 individuals (Robbins Ch 14). IgA is the dominant antibody at mucosal surfaces, where it neutralizes pathogens in the lumen before tissue invasion. Without secretory IgA, the respiratory mucosa and GI mucosa lack first-line neutralizing protection, leading to recurrent sinopulmonary infections and susceptibility to Giardia (which colonizes the intestinal lumen). Normal IgG and IgM explain why systemic infections are not a major problem. Selective IgM deficiency is rare and would cause susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria. Selective IgG deficiency causes susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria but IgG is normal here. Combined IgG and IgA deficiency describes CVID. Selective IgE deficiency would not explain Giardia susceptibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective IgA deficiency = most common primary immunodeficiency = recurrent sinopulmonary infections + Giardia + normal IgG/IgM. Also associated with increased risk of autoimmune disease and anaphylaxis to IgA-containing blood products. COMLEX: always screen for selective IgA deficiency before giving IVIG — anti-IgA antibodies can cause anaphylaxis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-month-old boy presents with his third episode of Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia. His mother reports he was healthy at birth and had no infections until 4 months of age. Physical examination reveals no palpable lymph nodes and no tonsillar tissue. Serum immunoglobulins show absent IgG, IgA, IgM, and IgE. Which mechanism best explains why this infant was healthy for the first 4 months of life despite having agammaglobulinemia since birth?","choices":{"A":"Maternal IgA was present in breast milk and provided mucosal immunity until weaning, protecting the infant from systemic infection","B":"Innate immune cells including macrophages and NK cells provided sufficient defense against bacterial pathogens for the first 4 months","C":"Residual fetal B cell IgM production from primordial germinal centers provides partial protection during the first trimester after birth","D":"Maternal IgG was transported transplacentally via FcRn and provided passive humoral immunity for the first 4 to 6 months of life","E":"Maternal IgM was transported across the placenta via FcRn and provided complement-activating antibody coverage early in life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA, Bruton agammaglobulinemia) due to BTK deficiency. The key clinical teaching point is that affected boys are healthy for the first 4 to 6 months because maternal IgG is transported transplacentally via the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), which recognizes IgG at slightly acidic pH and releases it at physiologic pH on the fetal side of the placenta. After birth, maternal IgG is catabolized over 21-day half-life cycles and is largely gone by 6 months, exposing the underlying agammaglobulinemia. Maternal IgA in breast milk protects mucosal surfaces but does not prevent systemic bacterial infections — and most serious infections in XLA are systemic. Innate immunity alone is insufficient against encapsulated bacteria. Fetal B cells in XLA produce no antibody due to the BTK mutation. IgM is too large (pentamer) to cross the placenta via FcRn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maternal IgG via FcRn → protects neonate for 6 months → then physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia → reveals XLA. This timeline is the classic board vignette clue. COMLEX: FcRn also extends IgG serum half-life by rescuing internalized IgG from lysosomal degradation in adult tissues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with severe peanut allergy presents to the allergy clinic. Serum testing reveals markedly elevated peanut-specific IgE. She is started on omalizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting total IgE. Which mechanism explains why targeting IgE specifically can prevent anaphylaxis from allergen exposure?","choices":{"A":"IgE binds FcεRI on mast cells and basophils, where allergen cross-linking triggers degranulation; omalizumab neutralizes free IgE to prevent this","B":"IgE activates the complement classical pathway to generate C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins, and omalizumab prevents complement fixation at allergic sites","C":"IgE mediates opsonization of peanut proteins for phagocytosis by eosinophils, and omalizumab prevents allergen uptake by antigen-presenting cells","D":"IgE acts as an antigen receptor on Th2 cells to present allergen to CD4 T cells, and omalizumab blocks this antigen presentation step","E":"IgE activates NK cells via FcεRIII to kill peanut-sensitized cells, and omalizumab prevents IgE-dependent natural killer cell cytotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IgE-mediated (Type I) hypersensitivity operates in two phases. Sensitization phase: allergen-specific IgE, produced after Th2-driven class switching, binds with high affinity to FcεRI on mast cells and basophils via its Fc region, sensitizing these cells. Elicitation phase: re-exposure to allergen cross-links adjacent IgE molecules on the mast cell surface, triggering calcium influx and degranulation with release of histamine, tryptase, PGD2, and leukotrienes — causing anaphylaxis. Omalizumab is a humanized anti-IgE antibody that binds free IgE in serum, reducing the availability of IgE to bind FcεRI on mast cells. This prevents sensitization and reduces the mast cell IgE-receptor burden over months. IgE does not activate the classical complement pathway — that requires IgG and IgM. IgE does not mediate opsonization — that is IgG. IgE is not a Th2 antigen receptor. IgE acts via FcεRI on mast cells, not FcεRIII on NK cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgE + FcεRI on mast cells = Type I hypersensitivity. Omalizumab = anti-IgE = used for severe allergic asthma and chronic urticaria. COMLEX: the sensitization → elicitation two-step model is essential for understanding anaphylaxis, atopy, and food allergies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient develops migratory polyarthritis and carditis three weeks after an untreated streptococcal pharyngitis. ASO titers are markedly elevated. Cardiac biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltrates targeting host tissue despite bacterial clearance. Which mechanism best explains how the prior infection triggered autoimmune cardiac injury?","choices":{"A":"Bystander activation, in which inflammatory cytokines released during pharyngitis nonspecifically activate pre-existing self-reactive lymphocytes in cervical lymph nodes without requiring antigen-specific cross-reactivity","B":"AIRE gene deficiency, in which medullary thymic epithelial cells fail to express cardiac myosin during thymic development, allowing autoreactive T cell clones to escape negative selection and enter the periphery","C":"Molecular mimicry, in which Group A Streptococcus M-protein epitopes share structural homology with cardiac myosin, activating cross-reactive T and B cell clones that persist and attack host tissue after bacterial clearance","D":"Complement deficiency, in which impaired clearance of streptococcal antigen-antibody complexes allows persistent immune stimulation and polyclonal generation of anti-myosin IgG autoantibodies over time","E":"Epitope spreading, in which initial immune responses to streptococcal antigens sequentially expand to target additional cardiac self-epitopes released during early complement-mediated tissue injury at the valve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rheumatic fever is the canonical example of molecular mimicry: Group A Streptococcus M-protein epitopes structurally resemble cardiac myosin, valve glycoproteins, and other host proteins, activating cross-reactive T and B cells that persist after bacterial clearance. Bystander activation involves nonspecific inflammatory milieu rather than antigen-specific cross-reactivity and would not explain selective cardiac targeting. AIRE deficiency causes APS-1, not rheumatic fever. Complement deficiency predisposes to SLE-like disease. Epitope spreading is a secondary phenomenon that can amplify established autoimmunity, not the initiating mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rheumatic fever is the canonical molecular mimicry example — not rheumatoid arthritis. Jones criteria: carditis, polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Elevated ASO titer confirms prior GAS infection. COMLEX: thoracic somatic dysfunction T1-T5 may accompany carditis via viscerosomatic reflexes; OMT is supportive after acute phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Mutations in a gene encoding a transcriptional regulator expressed in medullary thymic epithelial cells cause autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1, characterized by chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and Addison disease. Deficiency in this gene prevents ectopic expression of organ-specific antigens such as 21-hydroxylase in the thymus. Which gene is mutated?","choices":{"A":"FOXP3 (forkhead box P3 transcription factor), which is required for development and maintenance of CD25+CD4+ regulatory T cells; mutations cause IPEX syndrome with polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and eczema","B":"AIRE (autoimmune regulator gene), which drives ectopic expression of peripheral tissue antigens in the thymic medulla, enabling clonal deletion of autoreactive T cells that would otherwise escape into the periphery","C":"PTPN22 (protein tyrosine phosphatase non-receptor type 22), which encodes a phosphatase that modulates T cell and B cell receptor signaling thresholds; variants associate with RA, T1D, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis","D":"CTLA-4 (cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4), which encodes a co-inhibitory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7 ligands on antigen-presenting cells and dampens effector T cell activation in peripheral tissues","E":"RAG1 (recombination activating gene 1), which mediates V(D)J recombination required to generate a diverse T cell receptor repertoire; deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency rather than organ-specific autoimmunity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AIRE drives ectopic expression of hundreds of peripheral tissue antigens in medullary thymic epithelial cells, enabling negative selection of autoreactive T cells. AIRE deficiency causes APS-1: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + Addison disease (autosomal recessive). FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX (X-linked): enteropathy + eczema + endocrinopathy. PTPN22 variants confer broad multi-disease susceptibility but do not cause APS-1. CTLA-4 mutations cause a separate autoimmune lymphoproliferative phenotype. RAG1 deficiency impairs V(D)J recombination causing SCID, not autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE = central tolerance enforcer in thymic medulla. APS-1 = CHAR: Candidiasis, Hypoparathyroidism, Adrenal insufficiency; autosomal recessive. IPEX (FOXP3) = X-linked. COMLEX: APS-1 patients may also develop alopecia, primary hypogonadism, and chronic active hepatitis; full screening is needed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A co-inhibitory receptor expressed on regulatory T cells binds B7 ligands on antigen-presenting cells with higher affinity than CD28, transmitting inhibitory signals that prevent unchecked T cell proliferation. A polymorphism linked to this gene is associated with rheumatoid arthritis, type I diabetes, and autoimmune thyroiditis. Which molecule is described?","choices":{"A":"PD-1, which binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 expressed on tumor cells and peripheral tissues to induce T cell exhaustion and suppress antitumor immunity during chronic antigen stimulation in malignancy","B":"LAG-3, which binds MHC class II molecules on dendritic cells with high affinity and suppresses CD4+ T cell activation by disrupting dendritic cell maturation signals in peripheral lymphoid organs","C":"TIM-3, which recognizes galectin-9 and phosphatidylserine on apoptotic cells and promotes exhaustion of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells during chronic viral infection and tumor microenvironments","D":"BTLA, which binds herpesvirus entry mediator on activated lymphocytes and delivers co-inhibitory signals that suppress effector T cell responses during acute viral infection and transplant rejection","E":"CTLA-4, which outcompetes CD28 for B7 ligands and so denies the T cell its costimulatory signal, and whose variants impair peripheral tolerance by weakening regulatory T cell suppressor function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CTLA-4 is expressed on activated T cells and Tregs and binds CD80/CD86 with higher affinity than CD28, competitively inhibiting costimulation. Polymorphisms reduce inhibitory signaling, associating with RA, T1DM, and thyroid autoimmunity. Abatacept exploits this pathway as a biologic DMARD for RA. PD-1 (targeted by nivolumab/pembrolizumab) mainly operates in tumors and peripheral tissues. LAG-3, TIM-3, and BTLA are other checkpoint molecules associated with T cell exhaustion in cancer/infection but not classically linked to the same autoimmune diseases as CTLA-4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTLA-4 = brake on T cells. Abatacept = CTLA-4-Ig fusion = blocks B7-CD28 = used in RA. Trap: CTLA-4 inhibitor ipilimumab used in cancer can cause autoimmune adverse effects including colitis, hepatitis, and endocrinopathy. COMLEX: patients on checkpoint inhibitors presenting with new inflammatory arthritis should be evaluated for immune-related adverse events mimicking RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Among all known HLA-disease associations in autoimmune disease, one specific allele confers a relative risk exceeding 150 — the highest reported for any single HLA allele-autoimmune disease pair. This allele is present in over 90% of patients with a seronegative spondyloarthropathy characterized by sacroiliitis, enthesitis, and ascending spinal ankylosis. Which allele is this?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4, which encodes the shared epitope in the DRB1 chain between positions 70 and 74 and confers a relative risk of 6-9 for seropositive rheumatoid arthritis in most studied populations","B":"HLA-DQ2/DQ8, which are class II alleles that present gluten-derived peptides in celiac disease and confer elevated risk for type I diabetes, with relative risks well below 50 in each disease","C":"HLA-B27, the MHC class I allele strongly associated with ankylosing spondylitis, conferring the highest single-allele relative risk in autoimmune disease and present in more than 90% of affected patients","D":"HLA-DR3, which confers susceptibility to SLE, Graves disease, Addison disease, and Hashimoto thyroiditis with relative risks typically in the range of 2-7, and is often in linkage disequilibrium with HLA-B8","E":"HLA-DQ6.02, which confers susceptibility to narcolepsy with a relative risk exceeding 40 and is associated with reduced rather than elevated risk for type I diabetes mellitus in most populations studied"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HLA-B27 has a relative risk exceeding 150 for ankylosing spondylitis — the strongest single HLA allele-disease association known. Mechanisms proposed include aberrant peptide presentation, HLA-B27 heavy chain misfolding triggering UPR, and arthritogenic peptide hypothesis. HLA-DR4 (RR 6-9) is the RA shared epitope allele. DQ2/DQ8 are celiac/T1D alleles with much lower RR. HLA-DR3 predisposes to several autoimmune conditions but with RR of 2-7. DQ6.02 has RR >40 for narcolepsy but is protective for T1DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-B27 mnemonic PAIR — Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis. RR >150 for AS is a high-yield board fact. COMLEX: AS causes rib cage rigidity and thoracolumbar facet joint ankylosis; OMT is generally contraindicated in advanced ankylosis due to fracture risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid arthritis shows no HLA-A or HLA-B locus associations but is associated with a specific amino acid sequence in the DRbeta chain shared by DR1 and major DR4 subtypes. The MHC association with RA is strongest in patients who are serologically positive for a specific antibody. Which serologic finding is most strongly associated with this HLA risk factor?","choices":{"A":"Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) at a titer exceeding 1:160, which are present in over 95% of SLE patients and are associated with HLA-DR2 and HLA-DR3 haplotypes rather than the shared epitope in DR4","B":"Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibodies, which are found almost exclusively in SLE despite low sensitivity and are associated with HLA-DR2/DR3 haplotypes rather than the shared epitope found in DR1/DR4","C":"Rheumatoid factor (RF) IgM against Fc portions of IgG, which is found in 70-80% of RA patients but is also seen in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, and infection and is not uniquely linked to the shared epitope","D":"Anti-citrullinated protein antibodies (ACPA), the serologic marker that specifically co-associates with the HLA shared epitope in DR1/DR4-positive patients and predicts more erosive, aggressive RA","E":"Markedly elevated serum ferritin, in the range seen with macrophage activation syndrome or adult-onset Still disease, rather than a seronegative or seropositive shared-epitope RA phenotype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The HLA shared epitope encodes amino acids 70-74 of the DRbeta chain found in DR1 and DR4 subtypes, directly shaping the peptide-binding groove to present citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells. The SE association with RA is restricted to ACPA-positive patients, suggesting citrullinated antigens drive HLA-mediated pathology. The SE sequence is also found in EBV gp110 and bacterial dnaJ proteins, supporting molecular mimicry as a trigger. RF is present in 60-80% of RA patients (Robbins Table 6.10: 60-70%; Robbins Ch 26: 80%) but lacks SE-specific association. Anti-Sm and high-titer ANA are SLE markers. Very high ferritin suggests macrophage activation syndrome or Still disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA antibody pairs: RF (60-70% of RA in Robbins Table 6.10, 80% in Robbins Ch 26; not specific) + ACPA/anti-CCP (60-80% in Table 6.10, up to 70% in Ch 26; more specific, FA p113). ACPA = most specific RA antibody. ACPA+ RA = more erosive, higher risk of extra-articular manifestations. Shared epitope = HLA-DRB1 alleles encoding positions 70-74. COMLEX: citrullination is catalyzed by PAD enzymes (PAD4); enhanced by smoking, which is also an environmental RA risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A protein tyrosine phosphatase expressed in lymphocytes has a minor allele that causes gain-of-function inhibition of B cell and T cell receptor signaling. This variant is associated with type I diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis, but inversely associated with Crohn disease. Which gene encodes this phosphatase?","choices":{"A":"AIRE, located at chromosome 21q22, which encodes the autoimmune regulator transcription factor required for ectopic self-antigen expression in medullary thymic epithelial cells to enforce central tolerance","B":"PTPN22, located at chromosome 1p13, the most common non-HLA gene associated with multiple autoimmune diseases; its Trp620 allele alters lymphocyte receptor signaling and likely weakens thymic deletion","C":"CTLA-4, located at chromosome 2q33, which encodes the inhibitory T cell costimulatory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7 ligands and whose polymorphisms are linked to RA, T1DM, and thyroid autoimmunity","D":"IL-23R, located at chromosome 1p31, which encodes the IL-23 receptor subunit that drives Th17 differentiation and is associated with IBD, psoriasis, and ankylosing spondylitis rather than RA or SLE","E":"FCGR3B, located at chromosome 1q23, which encodes an Fc gamma receptor on neutrophils mediating immune complex clearance; null alleles predispose to SLE and ANCA-associated vasculitis rather than RA or T1DM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PTPN22 encodes lymphoid tyrosine phosphatase, which negatively regulates TCR and BCR signaling. The Trp620 minor allele gains function to further inhibit receptor signaling, paradoxically predisposing to autoimmunity — likely by impairing thymic deletion and Treg function. The common Arg620 allele associates with Crohn disease, illustrating how opposite effects on receptor signaling associate with distinct immunopathology. PTPN22 is the single most replicated non-HLA autoimmune susceptibility locus. It is on chromosome 1p13, distinct from CTLA-4 (2q33), AIRE (21q22), IL-23R (1p31), and FCGR3B (1q23).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-HLA autoimmune genes: PTPN22 (multiple autoimmune), CTLA-4 (RA/T1D/thyroid), IL-23R (IBD/spondylitis/psoriasis), AIRE (APS-1), FOXP3 (IPEX), FCGR genes (SLE). COMLEX: PTPN22 Trp620 often appears in vignettes with positive family history of multiple unrelated autoimmune conditions across generations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman presents with pallor, jaundice, and exertional dyspnea. Laboratory results show hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, elevated indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, and reticulocytosis. A direct Coombs test is positive for IgG coating of erythrocytes. Which hypersensitivity type and mechanism is responsible for the anemia?","choices":{"A":"Type I hypersensitivity, in which IgE anti-erythrocyte antibodies bound to FcεRI receptors on mast cells degranulate upon RBC antigen cross-linking, releasing histamine that causes hemolytic jaundice and pruritus","B":"Type III hypersensitivity, in which circulating IgG-antigen immune complexes form soluble aggregates that coincidentally deposit onto circulating erythrocytes and activate complement through the classical pathway","C":"Type IV hypersensitivity, in which sensitized CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes recognize altered peptide-MHC I complexes on RBC precursors in the bone marrow and directly destroy erythroid progenitor cells","D":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which IgG autoantibodies bind erythrocyte surface antigens and promote opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis by splenic macrophages, causing extravascular hemolysis","E":"Type II hypersensitivity mediated by IgM cold agglutinins that fix complement on erythrocytes at cold temperatures and cause agglutination in acral vessels, producing a positive direct Coombs test for complement rather than IgG"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is the prototype Type II hypersensitivity disorder. IgG anti-RBC antibodies detected by direct Coombs test opsonize erythrocytes, which are then phagocytosed by splenic macrophages via Fc receptors, producing extravascular hemolysis with elevated indirect bilirubin and low haptoglobin. The direct Coombs positive for IgG distinguishes warm AIHA (Type II, IgG, extravascular) from cold agglutinin disease (also Type II, but IgM with complement-positive Coombs; predominantly extravascular, complement-mediated hemolysis with acral agglutination). Type I requires IgE and mast cells. Type III involves soluble complexes. Type IV has no antibody component.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type II = antibody against cell surface or fixed tissue antigen. Direct Coombs = IgG on RBCs (warm AIHA) or complement on RBCs (cold agglutinin). Warm AIHA: IgG, 37 degrees C, extravascular. Cold agglutinin: IgM, cold temps, complement-mediated, mostly extravascular. COMLEX: splenomegaly is expected; splenectomy treats refractory warm AIHA. Associated with CLL, lymphoma, infections (Mycoplasma, EBV).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE presents with new hematuria and proteinuria. Kidney biopsy shows granular immune deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with C3 and IgG staining. Serum complement levels C3 and C4 are both low. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is the primary driver of renal injury in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Type III hypersensitivity, in which circulating IgG-anti-dsDNA immune complexes deposit in glomerular capillaries, activate complement via the classical pathway, and recruit neutrophils that injure the glomerular wall","B":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which IgG anti-GBM antibodies bind linearly along the glomerular basement membrane and activate complement, producing the same granular immunofluorescence pattern found in Goodpasture syndrome","C":"Type I hypersensitivity, in which renal mast cells sensitized by IgE anti-dsDNA antibodies degranulate upon antigen re-exposure, releasing histamine and prostaglandins that increase glomerular vascular permeability","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity, in which Th17 cells infiltrating the glomerulus produce IL-17A and recruit neutrophils to produce fibrinoid necrosis without immunoglobulin or complement deposition in glomerular capillaries","E":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which anti-C1q autoantibodies bind complement fixed along the GBM and produce a granular pattern on immunofluorescence indistinguishable from immune complex nephritis by light microscopy alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis is the prototypical Type III immune complex disease. Circulating anti-dsDNA IgG forms complexes with nucleosomal antigens that deposit in glomeruli, activating complement classically (explaining low C3 and C4). The granular IF pattern distinguishes it from Goodpasture syndrome (Type II = linear IgG). Low complement is the key clinical clue for active Type III. Type I requires IgE. Type IV involves T cells without immunoglobulin deposition and would not produce low serum complement or granular IF. Anti-C1q antibodies are seen in SLE but cause Type III pathology through immune complex formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Granular IF + low complement = Type III immune complex. Linear IF = Type II anti-GBM. Pauci-immune (no Ig) = ANCA vasculitis. Low C3 + low C4 = classical pathway activation = SLE/MPGN type I/post-infectious. Low C3 only = alternative pathway = MPGN type II/dense deposit disease. COMLEX: SLE patients should be monitored for nephritis with serial C3/C4 and urinalysis; drop in complement + proteinuria = flare.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old healthcare worker develops pruritus, erythema, and vesicles on her hands 48 to 72 hours after wearing latex gloves. A patch test applied for 48 hours reproduces the reaction with maximal induration at 72 hours. Skin biopsy shows perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates with few eosinophils. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity, in which IgE anti-latex antibodies bound to dermal mast cells cross-link upon re-exposure and release preformed histamine within 15-30 minutes, producing immediate urticaria and potential anaphylaxis","B":"Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity, in which sensitized CD4+ Th1 cells recognize latex hapten-protein complexes on MHC II and produce IFN-gamma, activating macrophages and driving lymphocytic dermal inflammation with 48-72 hour kinetics","C":"Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity, in which IgG anti-latex antibodies bind to latex-modified keratinocyte surface proteins and activate complement, generating C5a that recruits neutrophils into the dermis within hours","D":"Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity, in which soluble latex-IgG antibody complexes deposit in dermal capillaries, activate complement via the classical pathway, and trigger fibrinoid vasculitis and neutrophilic infiltrates","E":"Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity, in which IgM anti-latex antibodies activate the classical complement pathway on epidermal cells, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that recruit eosinophils to the dermis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allergic contact dermatitis is the prototype Type IV reaction. Sensitization: latex haptens bind host proteins, are processed by Langerhans cells, and presented to CD4+ T cells that differentiate into Th1 memory cells. Elicitation: re-exposure triggers Th1 IFN-gamma production, macrophage activation, and perivascular lymphocytic inflammation with 48-72 hour peak. Type I latex allergy (IgE-mediated) causes immediate urticaria/anaphylaxis within minutes — a distinct presentation. Patch testing positive at 48-72 h is the diagnostic test for Type IV. All three wrong choices misidentify the mechanism as antibody-mediated rather than T cell-mediated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type IV = DTH = Delayed (48-72 h) + T cells + no antibody. Classic examples: TB skin test, poison ivy/oak, nickel, contact dermatitis. Type I latex = immediate urticaria (minutes); Type IV latex = delayed vesicular dermatitis (48-72 h). Both can coexist. COMLEX: patch testing is performed at 48-72 hours for Type IV; intradermal skin testing is used for Type I evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-month-old male presents with severe watery diarrhea, failure to thrive, eczematous rash, and type I diabetes mellitus diagnosed at 8 months of age. Immunologic workup reveals absent CD4+CD25+ regulatory T cells in peripheral blood. Gene sequencing confirms a frameshift mutation in a transcription factor located on the X chromosome. Which gene is mutated, and what is the best mechanistic explanation for the multi-organ autoimmunity?","choices":{"A":"AIRE; loss of ectopic self-antigen expression in medullary thymic epithelial cells permits autoreactive T cells targeting gut and skin antigens to escape negative selection and enter the periphery unchecked","B":"PTPN22; a gain-of-function variant in this non-HLA phosphatase gene impairs thymic deletion of autoreactive T cells and reduces regulatory T cell function across the gut, skin, and endocrine pancreas","C":"FOXP3; loss of functional regulatory T cells eliminates peripheral suppression of autoreactive effector T cells in the gut, skin, and pancreatic islets, allowing unchecked tissue destruction across multiple organ systems","D":"IL-2RA; a null mutation in the high-affinity IL-2 receptor alpha chain abolishes IL-2-driven expansion and survival of FOXP3+ regulatory T cells, mimicking FOXP3 deficiency through a distinct signaling mechanism","E":"RAG2; biallelic loss of function prevents V(D)J recombination entirely, producing a SCID phenotype with absent T and B cells rather than autoimmune polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and eczema in a male infant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is IPEX syndrome caused by FOXP3 loss-of-function mutations. FOXP3 is the master transcription factor for CD4+CD25+ Tregs, which suppress autoreactive effector T cells via IL-10, TGF-beta, and contact-dependent mechanisms. Without Tregs, effector T cells attack gut (diarrhea), skin (eczema), and pancreatic islets (T1DM). It is X-linked and presents in infancy. AIRE mutation causes APS-1 (candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + Addison disease, autosomal recessive, onset in childhood). IL-2RA deficiency phenotypically resembles IPEX but is caused by a distinct mechanism. RAG2 deficiency causes SCID with absent lymphocytes rather than autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IPEX = FOXP3 + X-linked + triad of enteropathy + eczema + endocrinopathy in infancy. Fatal without HSCT. Distinguishing from APS-1: IPEX has enteropathy and eczema; APS-1 has Addison and candidiasis. COMLEX: male infants with chronic diarrhea + eczema + elevated glucose warrant genetic workup; IPEX is rapidly progressive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with a 6-year history of SLE is found to have monozygotic twin sister who has remained unaffected. Their physician notes that concordance for SLE in identical twins is approximately 25%. A systemic viral infection two months ago preceded worsening anti-dsDNA titers and declining complement levels. Which statement best integrates the genetic and environmental factors in SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"The 25% monozygotic twin concordance indicates SLE is primarily a single-gene disorder caused by a complement C4 null allele, with environmental triggers playing only a minor role in disease initiation","B":"SLE has no significant genetic component because concordance in identical twins below 50% indicates predominantly environmental causation with genetic factors providing minimal predisposition to disease","C":"SLE is caused exclusively by type I interferon overproduction triggered by viral infection; the 25% concordance reflects variation in antiviral immunity rather than shared genetic autoimmune susceptibility","D":"Identical twins with SLE always develop the same clinical phenotype since they share identical HLA haplotypes, and phenotypic variability within twin pairs would disprove a genetic contribution to SLE","E":"SLE requires both polygenic susceptibility (HLA-DR2/DR3, complement null alleles, type I IFN pathway variants) and environmental triggers such as viral infection and UV light, explaining twin discordance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLE is a complex polygenic disorder. Monozygotic twin concordance of 25% proves strong genetic predisposition compared with DZ concordance of 2-3%, while the 75% non-concordance demonstrates that genetic susceptibility alone is insufficient. SLE-predisposing genes include HLA-DR2/DR3, C4 null alleles (impaired immune complex clearance), type I IFN pathway genes (IRF5, IRF7, TYK2), TLR7, and FCGR genes. Environmental triggers include UV radiation, certain infections, estrogen, and drugs. The distinction between 25% concordance (strong genetic component, incomplete penetrance) and 100% concordance (monogenic) is a key board concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MZ concordance for SLE 25% vs DZ 2-3% = polygenic with strong HLA component. C4 null alleles (most common in SLE) impair apoptotic debris clearance. Type I IFN signature = hallmark of active SLE, activated by TLR7/9 sensing of nucleic acids. Environmental triggers: UV light, EBV, estrogen, drugs (procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid). COMLEX: drug-induced lupus = positive anti-histone antibodies; resolves with drug discontinuation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis asks her neurologist why her immune cells attack myelin basic protein when AIRE should have deleted such clones in the thymus. The physician explains that AIRE expression does not cover all self-antigens, and that escapee self-reactive T cells are normally suppressed by additional mechanisms. Which combination best describes the peripheral tolerance mechanisms that suppress these escaped autoreactive T cells?","choices":{"A":"Clonal anergy from absent costimulation during self-antigen presentation, Treg suppression via IL-10 and TGF-beta, and Fas-FasL activation-induced cell death together maintain tolerance to self-antigens not covered by AIRE","B":"Somatic hypermutation in peripheral B cells generates new BCR specificities that cross-react with self-antigens, but affinity maturation in germinal centers eliminates all self-reactive B cell clones before autoantibody production occurs","C":"Negative selection in the thymic cortex under supervision of cortical thymic epithelial cells provides complete elimination of all T cell clones reactive to tissue-specific antigens such as myelin basic protein","D":"Class switch recombination from IgM to IgG requires CD40-CD40L interaction, which is blocked in peripheral lymph nodes when self-reactive B cells encounter self-antigen without adequate T helper cell activation","E":"Receptor editing in peripheral B cells replaces autoreactive BCR light chains with non-self-reactive sequences during secondary V(D)J recombination, rescuing self-reactive clones from activation-induced apoptosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central tolerance (AIRE-driven negative selection) is incomplete — AIRE covers many but not all peripheral tissue antigens. Peripheral tolerance provides the second line of defense via three main mechanisms: (1) Clonal anergy: T cells recognizing self-antigen without CD28 costimulation become functionally unresponsive. (2) Treg suppression: FOXP3+ Tregs secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta and provide contact-dependent suppression. (3) AICD: Fas-FasL interaction triggers apoptosis of chronically activated T cells. In MS, peripheral tolerance breakdown to myelin antigens (MBP, PLP, MOG) leads to demyelination. The wrong choices describe B cell biology rather than T cell peripheral tolerance mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central tolerance = thymus (clonal deletion via AIRE). Peripheral tolerance = anergy + Treg suppression + AICD. Failure = autoimmune disease. MS = Type IV hypersensitivity, CD4+ Th1/Th17-mediated demyelination. Oligoclonal bands on CSF = intrathecal IgG from B cells. COMLEX: MS patients may have concurrent other autoimmune diseases; MS is more common in women and in people who grew up farther from the equator with less UV light exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with ACPA-positive rheumatoid arthritis and HLA-DRB1*04:01 reports that her sister has autoimmune thyroiditis and her mother has SLE. Genetic counseling reveals the family likely carries PTPN22 Trp620, HLA-DR3, and HLA-DR4 variants distributed across members. Which statement about the familial autoimmune clustering is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4 is co-inherited exclusively with PTPN22 Trp620 in a conserved haplotype on chromosome 1, so family members carrying DR4 automatically also carry the Trp620 variant and share identical overall disease risk","B":"The presence of multiple autoimmune conditions within the family indicates a shared C4 null allele that impairs immune complex clearance and predisposes all members equally to SLE, RA, and thyroid autoimmunity","C":"HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 within the same family indicate that only HLA genes contribute to autoimmune susceptibility, since non-HLA genes like PTPN22 have been shown to be statistically insignificant in GWAS studies","D":"Familial clustering of autoimmune diseases reflects exclusively environmental exposures shared by family members rather than genetic factors, since no autoimmune disease exceeds 50% concordance in identical twins","E":"PTPN22 Trp620 broadly alters lymphocyte receptor signaling and confers risk for RA, T1DM, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis across the family, while specific HLA alleles add organ-specific disease susceptibility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Non-HLA genes, especially PTPN22, confer risk across multiple autoimmune diseases simultaneously, explaining why different autoimmune conditions cluster in the same family. PTPN22 is on chromosome 1p13 and HLA is on chromosome 6p21 — they are on different chromosomes and segregate independently. HLA alleles provide organ-specific disease targeting (DR4 for RA, DR3 for SLE/thyroid), while PTPN22 provides a shared background of lymphocyte dysregulation. GWAS studies show that many risk loci are shared across autoimmune diseases (course Immuno Ch 20: over 100 SLE loci, \"many of which are shared\"). The 25% MZ concordance for SLE and similar values for other diseases prove genetic, not purely environmental, causation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTPN22 and HLA are on different chromosomes and not co-inherited. Familial autoimmune clustering = PTPN22 + HLA diversity. One patient with one autoimmune disease should be screened for others. COMLEX: RA patients have elevated rates of Sjogren overlap, thyroid disease, and interstitial lung disease; these co-occurrences are explained by shared genetic susceptibility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with progressive ankylosing spondylitis carries HLA-B27 and reports his father and brother are similarly affected. His 20-year-old son carries HLA-B27 but has no symptoms. Genetic counseling reveals that some HLA alleles are protective. Which HLA allele confers a dramatically reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus, with a relative risk far below 1.0, making it one of the best-characterized protective alleles in autoimmune disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27, which confers a relative risk exceeding 150 for ankylosing spondylitis and is not considered protective against any major autoimmune condition studied in large population cohorts","B":"HLA-DR4, which confers a relative risk of 6-9 for rheumatoid arthritis and is associated with more aggressive, erosive, seropositive disease rather than any protective effect against autoimmunity","C":"HLA-DQ8, which confers a relative risk of approximately 14 for type I diabetes and is further amplified when DQ2/DQ8 compound heterozygosity is present, conferring the highest known risk for T1DM","D":"HLA-DQ6.02, which is associated with a markedly reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus (relative risk about 0.02), making it a well-characterized protective HLA allele in autoimmune disease","E":"HLA-DR3, which confers susceptibility to SLE, Graves disease, and Addison disease with relative risks of 2-7, and is associated with elevated rather than reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HLA-DQ6.02 (also DQ0602) has a relative risk of approximately 0.02 for T1DM — a dramatic protective effect. The mechanism likely involves DQ6.02 binding insulin-derived peptides in a configuration that promotes tolerance rather than autoreactivity. This illustrates the key concept that HLA alleles can be protective (RR less than 1) as well as predisposing (RR greater than 1). Notably, the same DQ6.02 allele confers susceptibility to narcolepsy with RR exceeding 40, illustrating allele-specific bidirectional effects across diseases. All four wrong choices have RR greater than 1 for their associated diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Protective HLA = DQ6.02 for T1DM (RR approximately 0.02). Board trap: same DQ6.02 is susceptibility allele for narcolepsy (RR >40). Highest T1DM risk: DQ2/DQ8 compound heterozygosity. DQ8 alone: RR approximately 14. Board tip: protective alleles appear on USMLE to test whether students understand relative risk can be below 1. COMLEX: T1DM patients should receive annual thyroid screening given shared genetic risk with Hashimoto thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman newly diagnosed with SLE is found to have a null allele for complement component C4A, reducing C4A expression by 50%. Her rheumatologist explains that early complement deficiencies strongly predispose to lupus-like disease. What is the mechanism by which early complement component deficiency contributes to SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of early complement components impairs clearance of apoptotic cellular debris and circulating immune complexes, so persistent nuclear antigen stimulation drives anti-dsDNA and antinuclear antibody production","B":"Complement deficiency reduces terminal membrane attack complex formation, which directly increases susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria and prevents the normal lysis of autoreactive B cells in secondary lymphoid organs","C":"C4A null alleles reduce complement-dependent opsonization of bacteria and viruses, leading to recurrent infections that trigger molecular mimicry and generation of cross-reactive antibodies against nuclear antigens","D":"Loss of C4A reduces total serum complement levels, activating a compensatory feedback loop in which B cells upregulate anti-dsDNA IgG production that further depletes residual C3 and C4 via ongoing classical pathway activation","E":"C4A null alleles impair complement-mediated B cell tolerance checkpoints in the bone marrow, preventing the elimination of newly formed autoreactive B cell clones before they reach peripheral lymphoid organs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Early complement components C1q, C2, and C4 play a critical role in opsonization and clearance of apoptotic cells and immune complexes via complement receptor-mediated phagocytosis. When early complement is deficient, apoptotic cells and immune complexes accumulate. Nucleosomal antigens released from poorly cleared apoptotic cells persistently stimulate autoreactive B cells, driving anti-dsDNA and ANA production. Null alleles of C1q, C2 or C4 strongly predispose to a lupus-like disease (course Immuno Ch 20). This is distinct from terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), which increases susceptibility to Neisseria rather than causing autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C1q, C2 or C4 null alleles = strong single-gene lupus risk (course Immuno Ch 20). C2 deficiency = most common complement deficiency overall; also predisposes to SLE + encapsulated organisms. Mechanism: impaired apoptotic debris clearance = persistent nuclear antigen = anti-dsDNA. Low C3/C4 during SLE flare = complement consumption by active immune complexes. COMLEX: patients with recurrent Neisseria infections should be screened for terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), while those with lupus phenotype and recurrent infections may have early complement deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male with known peanut allergy develops generalized urticaria, laryngeal edema, bronchospasm, and hypotension within 10 minutes of eating a cookie. His roommate, who has never been exposed to peanuts, eats the same cookie with no adverse reaction. Intramuscular epinephrine is administered with rapid resolution. Which sequence of events best explains the anaphylaxis in this patient but not his roommate?","choices":{"A":"Prior sensitization activated peanut-specific CD4+ Th1 memory cells; re-exposure triggers rapid IFN-gamma production and macrophage activation causing systemic inflammation with onset within 10 minutes of antigen contact","B":"Prior sensitization generated peanut-specific IgE now bound to FcεRI on mast cells; re-exposure cross-links adjacent IgE-FcεRI complexes, triggering degranulation with release of histamine, tryptase, and leukotrienes","C":"Peanut lectins directly activate the mannose-binding lectin complement pathway, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that stimulate systemic basophil and mast cell degranulation without requiring prior IgE antibody formation","D":"Peanut-specific IgG antibodies formed during prior sensitization bind peanut antigens and form immune complexes that deposit in dermal vessels and activate complement, causing systemic urticaria and hypotension","E":"Peanut glycoproteins activate plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type I interferons that trigger systemic NK cell activation and vascular permeability, causing urticaria and bronchospasm within minutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type I hypersensitivity requires two exposures: (1) Sensitization: first exposure drives Th2 differentiation and IL-4 signaling, inducing IgE class switching by B cells; peanut-specific IgE binds FcεRI on mast cells and basophils. (2) Elicitation: re-exposure cross-links adjacent IgE-FcεRI complexes, triggering immediate degranulation. Preformed mediators (histamine, tryptase) and newly synthesized lipid mediators (LTC4/D4, PGD2, PAF) produce the immediate reaction within minutes; cytokines then recruit eosinophils, neutrophils, and T cells for the late-phase reaction 2–24 hours later. No prior exposure = no sensitization = no anaphylaxis in the roommate. Epinephrine acts via alpha-1 (vasoconstriction) and beta-2 (bronchodilation) to reverse shock.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anaphylaxis = Type I = IgE-FcεRI-mast cell. First-line = IM epinephrine. Preformed: histamine, tryptase, heparin. Newly synthesized: LTC4, LTD4, PGD2, PAF. Sensitization required — first exposure is silent. COMLEX: omalizumab (anti-IgE) prevents IgE from binding FcεRI and reduces anaphylaxis risk and asthma exacerbations; used in chronic refractory asthma and food allergy prevention protocols.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male smoker presents with hemoptysis, hematuria, and rapidly progressive renal failure. Kidney biopsy shows linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with C3 and crescentic glomerulonephritis. Serum anti-GBM antibodies targeting the NC1 domain of type IV collagen are positive. Which statement best distinguishes this condition from SLE nephritis by immunofluorescence pattern and hypersensitivity mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type II hypersensitivity with linear IgG against a fixed GBM antigen; SLE nephritis is Type III with granular deposits of circulating immune complexes; both activate complement by distinct mechanisms","B":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type III hypersensitivity with granular immune complex deposits because anti-GBM antibodies form circulating complexes first before depositing in the glomerulus in a subendothelial distribution","C":"SLE nephritis is Type II hypersensitivity with linear IgG deposition because anti-dsDNA antibodies directly bind fixed DNA antigens on glomerular epithelial cell surfaces in a continuous linear pattern","D":"Both Goodpasture syndrome and SLE nephritis are Type IV hypersensitivity with predominant CD8+ cytotoxic T cell infiltrates; the immunofluorescence pattern difference reflects only the degree of non-specific IgG accumulation","E":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type IV hypersensitivity; the linear IgG along the GBM represents non-specific immunoglobulin deposition, while the actual injury is mediated by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells destroying type IV collagen directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Key IF distinction: Goodpasture disease = LINEAR IgG = Type II (antibody against fixed tissue antigen — NC1 domain of type IV collagen alpha-3 chain). SLE nephritis = GRANULAR (\"lumpy-bumpy\") IF = Type III (immune complex deposition). Both activate classical complement pathway. Anti-GBM targets both kidney GBM and lung alveolar basement membrane, producing the pulmonary-renal syndrome. Smoking increases risk by exposing alveolar antigens to the immune system. Pauci-immune glomerulonephritis (no IF deposits) with ANCA is the third major pattern for rapidly progressive GN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linear IF = anti-GBM (Goodpasture), Type II. Granular IF = SLE/MPGN/post-strep, Type III. Pauci-immune = ANCA vasculitis (GPA, MPA). Pulmonary-renal syndrome: Goodpasture (anti-GBM) vs ANCA vasculitis (cANCA/pANCA) vs SLE (anti-dsDNA). Treatment: plasmapheresis + cyclophosphamide + steroids. COMLEX: young male smoker + hemoptysis + hematuria = Goodpasture until proven otherwise; smoking is thought to expose alveolar antigens to circulating anti-GBM antibodies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old nursing student has a tuberculin skin test placed for employment. At 48 hours, a 17 mm area of induration is present at the injection site. The same student recently received a second subcutaneous injection of rabbit serum protein for research; 4-6 hours later she developed erythema and palpable swelling at the injection site. Which statement correctly identifies the hypersensitivity type for each reaction and distinguishes their effector mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"The TST is Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity because it requires prior sensitization and involves IgG anti-tuberculin antibodies depositing in dermal vessels; the rabbit serum reaction is Type IV (delayed-type) because it recruits Th17 cells and neutrophils at 4-10 hours","B":"Both reactions are Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity because they both require prior sensitization, both peak within hours to days, and both involve T lymphocyte-mediated inflammation without requiring pre-formed antibody","C":"The TST is Type IV hypersensitivity mediated by sensitized CD4+ Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma at 48-72 hours; the rabbit serum reaction is a Type III Arthus reaction from local IgG-antigen complexes fixing complement at 4-10 hours","D":"The TST is Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity mediated by IgE anti-tuberculin antibodies on dermal mast cells; the rabbit serum reaction is Type II (antibody-mediated) mediated by IgG opsonizing rabbit protein-coated host cells for phagocytic destruction","E":"Both reactions are Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity; the TST involves IgG anti-mycobacterial antibodies opsonizing macrophages, while the Arthus reaction involves IgG opsonizing injected antigen-protein conjugates at the injection site"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The TST is the classic Type IV DTH reaction: tuberculin-sensitized CD4+ Th1 memory cells recognize tuberculin-MHC II complexes on APCs, produce IFN-gamma, and activate macrophages. Induration (not just redness) from mononuclear infiltrate peaks at 48-72 hours. The Arthus reaction is a local Type III reaction: preformed IgG antibodies in tissue react with injected antigen to form in-situ immune complexes, activate complement (C3a/C5a), and recruit neutrophils. Peaks at 4 to 10 hours (course immunology text, Ch 25). Fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls is characteristic. Neutrophils = key effectors in Type III; T cells = key effectors in Type IV. Both require prior sensitization but differ in effector mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthus reaction = local Type III; serum sickness = systemic Type III. DTH = local Type IV; systemic = transplant rejection/MS/T1DM. Timing: Type I = minutes; Arthus (Type III) = hours (peak 4-10 h); Type IV = days (48-72 h). PPD induration thresholds: >5 mm in immunocompromised, >10 mm in healthcare workers, >15 mm in low-risk individuals. COMLEX: induration = key physical finding; erythema alone does not count as positive TST.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Among environmental exposures associated with rheumatoid arthritis, one factor is thought to promote disease initiation by activating peptidyl-arginine deiminase enzymes in lung tissue, driving citrullination of self-proteins and generating neoantigens that trigger ACPA formation years before joint symptoms appear. Which exposure best fits this description?","choices":{"A":"UV radiation exposure, which activates PAD4 in keratinocytes to citrullinate histones released during apoptosis, creating circulating citrullinated nucleosomal antigens that trigger ACPA before joint symptoms develop","B":"Gluten ingestion, which activates tissue transglutaminase in the synovial lining to convert arginine to citrulline on joint fibronectin and type II collagen, initiating ACPA formation in HLA-DR4 positive individuals","C":"Oral contraceptive use, which drives PAD2 expression in endometrial stromal cells and promotes systemic citrullination of fibrinogen, allowing ACPA to form through a hormonal mechanism distinct from smoking","D":"Low-dose aspirin therapy, which inhibits COX-2 in neutrophils and promotes extracellular PAD enzyme release into synovial fluid, initiating citrullination of joint proteins before clinical arthritis is detectable","E":"Cigarette smoking, which activates PAD enzymes in the lungs and promotes citrullination of proteins such as fibrinogen and type II collagen, generating neoantigens that drive ACPA production in genetically susceptible individuals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is the best-established environmental risk factor for RA. Smoking promotes PAD enzyme activation in the lungs, leading to citrullination of proteins including fibrinogen, vimentin, and type II collagen. These citrullinated neoantigens can trigger ACPA formation years before joint symptoms appear — a concept called the pre-clinical window of RA. Periodontitis via Porphyromonas gingivalis (which expresses its own PAD enzyme) is the other well-established environmental trigger. UV radiation predisposes to SLE rather than RA. Gluten activates tissue transglutaminase (relevant in celiac disease) which converts glutamine to glutamate, not arginine to citrulline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Smoking + HLA-DR4 + PTPN22 = highest RA risk triad. Smoking drives citrullination via PAD in lungs. ACPA precedes joint symptoms by years. Periodontitis (P. gingivalis) is second major environmental trigger. COMLEX: smoking cessation is a therapeutic priority in RA; continued smoking worsens disease activity, reduces biologic efficacy, and increases cardiovascular risk already elevated in RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The HLA-DR4 allele is associated with ACPA-positive RA. Evidence suggests that an epitope on citrullinated proteins can mimic epitopes on certain microbes and be presented by HLA-DR4 to CD4+ T cells. Which post-translational modification creates the citrullinated neoantigens recognized by ACPA?","choices":{"A":"Post-translational phosphorylation of serine residues on synovial fibronectin by protein kinases activated during macrophage NF-kB signaling, creating phosphopeptide neoantigens presented by HLA-DR4","B":"Post-translational conversion of arginine residues to citrulline by peptidyl-arginine deiminase enzymes on joint proteins including fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin","C":"Post-translational deamidation of glutamine to glutamic acid on synovial type II collagen by tissue transglutaminase, producing deamidated collagen peptides structurally similar to citrullinated peptides","D":"Post-translational ubiquitination of lysine residues on joint structural proteins by E3 ubiquitin ligase complexes activated during synovial NF-kB signaling, creating ubiquitinated neoepitopes on collagen","E":"Post-translational acetylation of histone H3 lysine 27 by CBP/p300 acetyltransferases in synovial fibroblasts undergoing epigenetic remodeling during sustained inflammatory conditions in the synovium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Citrullination is the post-translational conversion of arginine to citrulline catalyzed by PAD enzymes (primarily PAD2 and PAD4). This removes a positive charge from arginine (positively charged guanidinium group converted to neutral citrulline), altering the shape and charge of proteins. Citrullinated forms of fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin are the primary ACPA targets. HLA-DR4 binds citrullinated peptides with high affinity, driving CD4+ T cell activation. Deamidation by transglutaminase is relevant to celiac disease (gliadin). Phosphorylation and ubiquitination do not generate ACPA targets. Histone acetylation is an epigenetic modification with no known ACPA-generating role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA targets citrullinated proteins. Citrullination = arginine to citrulline via PAD enzymes. Key ACPA targets: fibrinogen, vimentin, alpha-enolase, type II collagen. HLA-DR4 shared epitope presents citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells. Molecular mimicry: citrullinated protein epitopes resemble certain microbial antigens. COMLEX: ACPA can be positive years before clinical RA; use anti-CCP test (second-generation ACPA assay) for diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid arthritis has a prevalence of 0.25% to 1% in the United States and peaks in the third to fourth decades, with approximately 50% of disease risk attributed to genetic factors. It is three times more common in females than males. Which statement best characterizes the genetic and environmental interaction in RA pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"RA is a single-gene disorder caused by HLA-DR4 homozygosity alone, which is sufficient to cause the disease without any environmental cofactors given that genetic factors account for 50% of disease risk","B":"The female predominance in RA reflects X-chromosome inactivation skewing that silences the FOXP3 locus preferentially in females, leading to Treg deficiency that unmasks citrullinated joint antigens","C":"RA is a polygenic, multifactorial disorder in which HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 variants interact with smoking and periodontal infection to trigger autoimmunity against citrullinated self-proteins","D":"The 50% genetic contribution to RA risk is accounted for entirely by HLA-DR4, making non-HLA genes such as PTPN22 statistically insignificant compared to the dominant MHC class II contribution","E":"RA develops only in individuals who are compound heterozygous for HLA-DR4 and HLA-DR1 in the shared epitope alleles; single HLA-DR4 homozygosity alone has lower RA risk than compound heterozygosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RA is a polygenic disorder. Genetic factors (primarily HLA-DR4 shared epitope, PTPN22, and other loci) account for approximately 50% of RA risk. Environmental factors — especially cigarette smoking (activates PAD, promotes citrullination), periodontal infection (P. gingivalis PAD enzyme), and possibly other infections — account for the remainder. Neither genetics nor environment alone is sufficient; both are required. The female predominance is real (3:1) but reflects hormonal influences on immunity (estrogen drives B cell activation, Th2 responses) rather than FOXP3 silencing. HLA-DR4 is the dominant genetic risk factor but non-HLA genes including PTPN22 make statistically significant independent contributions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 50% RA risk = genetic (HLA-DR4 + PTPN22 + other loci). 50% = environmental (smoking + periodontitis + hormones). Female predominance 3:1. Peak onset 3rd-4th decade. COMLEX: RA increases cardiovascular risk beyond traditional risk factors; this excess risk is partially mediated by systemic inflammation and should be screened and treated aggressively.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Based on the dramatic clinical response to TNF antagonists, TNF is considered a key mediator of inflammation and joint damage in RA. Which best describes the primary cellular source of TNF in the inflamed RA synovium and its primary mechanism of articular cartilage destruction?","choices":{"A":"Synovial macrophages are the primary TNF source; TNF recruits activated leukocytes and stimulates synovial fibroblasts to secrete matrix metalloproteinases that degrade hyaline cartilage and destroy the joint","B":"CD4+ Th17 cells are the primary TNF producers in RA synovium; TNF recruits neutrophils and monocytes through CXCR1 and CXCR2 receptor signaling on vascular endothelium within the synovial membrane","C":"Regulatory T cells in synovial germinal centers produce TNF as a pro-survival signal for ACPA-secreting plasma cells; TNF acts directly on chondrocytes to induce autophagy-mediated cartilage matrix degradation","D":"Endothelial cells lining synovial capillaries secrete TNF in response to mechanical shear stress; TNF signals NK cells to upregulate RANKL expression, driving subchondral osteoclast activation and bone resorption","E":"B cells in subsynovial germinal centers are the predominant producers of TNF; TNF acts on dendritic cells to upregulate MHC II and CD80/CD86 co-stimulatory molecule expression for T cell activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TNF is produced primarily by synovial macrophages in RA and acts via multiple downstream mechanisms: (1) recruiting activated leukocytes to the joint through upregulation of adhesion molecules on endothelium; (2) stimulating synovial fibroblasts (type B synoviocytes) to secrete MMPs including MMP-1, MMP-3, and MMP-13 that degrade type II collagen and aggrecan; (3) inducing IL-6 production from fibroblasts, amplifying the inflammatory cascade. IL-17 from Th17 cells (not TNF) primarily recruits neutrophils. RANKL from T cells (not TNF) drives osteoclast activation. The therapeutic efficacy of etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, and similar agents confirms TNF centrality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF from macrophages = central RA cytokine. Mechanism: recruits leukocytes + stimulates MMP secretion from fibroblasts + cartilage destruction. Biologics: etanercept (fusion protein), infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab. TNF antagonism risk: TB reactivation (screen with TST/IGRA before starting). COMLEX: TB reactivation risk with anti-TNF therapy — must treat latent TB before initiating.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Periarticular bone erosion is a radiographic hallmark of RA and results from osteoclast activation by a ligand expressed on activated T cells in the inflamed synovium. Which molecule is this ligand, and what receptor on osteoclast precursors does it engage?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma expressed on activated Th1 cells binds IFN-gamma receptor on pre-osteoclasts and inhibits osteoclast differentiation; this explains why RA patients on IFN-gamma therapy paradoxically have worse bone erosion","B":"IL-17A expressed on activated Th17 cells binds IL-17RA on osteoclast precursors and directly drives their differentiation into mature bone-resorbing osteoclasts through STAT3-dependent NF-kB activation","C":"TRAIL (TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand) expressed on activated NK cells in the synovium binds DR4 and DR5 on osteoclast precursors and triggers their apoptosis, paradoxically reducing bone erosion","D":"RANKL (receptor activator of NF-kB ligand) expressed on activated T cells binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, driving their differentiation into mature multinucleated osteoclasts that cause periarticular erosions","E":"M-CSF (macrophage colony-stimulating factor) expressed on activated B cells in synovial germinal centers binds c-Fms on osteoclast precursors, driving their proliferation before RANKL-independent differentiation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RANKL (also called TRANCE, OPGL, or ODF) is expressed on activated T cells and synovial fibroblasts in the RA pannus. It binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB and c-Fos signaling that drives osteoclastogenesis. OPG (osteoprotegerin) acts as a decoy receptor that normally prevents osteoclast overactivation, but this balance is disrupted in RA. IFN-gamma actually inhibits osteoclast differentiation (paradoxical finding). IL-17 promotes neutrophil/monocyte recruitment rather than directly driving osteoclast differentiation. Denosumab (anti-RANKL antibody) is approved for osteoporosis and reduces bone erosions in RA when added to methotrexate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL on T cells/fibroblasts + RANK on osteoclast precursors = bone erosion in RA. OPG (osteoprotegerin) = decoy receptor that blocks this interaction. Denosumab = anti-RANKL = approved for osteoporosis + reduces RA erosions. Board trap: IFN-gamma inhibits osteoclast differentiation despite being pro-inflammatory overall. COMLEX: osteoporosis is a major comorbidity in RA due to both systemic inflammation and corticosteroid use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"CD4+ T helper cells in the RA synovium are polarized into distinct subsets that produce different cytokines with different inflammatory consequences. Which cell type is the primary source of IL-17 in RA, and what is the primary cellular target and functional result of IL-17 signaling in joint inflammation?","choices":{"A":"CD4+ Th1 cells are the primary IL-17 producers in RA synovium; IL-17 acts on chondrocytes to upregulate aggrecanase and MMP-13 expression, producing direct articular cartilage degradation without leukocyte recruitment","B":"CD4+ Th17 cells are the primary IL-17 producers; IL-17 recruits neutrophils and monocytes into the joint by upregulating CXCL8 and CCL2 on synovial fibroblasts and endothelial cells, amplifying leukocyte influx","C":"Synovial mast cells produce IL-17 after FcεRI cross-linking by IgE anti-citrullinated protein antibodies; IL-17 then activates synovial macrophages to produce TNF and IL-1 in a mast cell-dependent cascade","D":"FOXP3+ regulatory T cells paradoxically produce IL-17 in the inflamed RA synovium due to phenotypic conversion driven by elevated IL-6 and TGF-beta, switching Tregs into pathogenic Th17-like effector cells","E":"NK cells are the dominant source of IL-17 in RA synovium; IL-17 acts on subsynovial B cells to promote class switching from IgM to IgG4, reducing complement-fixing capacity and immune complex pathology"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th17 cells are a distinct CD4+ T cell subset that produces IL-17A and IL-17F in response to IL-6 + TGF-beta + IL-23 (for maintenance). In RA synovium, IL-17 acts on synovial fibroblasts and endothelial cells to upregulate CXCL8 (IL-8, neutrophil chemokine) and CCL2 (monocyte chemoattractant), amplifying leukocyte recruitment. IL-17 also promotes osteoclastogenesis and MMP production. Th1 cells are not the primary IL-17 source — they produce IFN-gamma. Tregs can lose FOXP3 expression in inflamed environments with high IL-6, but this is a minor contributor. Mast cells are not the primary IL-17 producers in RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th17 + IL-17 = recruit neutrophils and monocytes to RA joint. Th1 + IFN-gamma = activate macrophages. Macrophages + TNF/IL-1/IL-6 = MMP secretion and cartilage destruction. RANKL on T cells = osteoclast activation. Secukinumab/ixekizumab = anti-IL-17A biologics (used more in psoriatic arthritis/AS than RA). COMLEX: IL-17 plays an even larger role in psoriasis and spondyloarthritis than in RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"IgM and IgA autoantibodies present in approximately 80% of RA patients collectively referred to as rheumatoid factor target a specific structural region of IgG. Which statement best describes the molecular target and structural properties of IgM rheumatoid factor?","choices":{"A":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a pentameric antibody that binds the Fc region of IgG molecules, forming high-molecular-weight immune complexes that can fix complement and deposit in synovial tissue","B":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a dimeric antibody that binds the variable region (Fab) of IgG, blocking antigen recognition and preventing ACPA from binding citrullinated peptides in the joint space","C":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a monomeric antibody that binds the J chain of secretory IgA in mucosal tissues, forming complexes that activate toll-like receptors on synovial macrophages via HMGB1 signaling","D":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a pentameric antibody that targets the variable domain of IgE molecules, blocking FcεRI binding on mast cells and reducing histamine-mediated synovial vascular permeability in RA joints","E":"IgM rheumatoid factor binds the hinge region of IgM molecules rather than IgG, forming layered immune complexes that uniquely deposit in periarticular subchondral bone rather than the synovial membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rheumatoid factor is defined as an IgM (or IgA or IgG) antibody that binds the Fc region of IgG. IgM RF forms pentameric structures due to the intrinsic pentameric organization of IgM, making it highly effective at forming large immune complexes. These RF-IgG complexes can fix complement via the classical pathway (C1q binds the Fc region of complexed IgG) and deposit in the synovium, triggering inflammation. The Fc region of IgG (not the Fab, hinge, IgM J chain, or IgE) is the specific target. RF was historically defined as the antibody detected by the latex fixation test, which relies on RF-IgG interaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF = IgM (primarily) anti-Fc of IgG. IgM = pentamer = 10 antigen-binding sites = very efficient at forming immune complexes. Detected by latex fixation test or nephelometry. Present in 70-80% of RA but also in Sjogren, SLE, healthy elderly, SBE, TB. COMLEX: high-titer RF is associated with extra-articular manifestations of RA including rheumatoid nodules, vasculitis, and Felty syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies are detected in up to 70% of RA patients and target arginine-to-citrulline modified epitopes on several joint proteins. Which list best identifies the proteins whose citrullinated forms serve as the primary ACPA targets in the RA synovium?","choices":{"A":"Hyaluronic acid, type IV collagen, laminin, and fibronectin, which are citrullinated by PAD2 on the surface of endothelial cells during angiogenesis in the inflamed synovium of RA patients","B":"Glycosaminoglycans, aggrecan, versican, and decorin, which are citrullinated by PAD4 secreted by activated neutrophils as part of neutrophil extracellular trap formation in the joint space","C":"Synapsin-1, tau protein, myelin basic protein, and neurofilament light chain, which are citrullinated by PAD6 in perisynovial nerve endings during nociceptive sensitization in chronic RA pain","D":"IgG1 Fc region, complement C3b, fibrin, and MMP-13, which are citrullinated by PAD1 released during lysosomal exocytosis in activated macrophages during the initial innate immune response to joint injury","E":"Fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin, which are citrullinated by PAD enzymes and serve as the primary protein targets of ACPA; these are all abundant in inflamed synovial tissue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The four canonical ACPA targets are fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin — all proteins abundant in synovial tissue that become citrullinated by PAD2 and PAD4 during inflammation. Fibrinogen citrullination is particularly important because fibrin deposits are a major component of the synovial exudate in RA. Type II collagen is the main structural protein of articular cartilage. Alpha-enolase (an intracellular glycolytic enzyme) becomes accessible when cells undergo apoptosis. Vimentin is an intermediate filament citrullinated during cell death. None of the other options lists the canonical ACPA targets — glycosaminoglycans, myelin proteins, and complement components are not primary ACPA targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA targets citrullinated: fibrinogen, vimentin, alpha-enolase, type II collagen. PAD2 and PAD4 are the key enzymes. Anti-CCP (anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide) is the clinical ACPA test using a synthetic cyclic citrullinated peptide as the antigen. Positive in up to 70% of RA (Robbins Ch 26; 60-80% in Robbins Table 6.10) and more specific than RF (FA p113). COMLEX: ACPA is positive years before joint symptoms in high-risk individuals, making it useful for early identification.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid factor is present in approximately 80% of individuals with RA. Which statement best describes the diagnostic limitations of rheumatoid factor as a test for RA?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid factor has greater specificity than ACPA for RA because RF targets joint-specific citrullinated peptides on fibrinogen, making it highly positive only when synovial PAD enzymes are actively producing citrullinated antigens","B":"Rheumatoid factor is present exclusively in seropositive RA and is never detected in healthy individuals, healthy elderly, or patients with other autoimmune diseases or systemic infections","C":"Rheumatoid factor lacks specificity for RA because it can also be detected in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, chronic infections including subacute bacterial endocarditis and tuberculosis, and in healthy elderly individuals","D":"Rheumatoid factor is more specific than ACPA for RA because IgM RF targets only the Fc region of IgG found in inflamed joint tissue rather than ubiquitous citrullinated proteins distributed throughout the body","E":"A negative rheumatoid factor definitively excludes RA because the 80% sensitivity means that only a trivial minority of patients with RA are seronegative, making seronegative RA a misclassification rather than a true phenotype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RF lacks specificity for RA — it targets the Fc region of IgG, which is a non-organ-specific structure. RF is found in 5% to 10% of older adults in the general population (course SDL 44), about 75% of Sjogren syndrome patients (Robbins Ch 6), and in SLE and patients with subacute bacterial endocarditis, hepatitis B/C, tuberculosis, and other chronic infections. RF detection in conditions without RA is explained by the fact that B cells can generate anti-IgG responses during any chronic immune activation that produces large amounts of IgG. ACPA is far more specific than RF for RA (FA p113).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF = 80% of RA (Robbins Ch 26; 60-70% in Table 6.10), not specific. Non-RA conditions with positive RF: Sjogren (about 75%), SLE, SBE, viral hepatitis, TB, healthy older adults (5-10%). ACPA (anti-CCP) = up to 70% of RA (60-80% in Table 6.10), more specific. Higher RF titers mark more aggressive disease. Remember: 20% of RA is seronegative (RF and ACPA negative). COMLEX: seronegative RA diagnosis requires clinical and radiographic criteria; joint aspiration with synovial fluid analysis may help confirm inflammatory arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman is diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis. Stored serum samples from her years as a regular blood donor are tested for anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies. Which finding is most consistent with published studies?","choices":{"A":"ACPA first appears after synovitis has been present for at least six months","B":"ACPA can be detected up to a decade before clinical arthritis begins","C":"ACPA first appears with the onset of morning stiffness and is absent before it","D":"ACPA is present from birth in carriers of the HLA-DR4 shared epitope","E":"ACPA appears in the weeks after methotrexate is started, as a drug effect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Several studies have shown ACPAs up to a decade before the onset of clinical disease, and in early undifferentiated arthritis ACPAs predict later erosive RA. RF may also appear several years before clinical RA, but it is far less specific. ACPA therefore does not wait for six months of synovitis or for morning stiffness. HLA-DR shared epitope alleles shape the ACPA response but do not mean the antibody is present from birth. Methotrexate does not induce ACPA; effective treatment can lower RF titers. Sources: course SDL 44 reading (Rheumatology Ch 30, Laboratory tests in rheumatic disorders, ACPA and RF, p10–11).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA is more specific than RF for RA, and the cyclic citrullinated peptide (CCP) test is the usual assay (Rheumatology Ch 30). COMLEX: ACPA is found in about 25 percent of RF-negative RA patients (Rheumatology Ch 30).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — autoantibodies and their disease associations"},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the clinical significance of ACPA compared with RF in terms of diagnostic specificity and potential role in driving disease persistence in RA?","choices":{"A":"ACPA and RF have equivalent diagnostic sensitivity and specificity for RA; neither contributes to disease persistence because both are epiphenomena of B cell activation rather than serving as pathogenic mediators","B":"ACPA has lower specificity than RF for RA because ACPA targets ubiquitous citrullinated proteins found in muscle, skin, and lung, producing false positives in interstitial lung disease and dermatomyositis patients","C":"RF has greater specificity than ACPA for RA diagnosis and has a stronger association with HLA-DR4 shared epitope, predicting erosive disease and extra-articular complications better than ACPA in seropositive patients","D":"ACPA has greater specificity than RF for RA and, in combination with T cells reactive to citrullinated proteins, ACPA may actively contribute to disease severity and persistence rather than serving only as a passive biomarker","E":"ACPA and RF together have combined specificity approaching 100% for RA, making the simultaneous order of both tests sufficient to diagnose RA without clinical examination or radiographic evaluation of joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ACPA has higher specificity for RA than RF (FA p113; course SDL 44). More importantly, ACPA is not merely a biomarker — data suggest that in combination with T cells reactive to citrullinated proteins, ACPA may contribute to disease severity and persistence through FcgammaR engagement on macrophages, complement activation, and possibly direct effects on osteoclasts. This pathogenic role of ACPA distinguishes it from RF, which deposits in joints as immune complexes but has less evidence for independent pathogenicity. ACPA positivity also predicts more erosive disease course, extra-articular manifestations, and reduced likelihood of spontaneous remission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA (anti-CCP) = most specific RA antibody (up to 70% of RA in Robbins Ch 26; 60-80% in Table 6.10). RF = 80% in Ch 26 (60-70% in Table 6.10) but less specific. ACPA precedes symptoms by years. ACPA+ = worse prognosis (more erosive). Both positive together = highest diagnostic certainty. COMLEX: ACPA is measured as anti-CCP in clinical practice; the cyclic citrullinated peptide substrate increases sensitivity vs. linear peptides. High-titer ACPA predicts need for early aggressive DMARD therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — B cell activation and germinal center formation in the synovium produce the RA-specific autoantibodies covered in SDL 28"},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately 20% of RA patients are seronegative for both RF and ACPA. Which statement best describes the clinical characteristics and prognosis of seronegative RA compared to seropositive RA?","choices":{"A":"Seronegative RA is uniformly more severe than seropositive RA because the absence of autoantibodies indicates a more aggressive T cell-dominant pathogenesis with higher rates of joint ankylosis and disability","B":"Seronegative RA is characterized by markedly elevated complement levels because the absence of immune complex formation prevents classical pathway complement consumption in the joint and synovial tissue","C":"Seronegative RA patients are more likely to have disease that stabilizes or regresses spontaneously, and in these patients the diagnosis is based on clinical and radiographic findings rather than serologic testing","D":"Seronegative RA is always associated with HLA-B27 positivity because the absence of ACPA and RF indicates the disease is actually a spondyloarthropathy rather than true primary inflammatory arthritis","E":"Seronegative RA is defined by the absence of synovial inflammation on biopsy and represents a non-destructive arthralgia without pannus formation or the periarticular erosions characteristic of seropositive disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Seronegative RA (negative for both RF and ACPA) represents approximately 20% of RA cases and has a generally more favorable prognosis — these patients are more likely to have disease that stabilizes or even regresses spontaneously compared with seropositive patients. Diagnosis relies on clinical criteria: symmetric inflammatory arthritis with morning stiffness, appropriate joint distribution (MCP, PIP, wrist, ankle), elevated inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), and radiographic evidence of erosive joint disease. Seronegative RA is NOT the same as spondyloarthropathy (which has distinct features: axial involvement, HLA-B27, enthesitis). Seronegative RA still has synovial inflammation and pannus histologically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Seronegative RA = 20% of cases, RF negative + ACPA negative. Prognosis: better chance of spontaneous remission/stabilization. Diagnosis: clinical + radiographic (not serologic). Seronegative does NOT mean HLA-B27 positive or spondyloarthropathy. COMLEX: seronegative inflammatory arthritis with axial involvement, sacroiliitis, or enthesitis should prompt workup for spondyloarthritis rather than RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — B cell activation and germinal center formation in the synovium produce the RA-specific autoantibodies covered in SDL 28"},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male smoker is evaluated for a 6-month history of symmetric MCP and PIP joint swelling with 90-minute morning stiffness. He has a 10-year history of chronic periodontitis. ACPA is positive at 480 U/mL. HLA-DR4 genotyping is positive. Which statement best explains how smoking and periodontitis synergize to initiate RA in this genetically susceptible individual?","choices":{"A":"Both smoking and periodontitis activate the alternative complement pathway in synovial tissue, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that recruit mast cells and initiate synovitis independent of ACPA or T cell activation","B":"Both smoking and periodontitis promote post-translational citrullination via PAD enzyme activation — smoking in lung tissue and P. gingivalis PAD in oral tissue — generating citrullinated neoantigens that are presented by HLA-DR4 to drive ACPA production","C":"Cigarette smoking directly inhibits regulatory T cells in cervical lymph nodes draining periodontal tissue, allowing autoreactive B cells specific for synovial type II collagen to escape peripheral tolerance checkpoints","D":"Periodontitis generates IgM antibodies against P. gingivalis that cross-react with the Fc region of synovial IgG through molecular mimicry, generating a secondary rheumatoid factor in susceptible HLA-DR4 positive individuals","E":"Both smoking and periodontitis upregulate TLR4 signaling in synovial macrophages via circulating LPS, inducing MyD88-NF-kB activation and constitutive TNF production that is entirely independent of T cell or B cell involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Smoking activates PAD enzymes in bronchial and alveolar cells, promoting widespread protein citrullination and ACPA formation years before joint symptoms. Porphyromonas gingivalis, the dominant organism in chronic periodontitis, is unique among human-associated bacteria in expressing its own PAD enzyme (PPAD) that can citrullinate both bacterial and host proteins. Both exposures thus generate citrullinated protein neoantigens. When these neoantigens are presented by HLA-DR4 (which has a peptide-binding groove particularly suited to citrullinated peptides), they activate CD4+ T cells and drive B cell ACPA class switching. The synergy between multiple PAD-activating environmental exposures plus HLA-DR4 explains this patient's high ACPA titer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two canonical environmental triggers for RA citrullination: (1) cigarette smoking (PAD in lung), (2) P. gingivalis periodontitis (PPAD bacterial PAD). Both generate citrullinated neoantigens presented by HLA-DR4. ACPA can appear up to a decade before arthritis (pre-clinical window; course SDL 44). COMLEX: periodontal disease screening and treatment is recommended in RA patients; treatment of periodontitis may reduce RA disease activity in some studies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with 8-year RA history presents for follow-up. Wrist radiographs show periarticular erosions, narrowed joint space, and juxtaarticular osteopenia. Synovial biopsy of the wrist joint shows edematous, hyperplastic tissue with lymphoid follicles, plasma cells, fibrin-rich exudate with neutrophils on the surface, and abundant new vessel formation. What is the term for the pathologic structure formed by these combined changes?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid nodule, a subcutaneous mass with central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by a rim of palisaded macrophages and lymphocytes, most commonly found over extensor surfaces of the forearm and elbows","B":"Tophus, a mass composed of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by a giant cell inflammatory reaction found in periarticular soft tissue, bursae, and tendon sheaths in patients with chronic gout","C":"Osteophyte, a bony outgrowth at joint margins formed by endochondral ossification driven by TGF-beta and BMP signaling from subchondral bone during mechanical stress in osteoarthritis","D":"Pannus, a mass of edematous synovium, inflammatory cells, granulation tissue, and fibroblasts that grows over articular cartilage causing progressive erosion and eventually leading to fibrous or bony ankylosis","E":"Fibrous ankylosis, the end-stage fibrotic bridge between apposing bone surfaces after complete cartilage destruction in late RA, representing the terminal consequence of pannus erosion rather than its active form"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pannus is the pathognomonic lesion of RA: a proliferative mass of edematous synovium, inflammatory cells (CD4+ T cells, B cells with germinal centers, plasma cells, macrophages), granulation tissue, and synovial fibroblasts. It grows over and erodes articular cartilage through MMP secretion and direct cellular invasion. Neutrophilic fibrin exudate on the surface and angiogenesis are also hallmarks. The pannus eventually bridges the joint space to form fibrous ankylosis, which ossifies into bony ankylosis. Rheumatoid nodules are separate subcutaneous lesions (central fibrinoid necrosis + palisaded macrophages). Tophi are urate deposits in gout. Osteophytes are OA-specific bony outgrowths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pannus = proliferative synovium + inflammatory cells + granulation tissue + fibroblasts that erodes cartilage. RA histology: (1) synovial cell hyperplasia, (2) dense CD4+ T cell/B cell/plasma cell infiltrate with germinal centers, (3) angiogenesis, (4) neutrophilic fibrin exudate on surface, (5) osteoclastic subchondral bone erosion. Board distinction: pannus erodes from the joint edge inward in RA; OA degenerates from the weight-bearing surface downward. COMLEX: pannus formation correlates with elevated ESR/CRP and positive ACPA titer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman presents with symmetric swelling and tenderness of multiple joints of both hands, 90-minute morning stiffness, elevated ESR, and positive ACPA. Radiographs show periarticular erosions at the wrists. Which joint distribution in the hand characteristically distinguishes RA from osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"RA primarily affects the distal interphalangeal joints and first carpometacarpal joint with Bouchard nodes, while OA affects the MCP joints and wrists with morning stiffness lasting over 60 minutes","B":"RA produces asymmetric monoarticular or oligoarticular involvement of the knee and hip, while OA produces symmetric polyarticular involvement of all finger joints including MCP, PIP, and DIP joints equally","C":"RA preferentially involves the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints while sparing the distal interphalangeal joints, while OA preferentially affects the DIP joints and the first carpometacarpal joint","D":"RA and OA have identical joint distribution in the hand; the two conditions are distinguished only by duration of morning stiffness (greater than 60 minutes in RA versus less than 30 minutes in OA) and inflammatory markers","E":"RA characteristically begins in the large joints of the axial skeleton including the spine and sacroiliac joints before progressing distally to the small joints of the hands and feet over a period of years"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The joint distribution in the hand is a classic board-tested distinguishing feature: RA involves MCP and PIP joints (and spares DIP joints) + wrists, ankles, and elbows in a symmetric pattern. OA involves DIP joints (producing Heberden nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard nodes) + first CMC (base of thumb) + first MTP. Both can involve the knee and hip. In RA, the MCP involvement leads to characteristic ulnar deviation; PIP involvement contributes to swan-neck and boutonniere deformities. The DIP sparing in RA is a distinguishing board feature. Morning stiffness >60 minutes supports RA (OA morning stiffness typically <30 min and improves with use).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA = MCP + PIP + wrist (DIP spared). OA = DIP + PIP + first CMC (MCP less affected). Both can affect knee and hip but by different mechanisms. Ulnar deviation = RA. Heberden nodes (DIP) + Bouchard nodes (PIP) = OA. COMLEX: RA morning stiffness lasts >1 h and eases with activity; OA stiffness lasts <30 min, while OA pain worsens with use. This symptom pattern helps differentiate inflammatory from non-inflammatory arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with moderate-to-severe RA is starting etanercept therapy. Pre-treatment tuberculin skin test shows 18 mm induration. Her rheumatologist explains the dual role of TNF in RA pathogenesis and TB immunity. Which best explains why TNF blockade both treats RA and increases risk of TB reactivation?","choices":{"A":"TNF drives synovial macrophage activation and cartilage destruction in RA and also maintains granuloma integrity around latent M. tuberculosis, so TNF blockade calms the joints but impairs mycobacterial containment","B":"TNF antagonists reduce RANKL expression on T cells in the synovium; this also reduces macrophage differentiation from monocyte precursors in the bone marrow, eliminating the cellular basis of granuloma formation in lymph nodes","C":"TNF blockade directly inhibits IL-12 production by macrophages, preventing Th1 differentiation; without Th1 responses, IFN-gamma production is abolished and mycobacterial killing by activated macrophages is eliminated","D":"Etanercept binds both TNF and lymphotoxin-alpha; lymphotoxin-alpha is required for secondary lymphoid organ formation, so its blockade eliminates germinal centers and all memory B cell responses to M. tuberculosis antigens","E":"TNF antagonism eliminates NK cell cytotoxicity against M. tuberculosis-infected macrophages because NK cells require TNF as a survival signal to maintain perforin, granzyme B, and NKG2D receptor expression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TNF plays a dual physiologic role that explains this paradox. In RA synovium: TNF from macrophages recruits leukocytes, activates synovial fibroblasts to secrete MMPs, and drives cartilage destruction — therefore blocking TNF reduces joint inflammation effectively. In latent TB granulomas: TNF is required to maintain the structural integrity of the macrophage granuloma that contains dormant M. tuberculosis. TNF blockade dissolves granuloma structure, allowing mycobacterial reactivation, dissemination, and development of active TB. This is why all patients must be screened for latent TB (with TST or IGRA) and treated for latent TB before starting anti-TNF therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Screen for latent TB before all TNF antagonists. Treat latent TB with isoniazid x 9 months (or rifampin x 4 months). Risk is highest with infliximab/adalimumab (monoclonal antibodies that fully block TNF) compared to etanercept (which also neutralizes lymphotoxin-alpha). COMLEX: disseminated TB from anti-TNF reactivation can present with extrapulmonary disease (miliary TB, TB meningitis) rather than classic pulmonary presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old male with RA has a synovial biopsy showing dense lymphoid follicles with CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, and activated macrophages with high MHC II and co-stimulatory molecule expression. Which cytokine produced by CD4+ T cells is primarily responsible for activating these macrophages and resident synovial cells in the biopsy?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 produced by CD4+ Th2 cells, which binds IL-4R on synovial macrophages and drives their differentiation toward an anti-inflammatory M2 phenotype that suppresses germinal center responses and reduces synovial inflammation","B":"IL-17A produced by CD4+ Th17 cells, which binds IL-17RA on synovial macrophages to upregulate CXCL8 and G-CSF expression, predominantly recruiting neutrophils rather than classically activating resident tissue macrophages","C":"IL-10 produced by CD4+ Treg cells, which binds IL-10R on synovial macrophages to suppress MHC II expression and co-stimulatory molecule upregulation, reducing rather than increasing T cell activation in the joint","D":"IFN-gamma produced by CD4+ Th1 cells, which activates synovial macrophages through the JAK1-STAT1 pathway, upregulating MHC II expression, co-stimulatory molecules CD80/CD86, and pro-inflammatory cytokine production","E":"TGF-beta produced by CD4+ Treg cells, which acts on synovial macrophages to induce M2 polarization and fibroblast activation, contributing to pannus fibrosis but suppressing rather than promoting the M1 inflammatory phenotype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IFN-gamma is the prototypical macrophage-activating cytokine, produced predominantly by CD4+ Th1 cells (and CD8+ T cells and NK cells). In the RA synovium, IFN-gamma binds IFN-gamma receptor on macrophages, activating JAK1/JAK2-STAT1 signaling. This upregulates MHC II expression (amplifying antigen presentation), CD80/CD86 co-stimulatory molecules (amplifying T cell activation), and pro-inflammatory cytokine production (TNF, IL-1, IL-12). IL-4 drives M2 anti-inflammatory polarization (anti-RA direction). IL-17 recruits neutrophils/monocytes but does not classically activate macrophages. IL-10 suppresses macrophages. TGF-beta drives M2 polarization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma from Th1 = classical macrophage activator (M1 polarization). In RA synovium: Th1 (IFN-gamma activates macrophages) + Th17 (IL-17 recruits neutrophils) + macrophage TNF/IL-1/IL-6 (destroys cartilage) + T cell RANKL (erodes bone). IFN-gamma upregulates MHC II and co-stimulatory molecules, amplifying the antigen presentation cycle. COMLEX: IFN-gamma drives granuloma formation in TB and sarcoidosis, linking innate and adaptive immunity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with active RA has elevated ESR 85 mm/h, CRP 42 mg/L, thrombocytosis, elevated fibrinogen, and normocytic normochromic anemia. She is afebrile. Which cytokine is the primary driver of the systemic acute phase response including the elevated CRP, fibrinogen, and thrombocytosis in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells, which acts on hepatocytes via STAT1 to upregulate fibrinogen, CRP, serum amyloid A, and hepcidin, and which also directly stimulates megakaryocytes to produce excess platelets causing thrombocytosis","B":"IL-17A from Th17 cells, which acts on hepatocytes via the STAT3 pathway to upregulate all acute phase reactant production including CRP, fibrinogen, complement C3, and thrombopoietin in RA patients with active disease","C":"RANKL expressed on activated T cells, which acts on hepatocytes via NF-kB to stimulate acute phase protein synthesis while simultaneously driving osteoclast activation and periarticular bone erosion in the inflamed joint","D":"TNF-alpha from synovial macrophages, which is the primary driver of the systemic acute phase response in RA by acting on hepatocytes via NF-kB to upregulate CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, and thrombopoietin directly","E":"IL-6 from synovial macrophages and fibroblasts, which acts on hepatocytes through the JAK1-STAT3 signaling pathway to drive synthesis of CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, and hepcidin, causing anemia of chronic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary cytokine driving the systemic acute phase response in RA. Synovial macrophages and fibroblasts produce large amounts of IL-6 in the inflamed joint, which enters the circulation and acts on hepatocytes via JAK1/TYK2-STAT3 signaling to upregulate CRP (the most sensitive acute phase reactant), fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, complement C3, and hepcidin. Hepcidin decreases ferroportin expression on enterocytes and macrophages, trapping iron intracellularly and causing the normocytic normochromic anemia of chronic inflammation. IL-6 also increases hepatic thrombopoietin production, explaining reactive thrombocytosis. Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R) normalizes CRP and ESR within days of infusion, confirming IL-6 as the primary acute phase driver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 = main driver of acute phase response. IL-6 hepatic effects: CRP, fibrinogen, SAA, complement, hepcidin (anemia of chronic disease). Tocilizumab = anti-IL-6 receptor. CRP drops rapidly with tocilizumab. Anemia of chronic disease: hepcidin blocks iron release from macrophages and absorption from gut; treat by treating underlying RA. COMLEX: tocilizumab is also approved for cytokine release syndrome (CRS) in CAR-T therapy and COVID-19 pneumonia, reflecting IL-6 centrality across inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with long-standing seropositive RA has an RF titer of 1:640, bilateral subcutaneous nodules over the elbows, and mononeuritis multiplex. How do IgM RF pentamers mechanistically contribute to joint damage in RA?","choices":{"A":"IgM RF binds the variable (Fab) domain of IgG in synovial fluid, sterically blocking ACPA from accessing citrullinated fibrinogen, which paradoxically reduces joint inflammation by preventing ACPA-driven complement activation","B":"IgM RF is a monomeric antibody that binds the J chain of secretory IgA dimers in synovial fluid, activating the lectin complement pathway selectively at the synovial membrane rather than in the joint space","C":"IgM RF binds the hinge region of IgM molecules in synovial germinal center B cells, disrupting B cell receptor signaling and paradoxically reducing ACPA production by competing with BCR-bound IgG for B cell surface receptors","D":"IgM RF pentamers bind the Fc region of IgG, forming large immune complexes that activate classical complement via C1q, generating C3a, C5a, and the membrane attack complex that drive leukocyte recruitment in the joint","E":"IgM RF binds the CH1 domain of IgG heavy chains in circulation, disrupting FcRn-mediated IgG recycling and markedly reducing IgG half-life in patients with high RF titers and active RA disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IgM RF pentamers have 10 binding sites for IgG Fc regions, making them highly efficient at forming immune complexes. These RF-IgG complexes activate the classical complement pathway because C1q binds the Fc regions of aggregated IgG, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins (which increase vascular permeability and recruit neutrophils/monocytes) and C5b-9 membrane attack complex. The deposited complexes in synovium trigger leukocyte activation and release of lysosomal enzymes including cathepsins and MMPs, amplifying joint destruction. High RF titers correlate with extra-articular RA manifestations including rheumatoid nodules (fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by macrophages), vasculitis, and Felty syndrome (RA + splenomegaly + neutropenia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM RF pentamers + IgG Fc = large immune complexes + C1q binding + classical complement activation. Extra-articular RA with high-titer RF: rheumatoid nodules, vasculitis, Felty syndrome (RA + splenomegaly + neutropenia), pleuritis/pericarditis. Board association: high-titer RF + extra-articular disease = more aggressive RA needing earlier biologic therapy. COMLEX: rheumatoid nodules have central fibrinoid necrosis + palisaded macrophages; must distinguish from gouty tophi (urate crystals + giant cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A laboratory reports a patient's rheumatoid factor from an assay that detects IgM RF. The rheumatologist explains that rheumatoid factor is a family of autoantibodies directed at the Fc portion of IgG. Which statement about RF isotypes is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"RF is an IgM antibody by definition, because a pentamer is needed to bind IgG Fc","B":"RF can be IgG, IgA, or IgM, and IgA and IgM RF are the forms readily detected in blood","C":"RF is an IgE antibody, which explains the mast cell activation in rheumatoid synovitis","D":"RF is IgD that stays bound to naive B cells and is not secreted into the blood","E":"RF is IgG4, a subclass that cannot fix complement or form immune complexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rheumatoid factor is the name for autoantibodies against the Fc chains of IgG; laboratories usually test for IgM RF, but RF can belong to all the major classes, IgG, IgA, and IgM, and occasionally IgD and IgE, and the forms easily detected in the circulation are IgA and IgM RF. Robbins describes RF as IgM and IgA autoantibodies to the Fc of IgG, present in 80 percent of patients with RA. RF is therefore not IgM by definition, not mainly IgE, not a membrane-bound IgD, and not restricted to IgG4; RF immune complexes deposit in joints. Sources: course SDL 44 reading (Rheumatology Ch 30, Rheumatoid factor, p10); course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, RA pathogenesis, p77–78).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF = antibodies to the Fc of IgG; ACPA = antibodies to citrullinated peptides (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: higher RF titers are a poor prognostic factor associated with more aggressive, erosive disease (Rheumatology Ch 30).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — structure of the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with 15-year RA history presents with bilateral finger deformities. Examination of her right hand shows hyperextension at the PIP joints and flexion at the DIP joints. Which deformity is this, and what is the primary mechanism responsible for its development in RA?","choices":{"A":"Swan-neck deformity, characterized by PIP hyperextension and DIP flexion, resulting from FDS rupture or volar plate laxity at the PIP combined with extensor tendon tightening caused by chronic synovitis at the MCP and PIP joints","B":"Boutonniere deformity, characterized by PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension, caused by central slip rupture of the extensor tendon with volar displacement of lateral bands at the PIP joint after chronic synovitis","C":"Mallet deformity, characterized by terminal DIP flexion and inability to actively extend the fingertip, caused by rupture or avulsion of the terminal extensor tendon at the DIP joint insertion in chronic RA","D":"Trigger finger deformity, characterized by locking of the finger in flexion due to nodular thickening of the flexor tendon as it catches on the A1 pulley during finger flexion and extension in chronic RA","E":"Ulnar deviation deformity, in which the fingers deviate toward the ulnar side at the MCP joints due to erosion of the radial-sided collateral ligaments and joint capsule after chronic synovitis at the MCP joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Swan-neck deformity = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion. Mechanism: RA-related synovitis causes FDS (flexor digitorum superficialis) rupture or volar plate laxity at the PIP, allowing extensor forces to dominate and hyperextend the PIP. Simultaneously, the DIP flexes. Boutonniere deformity = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension. Mechanism: central slip of the extensor tendon ruptures over the PIP joint, allowing the PIP to \"buttonhole\" through the two lateral bands. The lateral bands slip volarly and now flex the PIP while hyperextending the DIP. Mallet finger = DIP terminal extensor tendon rupture = DIP flexion only. Ulnar deviation = MCP joint collateral ligament erosion. These deformities are caused by tendon rupture and ligament laxity secondary to chronic synovitis rather than by direct joint destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan-neck = PIP hyper-EXT + DIP FLEX. Boutonniere = PIP FLEX + DIP hyper-EXT. Mnemonic: swan-neck looks like a swan (neck extended). Boutonniere = button in a hole (PIP flexed through lateral bands). Ulnar deviation = MCP involvement. COMLEX: all these deformities can be assessed on physical exam; knowing the mechanism helps predict which tendons/ligaments are affected and plan surgical vs. conservative intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with symmetric polyarthritis and 90-minute morning stiffness has a positive RF at titer 1:80. Her physician explains that a positive RF alone does not confirm RA. Which additional antibody test would most increase diagnostic specificity for RA in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Antinuclear antibody (ANA) at a titer exceeding 1:320, which has about 50% sensitivity in RA and poor specificity, being present in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, scleroderma, and 5% of healthy controls regardless of joint disease","B":"Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibody, which is found almost exclusively in SLE and would instead suggest reclassification as SLE-associated arthritis rather than primary seropositive RA","C":"ACPA (anti-citrullinated protein antibody / anti-CCP), which is more specific than RF for RA and, when positive in combination with RF, strongly supports RA diagnosis and predicts more erosive disease course","D":"Anti-Ro (SSA) antibody, which is part of the classification criteria for Sjogren syndrome and is frequent in SLE, and when present alongside RF would suggest a Sjogren-RA overlap syndrome rather than primary RA","E":"Anti-Jo-1 antibody, which is an antisynthetase antibody associated with inflammatory myopathy and interstitial lung disease, and is not a diagnostic marker for RA regardless of the presence of joint symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When RF is positive but the diagnosis remains uncertain, ACPA (anti-CCP) is the next test to order because of its high specificity for RA (FA p113; course SDL 44). A positive ACPA alongside RF strongly supports RA diagnosis and indicates seropositive disease, which tends to be more erosive, have more extra-articular manifestations, and be less likely to remit spontaneously. ANA is non-specific (positive in many autoimmune conditions and healthy individuals). Anti-Sm is SLE-specific. Anti-Ro is Sjogren/SLE-associated. Anti-Jo-1 is myositis-associated. None of these other antibodies increase diagnostic certainty for RA — they would instead suggest alternative or overlapping autoimmune diagnoses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF positive + ACPA positive = highest diagnostic certainty for RA. RF alone = non-specific (SASHES). ACPA alone = highly specific. Combined = greater confidence. Anti-CCP is the clinical ACPA assay. Other antibodies that do NOT confirm RA: ANA (non-specific), anti-dsDNA (SLE), anti-Sm (SLE), anti-Ro/La (Sjogren/SLE), anti-Jo-1 (myositis). COMLEX: order ACPA before starting DMARD therapy; ACPA status influences prognosis, treatment intensity, and patient counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response"},{"id":"sdl37_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman is evaluated for bilateral knee pain. RF is positive at 1:40. She has no morning stiffness greater than 15 minutes, no synovial thickening, and no periarticular erosions on radiography. She has no history of other autoimmune disease or chronic infection. Which of the following best explains the positive RF in this patient without underlying RA?","choices":{"A":"Healthy aging, as RF is detectable in approximately 5-10% of older adults without any underlying inflammatory joint disease, reflecting age-related polyclonal B cell activation and decreased immune tolerance","B":"Osteoarthritis, in which mechanical cartilage degradation generates citrullinated fibrin fragments that stimulate RF-producing B cells in synovial tissue, causing secondary RF elevation during osteoarthritic joint flares","C":"Fibromyalgia, in which central sensitization of pain processing pathways upregulates B cell activation markers and drives polyclonal IgM production including RF through a neuro-immune cross-talk mechanism","D":"Vitamin D deficiency, in which reduced 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels specifically impair Treg function in synovial tissue, allowing polyclonal IgM B cell activation and low-titer RF production","E":"Hypothyroidism, in which reduced thyroid hormone levels directly activate follicular B cells to produce IgM autoantibodies including RF through a thyroid hormone receptor-mediated B cell differentiation pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RF prevalence increases with age and can be detected in 5% to 10% of older adults without any underlying inflammatory disease (course SDL 44). This reflects age-related polyclonal B cell activation (declining tolerance mechanisms, accumulation of memory B cells generating low-affinity IgM antibodies), and the general principle that many autoantibodies increase in prevalence in the elderly. A low-titer RF (1:40) in an elderly patient without clinical signs of synovitis is most likely an age-related finding rather than indicative of RA. OA does not stimulate citrullination or RF production through the mechanisms described. RF is not associated with fibromyalgia, vitamin D deficiency, or hypothyroidism as primary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-titer RF in elderly = likely age-related (5-10% prevalence), not diagnostic of RA. Higher-titer RF in the right clinical context is more meaningful. Always combine RF with clinical assessment: morning stiffness >60 min, symmetric synovitis, elevated ESR/CRP, periarticular erosions on X-ray. COMLEX: differentiate RA from osteoarthritis in elderly patients using the ACPA test and clinical examination rather than RF alone; OA does not elevate CRP significantly and has no periarticular erosions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response"},{"id":"sdl37_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Citrullinated proteins are produced in the synovium in many kinds of joint inflammation, because peptidylarginine deiminases are activated during inflammation-induced cell death. Yet significant amounts of ACPA are made mainly by patients with RA and some with psoriatic arthritis. Which explanation is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Citrullinated proteins in other inflamed joints are cleared before they can reach the lymph nodes","B":"ACPA made in other arthritides is bound completely by rheumatoid factor in the serum","C":"Smoking-induced lung citrullination is required, and few patients with other arthritides smoke","D":"PAD enzymes in other joints add citrulline to residues that antibodies cannot recognize","E":"HLA-DR shared epitope molecules present citrullinated peptides to T cells and shape the response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Citrullination occurs in many types of synovial inflammation, yet typically only RA patients and some PsA patients respond with significant ACPA, likely because of the specific presentation of citrullinated peptides to T cells by shared epitope motifs on HLA-DR molecules; this is also why ACPA is more specific than RF. Clearance before reaching lymph nodes, complete binding by RF, and an unrecognizable form of citrulline are not described. Smoking is associated with ACPA positivity and promotes citrullination, but it is not a requirement that explains the restriction to RA. Sources: course SDL 44 reading (Rheumatology Ch 30, antibodies to citrullinated proteins, p11–12); course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, HLA-DR4 and ACPA-positive RA, p78).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-DR4 is associated with ACPA-positive RA (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: ACPA helps separate RA from erosive psoriatic arthritis and from hepatitis C–associated polyarthritis (Rheumatology Ch 30).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following accounts for the majority of cases of septic arthritis in native joints?","choices":{"A":"Direct inoculation of bacteria into the joint during an intra-articular corticosteroid injection performed under sterile technique","B":"Contiguous spread from adjacent cortical bone infection through the periosteal membrane into the overlying joint cavity","C":"Hematogenous seeding of the synovial space during a concurrent bacteremic episode originating from a distant infectious source","D":"Retrograde dissemination via lymphatic channels from a regional soft-tissue abscess ascending into the adjacent joint","E":"Transcapsular migration of organisms from a septic adjacent bursa through the joint capsule into the intra-articular space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hematogenous seeding accounts for approximately 80% of septic arthritis cases in native joints. The synovial membrane lacks a limiting basement membrane, allowing circulating organisms to directly enter the joint space during bacteremia. Direct inoculation (injections, trauma, arthroscopy) and contiguous spread from adjacent osteomyelitis account for the remaining 20%. Pre-existing joint disease increases susceptibility to hematogenous seeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic vignette — sudden monoarticular hot swollen joint in a patient with recent bacteremia or distant infection (UTI, skin infection, endocarditis). COMLEX: Joint aspiration is always the next step; fluid with >50,000 WBC/mm3 and >90% PMNs strongly suggests septic arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomic characteristic of the synovial membrane makes the joint space particularly susceptible to hematogenous bacterial seeding during bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Absence of a limiting basement membrane in the synovial lining allows circulating organisms to enter the joint space directly from blood vessels","B":"Dense capillary network with fenestrated endothelium that provides unfiltered vascular access to the intra-articular synovial compartment","C":"High concentration of glycosaminoglycans within synovial fluid that creates an osmotic gradient drawing bacteremic blood into the joint","D":"Presence of toll-like receptor-rich macrophages that actively phagocytose and transport bacteria across the synovial membrane barrier","E":"Lack of lymphatic drainage within the synovial space creating a stagnant fluid environment that promotes sustained bacterial growth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The synovial membrane lacks a limiting basement membrane, unlike most other tissues. This structural absence means that bacteria circulating in the bloodstream can pass directly from synovial capillaries into the joint space without the mechanical barrier that basement membranes normally provide. Organisms with propensity to adhere to synovial tissues, especially Staphylococcus aureus, exploit this vulnerability preferentially during bacteremia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This anatomic fact explains why joints with pre-existing synovitis (RA, gout, OA) are disproportionately seeded during bacteremia — their inflamed synovium has increased vascularity and permeability. COMLEX: RA patients on biologics face compounded risk: pre-existing synovitis plus immunosuppression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Involvement of which joint in septic arthritis should most specifically prompt consideration of injection drug use as a contributing route of infection?","choices":{"A":"The knee joint, because its large synovial surface area makes it the most frequent anatomic target during high-level bacteremic episodes","B":"The sternoclavicular joint, because axial joint involvement is characteristically associated with septic arthritis in injection drug users","C":"The hip joint, because its deep anatomic location creates difficulty with early clinical recognition and facilitates prolonged undetected bacteremia","D":"The wrist joint, because small joint involvement is a typical feature of disseminated gonococcal infection in sexually active individuals","E":"The ankle joint, because its proximity to the lower extremity injection sites used for intravenous drug administration increases direct inoculation risk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Axial joint involvement including the sternoclavicular, sacroiliac, and acromioclavicular joints is strongly associated with injection drug use (IDU). In the general population, the knee is the most commonly affected joint. IDU is associated with gram-negative organisms such as Pseudomonas and Serratia in addition to S. aureus. The sternoclavicular joint in particular raises strong clinical suspicion for IDU as the route of infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sternoclavicular joint + septic arthritis = injection drug use until proven otherwise. Also remember pubic symphysis and facet joints. COMLEX: PWID with septic arthritis of axial joints often have concurrent endocarditis; always perform blood cultures and auscultate the heart.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Staphylococcus aureus has become the most common cause of bacterial arthritis across all age groups in the United States primarily due to which change in the epidemiologic landscape?","choices":{"A":"Increased prevalence of S. aureus skin colonization secondary to widespread use of broad-spectrum antibiotics selecting for resistant strains","B":"Rising rates of diabetes mellitus and immunosuppressive therapy that specifically predispose patients to Staphylococcal joint infection","C":"Vaccination sharply reduced Haemophilus influenzae arthritis in children, leaving S. aureus as the predominant pathogen in all age groups","D":"Emergence of community-acquired MRSA strains with enhanced virulence factors enabling preferential colonization of synovial tissue","E":"Shift in prescribing patterns leading to S. aureus replacement of streptococcal species through ecological niche displacement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins Ch 26 (course reading for SDL 38): with the advent of effective vaccination, \"the incidence of H. influenzae arthritis has fallen\", and S. aureus is now the most common cause of bacterial arthritis in all age groups in the United States. The course orthopedics reading (Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Orthopedics Ch 12, SDL 16) still lists H. influenzae as a septic-joint organism at 6–24 months of age in children not immunized against Hib. In the New Zealand series cited in the course UpToDate reading (SDL 38), S. aureus caused 58% of 500 cases, 13% of which were MRSA. The other choices name changes (antibiotic selection of colonizing strains, rising diabetes and immunosuppression, CA-MRSA synovial tropism, streptococcal displacement) that the course sources do not give as the reason S. aureus became the leading cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the organism-by-population grid: Neonates = Group B Strep + E. coli; all ages = S. aureus; sexually active young adults = N. gonorrhoeae; sickle cell = Salmonella; IDU = Pseudomonas/gram-negative; complement MAC deficiency (C5-C9) = Neisseria. COMLEX: S. aureus remains #1 in adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the neonatal period, which combination of pathogens is most commonly responsible for septic arthritis, and through which primary mechanism do they typically access the joint space?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumoniae, entering the joint directly via hematogenous seeding during systemic bacteremia","B":"Group B Streptococcus and Escherichia coli, entering the joint via contiguous spread from underlying epiphyseal osteomyelitis in the neonate","C":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Haemophilus influenzae, transmitted perinatally and gaining joint access via bacteremia from mucosal surfaces","D":"Salmonella species and gram-negative bacilli, spreading to the joint from bowel translocation during episodes of neonatal feeding stress","E":"Coagulase-negative staphylococci and Klebsiella pneumoniae, introduced via direct inoculation from neonatal intravenous catheter sites"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In neonates, Group B Streptococcus and E. coli are the dominant organisms. Unlike in older patients where hematogenous spread predominates, neonatal septic arthritis frequently occurs via contiguous extension from epiphyseal osteomyelitis. In neonates, metaphyseal blood vessels penetrate the growth plate, allowing infection to spread from bone to joint. This anatomic relationship resolves with skeletal maturation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neonatal osteomyelitis often coexists with septic arthritis because metaphyseal vessels cross the growth plate. After the growth plate forms, this pathway closes. COMLEX: GBS is the most important organism to cover empirically in a neonate with a swollen joint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old male presents with recurrent disseminated gonococcal infection involving the knee joint. Testing reveals a terminal complement pathway deficiency. Which component deficiency most specifically explains his increased susceptibility to this organism?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of C1q, which impairs classical pathway initiation via antibody-antigen complexes deposited on the bacterial surface","B":"Deficiency of C3, which eliminates opsonization of the organism and prevents neutrophil phagocytosis within the inflamed synovium","C":"Deficiency of C4b-binding protein, which allows unregulated activation of the amplification loop at the C3b convertase stage","D":"Deficiency of terminal complement components C5 through C9, which prevents membrane attack complex lysis of Neisseria species","E":"Deficiency of mannan-binding lectin, which impairs the lectin pathway required for innate clearance of encapsulated cocci"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Terminal complement components C5-C9 form the membrane attack complex (MAC), which lyses Gram-negative bacteria by creating pores in their outer membranes. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and N. meningitidis are uniquely dependent on MAC-mediated lysis because they can resist opsonophagocytosis. Patients with MAC deficiency (C5-C9) are specifically susceptible to recurrent disseminated Neisseria infections. This is distinct from the general susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria caused by C3 deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MAC deficiency (C5-C9) = recurrent Neisseria infections (gonorrhoeae and meningitidis). C3 deficiency = susceptibility to ALL encapsulated bacteria. C1q deficiency = impaired apoptotic debris clearance, SLE predisposition. COMLEX: Any patient with recurrent disseminated Neisseria should be tested for complement deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which organism causes Lyme disease, and through which primary vector is it transmitted to humans in the United States?","choices":{"A":"Borrelia recurrentis, a spirochete transmitted by the human body louse Pediculus humanus corporis during episodes of close human contact","B":"Borrelia hermsii, a spirochete transmitted by soft-bodied Ornithodoros ticks during nighttime blood meals in endemic western forest regions","C":"Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete transmitted by Ixodes deer ticks, predominantly in New England, the mid-Atlantic states, and upper Midwest","D":"Borrelia mayonii, a spirochete transmitted by the American dog tick Dermacentor variabilis during prolonged attachment in grassland areas","E":"Rickettsia rickettsii, a gram-negative obligate intracellular pathogen transmitted by the wood tick Dermacentor andersoni in the Rocky Mountains"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lyme disease is caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete transmitted by Ixodes deer ticks (Ixodes scapularis in the Northeast/Midwest, Ixodes pacificus in the West). It is the most common arthropod-borne disease in the United States, endemic to New England, mid-Atlantic states, and upper Midwest. While Borrelia mayonii exists in the upper Midwest, B. burgdorferi sensu stricto is the dominant species in the US. Borrelia recurrentis and hermsii cause relapsing fever, not Lyme disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Borrelia burgdorferi + Ixodes tick = Lyme disease. The three phases: erythema migrans (early localized) → carditis/Bell palsy/meningitis (early disseminated) → arthritis (late). COMLEX: Lyme is the most common vector-borne illness in North America; know the geographic distribution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old hiker from Connecticut presents with a 3-month history of intermittent swelling of the left knee that recurs after aspiration. He denies fever. Which of the following best characterizes the articular manifestation of late Lyme disease?","choices":{"A":"Monoarthritis of the knee is the most common presentation, with large effusions that often recur and attacks lasting weeks to months","B":"Symmetric small joint polyarthritis of the hands and feet is the most distinctive articular pattern of late disseminated Borrelia infection","C":"Migratory polyarthritis affecting more than five joints simultaneously is the defining late-stage articular manifestation of untreated Lyme","D":"Axial arthritis with sacroiliitis and enthesitis is the predominant joint pattern in untreated late-stage Borrelia burgdorferi infection","E":"Cricoarytenoid joint involvement producing hoarseness and dysphagia is the classic articular manifestation of chronic Lyme disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Late Lyme arthritis characteristically presents as monoarthritis of the knee — the most commonly affected joint — with large, recurrent effusions. Attacks are intermittent and last weeks to months. Unlike RA, small joint involvement of the hands and feet is uncommon. The arthritis is not particularly painful relative to the degree of swelling. Without treatment, up to 80% of untreated patients develop Lyme arthritis, but with antibiotics, arthritis occurs in less than 10% of infected individuals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late Lyme arthritis = intermittent monoarthritis of the knee in a patient from an endemic region. Large effusion, not particularly painful. COMLEX: Lyme arthritis large joints in descending order: knee > shoulder > ankle > elbow > TMJ > wrist. Always fewer than 5 joints affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with confirmed Lyme arthritis completes two months of oral doxycycline followed by one month of intravenous ceftriaxone. Joint swelling persists, and synovial fluid PCR is negative for Borrelia DNA. Which mechanism best explains the persistence of arthritis in this antibiotic-refractory case?","choices":{"A":"Development of doxycycline-resistant Borrelia strains through upregulation of efflux pump mechanisms during prolonged antibiotic exposure","B":"Biofilm formation by residual Borrelia within the synovial pannus creating a protected sanctuary that prevents antibiotic penetration","C":"T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity reaction to persistent dead Borrelia antigens trapped within the synovial membrane matrix","D":"Immune complex deposition of circulating anti-Borrelia IgG antibodies within synovial microvessels triggering Type III hypersensitivity","E":"Cross-reactive autoimmunity between Borrelia outer surface protein A and a synovial self-antigen persisting after organism clearance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Antibiotic-refractory (postinfectious) Lyme arthritis occurs when joint inflammation persists despite complete antibiotic treatment and PCR-negative synovial fluid. The proposed mechanism is molecular mimicry: immune responses primed against Borrelia outer surface protein A (OspA) cross-react with an unknown synovial self-antigen, sustaining inflammation in the absence of viable organisms. This is essentially an autoimmune process triggered by infection. HLA-DR4 individuals may be at higher risk for this complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis = autoimmune mechanism (molecular mimicry with OspA), not antibiotic resistance. Negative PCR confirms no live organisms. COMLEX: This is a classic example of infection-triggered autoimmunity; the infection is the initial trigger but the ongoing pathology is autoimmune.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes and known chronic osteomyelitis of the distal femur presents with 3 days of acute right knee pain, swelling, and fever. Knee aspiration yields 80,000 WBC/mm3 with 95% neutrophils. X-ray shows metaphyseal lucency within the capsular reflection of the knee. Which mechanism best explains the development of his septic arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Hematogenous seeding of the knee joint during a bacteremic episode originating from a concurrent urinary tract infection with gram-negative rods","B":"Direct inoculation of bacteria into the knee joint during a recent intra-articular corticosteroid injection administered for osteoarthritis","C":"Contiguous extension of metaphyseal osteomyelitis directly into the joint space via the intracapsular metaphysis of the distal femur","D":"Retrograde spread via lymphatics from a pedal soft-tissue abscess ascending along the femoral lymphatic channels to the joint","E":"Transcapsular migration of organisms from an adjacent septic prepatellar bursitis through the anterior joint capsule into the joint space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The knee, hip, shoulder, and elbow are joints in which the metaphysis lies within the capsular reflection. When osteomyelitis involves an intracapsular metaphysis, pus can break through the bone cortex and discharge directly into the joint space — this is contiguous spread. The X-ray showing metaphyseal lucency within the capsular reflection is the key finding. This mechanism is distinct from hematogenous seeding, where bacteremia seeds the synovium directly rather than spreading from adjacent bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Joints with intracapsular metaphysis (knee, hip, shoulder, elbow) are at risk for septic arthritis secondary to adjacent osteomyelitis. COMLEX: Diabetic patients with chronic osteomyelitis can develop septic arthritis from contiguous spread; always image the bone when septic arthritis is found near a known osteomyelitis site.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with a 20-year history of rheumatoid arthritis managed with methotrexate and adalimumab presents with a single acutely swollen, hot, and painful right wrist disproportionate to her chronic bilateral joint disease. Synovial fluid reveals 95,000 WBC/mm3 with 98% neutrophils, and Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which combination of risk factors most directly explains why her joint was susceptible to hematogenous seeding?","choices":{"A":"Her postmenopausal status and vitamin D deficiency, which impair synoviocyte barrier function and reduce mucosal protection against bacteremic organisms","B":"Her use of methotrexate selectively reducing neutrophil production and eliminating the primary cellular defense against gram-positive organisms","C":"Her chronic synovitis and prior intra-articular injections reducing the bactericidal capacity of synovial fluid and promoting biofilm formation","D":"Her advanced age and female sex reducing complement-mediated clearance of encapsulated gram-positive organisms during bacteremia","E":"Her pre-existing synovitis increasing vascular permeability and her immunosuppressive therapy, each raising susceptibility to hematogenous seeding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"RA patients face dual risk for hematogenous septic arthritis: (1) pre-existing synovitis increases local blood flow and vascular permeability, making the joint a preferred site of bacterial localization during bacteremia; and (2) immunosuppressive medications including biologic DMARDs impair the immune response needed to clear bacteremia before joint seeding occurs. The gram-positive cocci in clusters indicate S. aureus, the most common organism in adult septic arthritis. The key is recognizing a disproportionately inflamed joint in a chronic arthritis patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In an RA patient, a single dramatically inflamed joint should prompt immediate aspiration for septic arthritis — never assume it is a flare without ruling out infection. COMLEX: Anti-TNF biologics substantially increase infection risk; superimposed septic arthritis on RA carries high morbidity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old man punched another man in the mouth 2 days ago. He now has a hot, swollen, painful third metacarpophalangeal joint with a small laceration over the knuckle. Arthrocentesis yields purulent fluid. Which of the following best describes the route of joint infection and the expected microbiology?","choices":{"A":"Direct inoculation through the bite wound, often polymicrobial with streptococci, Eikenella, or anaerobic bacteria","B":"Hematogenous seeding during transient bacteremia, typically a single organism such as Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Contiguous spread from metacarpal osteomyelitis, typically Salmonella reaching the bone through the metaphysis","D":"Hematogenous seeding from a mucosal source, typically Neisseria gonorrhoeae with migratory polyarthritis","E":"Contiguous spread from a dorsal hand abscess, typically Pseudomonas acquired from contaminated wound irrigation fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Septic arthritis arises by hematogenous seeding, by direct inoculation of bacteria into the joint, or, rarely, by contiguous spread from adjacent bone or soft tissue. Hematogenous seeding is the most common route; in one series 80% of cases were hematogenous and 20% followed direct inoculation. Mechanisms of direct inoculation include intra-articular injections, bite wounds, trauma, arthroscopy and other surgery. A laceration over the knuckle after a punch to the mouth is a human-bite injury that carries oral flora straight into the joint. Small-joint septic arthritis that follows a bite is more likely to be polymicrobial, with streptococci, Eikenella or anaerobic bacteria. Hematogenous seeding usually involves large joints, and there is no bacteremic source here. There is no osteomyelitis or abscess to spread from. Gonococcal arthritis is a hematogenous infection in sexually active patients and does not explain a single bitten joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Routes of septic arthritis: hematogenous (most common, large joints), direct inoculation (injections, bites, trauma, arthroscopy, surgery), contiguous spread (rare). Bite-related small-joint infection is often polymicrobial (streptococci, Eikenella, anaerobes). COMLEX: A knuckle laceration after a punch to the mouth is a human bite until proven otherwise; the joint needs drainage like any closed abscess.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with sickle cell disease presents with fever, right hip pain, and inability to bear weight. Blood and synovial fluid cultures are sent. Which organism should be specifically targeted in the empiric antibiotic regimen given his underlying hematologic condition?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus alone, which causes the majority of bacterial arthritis in all age groups regardless of underlying comorbid conditions","B":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, which is the dominant pathogen in asplenic patients due to functional asplenia impairing opsonophagocytosis","C":"Haemophilus influenzae, which causes a disproportionate share of joint infections in patients with impaired opsonization of encapsulated bacteria","D":"Salmonella species, which cause a disproportionately high proportion of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease","E":"Gram-negative enteric bacilli, which seed the joint preferentially via bacteremia from ischemic bowel during vasoocclusive crises"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sickle cell disease creates areas of bone and joint ischemia from vascular occlusion, which serve as a nidus for Salmonella infection. While S. aureus remains the overall most common cause of septic arthritis in sickle cell patients, Salmonella is uniquely overrepresented in this population compared to any other patient group. The impaired splenic function from autosplenectomy also contributes to susceptibility to encapsulated organisms. Coverage for both S. aureus and Salmonella is warranted empirically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sickle cell disease + osteomyelitis or septic arthritis = think Salmonella first. The ischemic bone provides a nidus for Salmonella bacteremia to localize. COMLEX: Sickle cell patients also have functional asplenia predisposing to encapsulated organisms, but Salmonella is the most tested organism for joint/bone infections specifically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with fever, migratory polyarthritis involving the right knee and left ankle, and painless pustular skin lesions on her distal extremities. Joint aspiration yields no organisms on Gram stain. Cervical cultures confirm the diagnosis. Which feature of this patient population primarily explains the increased susceptibility to dissemination of this pathogen compared with infected males?","choices":{"A":"Female hormonal cycling that directly suppresses complement activation during the luteal phase and permits gonococcal bacteremia","B":"Higher mucus viscosity in female genitourinary secretions that reduces local innate immune clearance of urogenital gonococci","C":"Greater membrane attack complex consumption during menstruation that transiently lowers complement-mediated gonococcal lysis","D":"Cervical anatomy that limits physical examination recognition of primary infection and delays presentation to medical care","E":"Asymptomatic primary infection is more common in women, allowing prolonged untreated infection and spread before diagnosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI) is more common in women than men largely because female urogenital gonorrhea is frequently asymptomatic. The absence of symptoms allows infection to persist longer without treatment, increasing the risk of bacteremia and dissemination to joints and skin. DGI typically presents with the triad of migratory polyarthritis, tenosynovitis, and petechial or pustular skin lesions. Gram stain of joint fluid is usually negative because the organism is present in low numbers; cultures of urogenital sites are more reliable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DGI triad = migratory polyarthritis + tenosynovitis + skin lesions (petechial/pustular). Gram stain of joint fluid is often negative. Culture the urethra/cervix, pharynx, and rectum. COMLEX: Women, MSM, and C5-C9 complement deficient patients are at highest risk for DGI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia receiving induction chemotherapy develops fever and an acutely swollen left knee. Synovial fluid cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Which patient characteristic best explains the shift toward gram-negative organism causation compared with immunocompetent adults?","choices":{"A":"Advanced age causing impaired neutrophil chemotaxis and reduced synovial phagocytic capacity specifically against extracellular gram-negative pathogens","B":"Underlying immunosuppression from induction chemotherapy, with neutropenia and impaired neutrophil clearance of gram-negative bacteria during bacteremia","C":"Chemotherapy-induced hyperuricemia depositing urate crystals in the knee joint that selectively support the growth of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in synovial fluid","D":"Corticosteroid premedication reducing TNF-alpha-mediated macrophage activation that normally clears gram-negative organisms from the bloodstream","E":"Leukopenia from chemotherapy selectively eliminating NK cells responsible for extracellular bactericidal activity against gram-negative rods"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Septic arthritis due to gram-negative bacilli such as Pseudomonas and Serratia generally occurs in older adults, patients with underlying immunosuppression, or injection drug users; in immunocompetent adults, S. aureus dominates. In this patient, induction chemotherapy for AML causes profound neutropenia and impaired neutrophil function, so gram-negative bacteremia is cleared poorly and seeds the joint. He is 42, so advanced age does not apply, and age does not selectively impair defense against gram-negative organisms. Hyperuricemia from tumor lysis can precipitate gout but does not promote bacterial growth in the joint. Corticosteroid premedication is not what produces his profound immunosuppression, and TNF-driven macrophage activation is not the main defense against gram-negative bacteremia. NK cells do not kill extracellular gram-negative rods; neutrophils are the key effectors, and their loss is the problem here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immunocompromised + septic arthritis = cover gram-negative organisms including Pseudomonas. This includes chemotherapy patients, HIV with low CD4, and transplant recipients. COMLEX: The empiric antibiotic choice changes based on the patient context; always tailor coverage to host factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman from coastal New England presents with 2 months of intermittent left knee swelling. She recalls a bull's-eye rash on her thigh 6 months ago that resolved spontaneously without treatment. Knee aspiration shows 25,000 WBC/mm3 with 70% neutrophils. Which diagnostic approach is most appropriate for confirming Lyme arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Synovial fluid PCR for Borrelia burgdorferi DNA alone, as this test provides superior sensitivity compared with serology at the late articular stage","B":"ELISA for IgM antibodies against Borrelia, as the IgM response predominates during late articular manifestations of Lyme disease","C":"Synovial biopsy with silver stain to detect spirochetes directly within the synovial tissue for definitive histologic confirmation","D":"Serum IgG antibody serology for Borrelia burgdorferi using a two-tier testing approach as the primary diagnostic method for late Lyme disease","E":"Empiric antibiotic treatment without confirmatory testing, as the characteristic history of erythema migrans is sufficient for clinical diagnosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All patients with Lyme arthritis will have positive serum IgG antibodies for B. burgdorferi. Two-tier serologic testing (ELISA followed by Western blot) is the standard diagnostic approach. By the time arthritis develops (late Lyme disease), IgG — not IgM — predominates. PCR of synovial fluid has approximately 70% sensitivity and should supplement, not replace, serology. A positive IgM alone in late disease likely represents a false positive. The history of untreated erythema migrans 6 months prior followed by knee arthritis is classic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late Lyme = IgG positive serology (two-tier). PCR of synovial fluid is ~70% sensitive and supplements serology. IgM alone in late disease = likely false positive. COMLEX: Erythema migrans is the most specific finding in early Lyme; in late disease, serology is required because EM is no longer present.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from New England presents with 8 weeks of left knee swelling. Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP are negative. Synovial biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltration resembling rheumatoid arthritis, but the pathologist notes a unique vascular finding. Which specific vascular change in the synovial biopsy most specifically supports Lyme arthritis over rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Obliterative endarteritis with concentric onion-skin thickening of small arteriolar walls in the synovium, characteristic of Lyme synovitis","B":"Endothelial cell activation with ICAM-1 upregulation and leukocyte rolling in synovial postcapillary venules consistent with any inflammatory arthritis","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of medium-sized synovial vessels consistent with necrotizing vasculitis driven by immune complex deposition in the vessel walls","D":"Angiogenesis with abundant new vessel formation characteristic of synovial pannus growth driven by VEGF from activated fibroblasts in RA","E":"Granulomatous inflammation around small vessels with multinucleated giant cells consistent with tuberculosis-related mycobacterial synovitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The histopathology of Lyme synovitis can closely mimic RA with synoviocyte hyperplasia, fibrin deposition, and mononuclear infiltrates. The distinguishing feature is obliterative endarteritis — onion-skin thickening of arteriolar walls — which is characteristic of Borrelia synovitis and not a feature of RA. The infiltrate in Lyme synovitis is predominantly CD4+ T cells. This morphologic feature reflects the vascular tropism of Borrelia and helps distinguish it from autoimmune joint disease when serologic data are ambiguous.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lyme synovitis histology = mimics RA, but the distinguishing feature is obliterative endarteritis (onion-skin arteriolar thickening). COMLEX: In severe Lyme arthritis histology may be indistinguishable from RA; clinical context and serology remain essential for diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman from Connecticut had an expanding red rash on her thigh 4 weeks ago that she did not have treated. She now has several new annular skin lesions far from the original site, weakness of the left side of her face, and lightheadedness. An ECG shows second-degree atrioventricular block. Which mechanism best explains these new findings?","choices":{"A":"A neurotoxin in Ixodes saliva, absorbed at the bite site, blocks acetylcholine release at cardiac and facial nerve terminals","B":"Borrelia spreads from the skin infection within days to weeks to other skin sites, cranial nerves, heart, and meninges","C":"T cells primed against outer surface protein A attack cross-reactive self antigens after the spirochetes have been cleared","D":"Immune complexes of anti-Borrelia IgG deposit in small vessels of the skin, nerves, and heart, causing a type III vasculitis","E":"Borrelia travels along peripheral nerves from the bite to the brainstem and then down the vagus nerve to reach the heart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lyme disease involves several organ systems in successive phases. The initial skin infection (erythema migrans) is followed within days or weeks by dissemination of Borrelia burgdorferi to other cutaneous sites, cranial nerves, heart and meninges. First Aid lists the early disseminated stage as secondary skin lesions, carditis with AV block, facial nerve (Bell) palsy and migratory myalgias or transient arthritis. Myocarditis occurs in about 5% of patients and is mainly a self-limited conduction-system disorder that may need a temporary pacemaker. Cross-reactive immunity to outer surface protein A is the proposed mechanism of antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis, a late problem that persists after the organism is gone; this patient is untreated and early. Lyme disease is an infection by the spirochete itself, not a toxin effect, and the sources describe dissemination of the organism, not immune-complex vasculitis or spread along nerves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lyme stages: early localized (erythema migrans), early disseminated (secondary skin lesions, AV block, facial palsy that is often bilateral, migratory arthralgias), late disseminated (chronic arthritis, encephalopathy, peripheral neuropathy). COMLEX: Doxycycline is first line; ceftriaxone is used when IV therapy is needed, as in disseminated disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic characteristic is most consistently associated with polymyalgia rheumatica and should raise clinical suspicion in a patient presenting with new-onset proximal muscle aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Female sex under 40 years with northern European ancestry and a personal or family history of autoimmune thyroid disease","B":"Age greater than 50 years, with peak incidence between 70 and 80; PMR is almost exclusively a disease of older adults","C":"Male sex over 60 years with a personal history of giant cell arteritis or recent temporal artery biopsy procedure","D":"Postmenopausal women with low bone mineral density and concurrent osteoporosis requiring bisphosphonate therapy","E":"Individuals of Asian or African American descent presenting with acute-onset bilateral shoulder girdle aching and stiffness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PMR is almost exclusively a disease of adults over 50, with the peak incidence between 70 and 80 years. The prevalence increases progressively with advancing age. It does not occur in younger adults. It is more common in females (2-3:1 ratio) and in people of northern European descent, but the age criterion over 50 is the most fundamental epidemiologic requirement for the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any patient under 50 presenting with proximal aching should not be labeled PMR — consider other diagnoses. COMLEX: PMR is the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults after RA; lifetime risk ~2.4% in females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The annual incidence of polymyalgia rheumatica is substantially higher in Scandinavian countries and among people of northern European descent. Which factor most likely explains this geographic and ethnic variation in disease incidence?","choices":{"A":"Genetic predisposition from specific HLA-DRB1 alleles that are more prevalent in northern European populations, raising disease susceptibility","B":"Environmental exposure to colder temperatures in Scandinavian climates that directly triggers periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis in aging joints","C":"Higher dietary intake of omega-6 fatty acids characteristic of traditional Scandinavian cuisine that promotes systemic inflammatory responses","D":"Greater physician awareness and systematic screening practices in Scandinavian healthcare systems leading to higher diagnostic capture rates","E":"Increased prevalence of giant cell arteritis in northern European climates that drives secondary polymyalgia rheumatica through shared pathophysiology"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The geographic clustering of PMR in Scandinavian countries and people of northern European descent reflects genetic susceptibility, particularly HLA-DRB1 alleles. The same HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region polymorphism associated with PMR and GCA is more prevalent in northern European populations. Incidence in Norway is 113/100,000/year while Italy reports only 13/100,000/year for those over 50 — a 9-fold difference that cannot be explained by climate or diet alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR has the highest incidence in Scandinavian and northern European descent populations; Olmsted County, MN (predominantly Scandinavian) incidence is 63.9/100,000/year. COMLEX: HLA-DRB1 is the primary genetic risk factor; this same gene locus also confers risk for GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polymyalgia rheumatica is described as the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults after rheumatoid arthritis. Which additional epidemiologic feature is most characteristic of its distribution in the population?","choices":{"A":"Equal prevalence between males and females, with a lifetime risk exceeding 10 percent in adults over the age of 70 years","B":"Strong male predominance in individuals over 80 years of age due to declining estrogen-mediated immune regulation effects","C":"Female predominance of about 2 to 3:1, with lifetime risk of approximately 2.43 percent in females and 1.66 percent in males","D":"Exclusive occurrence in adults over 70 years of age with no documented cases reported in the 50 to 69 year age range","E":"Similar prevalence across racial and ethnic groups with no significant differences in incidence rates documented between populations worldwide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PMR affects females 2-3 times more often than males, consistent with most autoimmune and inflammatory rheumatic diseases. The lifetime risk is 2.43% for females and 1.66% for males. It is less common — but not absent — in Asian, African American, and Latin American populations. PMR is notably second only to RA in prevalence among systemic inflammatory rheumatic diseases in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR demographics: female predominance (2-3:1), age >50, northern European descent. Lifetime risk ~2.4% females, ~1.7% males. COMLEX: PMR is relatively common — physicians should recognize the classic presentation of acute-onset bilateral proximal stiffness in an older woman.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately what percentage of patients diagnosed with giant cell arteritis also have polymyalgia rheumatica at some point during the course of their disease?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent, as the two conditions share the same vascular inflammatory pathway but manifest in entirely different tissue compartments","B":"Approximately 20 percent, representing the subset with large-vessel GCA who also develop periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis","C":"Approximately 30 percent, which reflects the proportion with positive temporal artery biopsy and concurrent proximal shoulder aching","D":"Approximately 50 percent, as PMR and GCA frequently co-occur and may represent different manifestations of a shared underlying disease spectrum","E":"Greater than 70 percent, because the cytokine environment in GCA invariably causes periarticular bursitis through IL-6-driven inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PMR occurs in approximately 50% of patients with GCA, making it one of the most common associated findings. Conversely, roughly 5-30% of patients with PMR will develop GCA at some point. PMR is 2-3 times more common than GCA overall. The two conditions share overlapping immunopathology — HLA-DRB1 alleles, Th17/Treg imbalance, elevated IL-6 — and may represent different points along a continuum of the same disease process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR + GCA association: ~50% of GCA patients have PMR. ~5-30% of PMR patients develop GCA. PMR is 2-3x more common than GCA. COMLEX: Any PMR patient who develops new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms needs urgent evaluation for GCA — blindness is a preventable complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"When comparing the immunopathology of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis, which finding is characteristic of GCA but is NOT typically seen in polymyalgia rheumatica?","choices":{"A":"Elevated serum IL-6 levels driving constitutional symptoms including fever, fatigue, anorexia, and elevated acute phase reactants","B":"Increased circulating Th17 cells and decreased regulatory T cells reflecting an imbalanced adaptive immune response","C":"Prominent interferon-gamma producing T cells that drive macrophage activation and granuloma formation within the arterial wall","D":"HLA-DRB1 polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft of the HLA-DR molecules","E":"Subclinical vascular inflammation detectable by PET scan showing fluorodeoxyglucose uptake in major arterial vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both PMR and GCA share elevated IL-6, Th17/Treg imbalance, and the HLA-DRB1 polymorphism. The key distinguishing feature is that GCA is characterized by prominent IFN-gamma-producing CD4+ T cells that activate macrophages, leading to granulomatous inflammation within the arterial wall. In PMR, IFN-gamma-producing T cells are NOT prominent — this is a critical distinction. Evidence of subclinical arterial inflammation with IL-1 and IL-6 is present in PMR temporal arteries, but the IFN-gamma-driven granulomas are specific to GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA has IFN-gamma-producing T cells driving granulomatous arteritis; PMR does NOT have prominent IFN-gamma T cells despite sharing IL-6 elevation and Th17 increases. COMLEX: This mechanistic difference explains why GCA causes arterial wall destruction and ischemic complications while PMR primarily affects periarticular structures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman is diagnosed with polymyalgia rheumatica based on clinical and laboratory criteria. She has no headache, jaw claudication, or visual changes. Based on published data, approximately what proportion of PMR patients without clinical features of GCA have subclinical large-vessel vasculitis detectable on imaging?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent, as subclinical GCA is extremely rare in the absence of constitutional symptoms pointing toward arterial involvement","B":"Approximately 10 percent, representing those with subclinical carotid artery involvement who later develop temporal artery symptoms","C":"Approximately 15 percent, limited to the subset with very high ESR values above 100 mm per hour at time of diagnosis","D":"Approximately 35 percent, as MRI routinely shows subclavian artery wall edema in the majority of newly diagnosed PMR patients","E":"Approximately 23 percent, based on meta-analysis demonstrating subclinical GCA in roughly one-quarter of patients with polymyalgia rheumatica"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A meta-analysis of 566 patients with PMR found subclinical GCA in approximately 23% of cases. On PET scan, increased FDG vascular uptake compatible with vasculitis was reported in approximately one-third of consecutive PMR patients, predominantly in the subclavian arteries (but less intense than in clinical GCA). Despite this subclinical vascular inflammation, patients with \"pure\" PMR rarely develop severe ischemic complications such as blindness unless clinical GCA features emerge. Close monitoring throughout the disease course is warranted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ~23% of PMR patients have subclinical GCA on imaging (meta-analysis). Clinical GCA features (jaw claudication, visual changes, headache) should always be assessed at each visit. COMLEX: PET scanning for subclinical GCA in PMR is a research tool; temporal artery biopsy is not required in PMR without GCA symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis share a specific HLA association that differs from that found in rheumatoid arthritis. Which HLA allele and structural region are associated with both PMR and GCA?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 at the peptide-binding groove, which is shared with ankylosing spondylitis and other seronegative spondyloarthropathies","B":"HLA-DR4 shared epitope at positions 70 to 74 of the DRbeta chain, identical to the epitope responsible for rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","C":"HLA-DQ6.02 at the antigen-presenting cleft, which is the same protective allele that reduces risk for type 1 diabetes mellitus","D":"HLA-DRB1 with a sequence polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft of the HLA-DR molecule","E":"HLA-DR2 at the complement receptor binding site, linking PMR pathogenesis to impaired clearance of apoptotic cell debris in aged tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PMR and GCA share an HLA-DRB1 sequence polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft. This is distinct from the RA shared epitope at positions 70-74 of the DRbeta chain — patients with GCA and PMR share the DRB1 polymorphism, but it is NOT found in patients with RA. This different HLA allele suggests that different antigens are being presented in PMR/GCA versus RA, despite all three conditions being HLA-DR4 related. HLA-B27 is associated with spondyloarthropathies, not PMR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR/GCA HLA = HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region polymorphism (antigen-binding cleft); RA HLA = shared epitope positions 70-74. Same HLA-DR4 broad allele group but DIFFERENT specific polymorphisms. COMLEX: The distinction between the PMR/GCA HLA association and the RA shared epitope is a classic exam discriminator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is primarily responsible for the constitutional manifestations of polymyalgia rheumatica, and what evidence supports this role?","choices":{"A":"IL-6, which is elevated in the peripheral blood of both PMR and GCA patients and is thought to drive fever, fatigue, and acute phase reactants","B":"IL-17, which is produced by the elevated Th17 cells in PMR and directly drives periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis at the shoulder and hip","C":"IFN-gamma, which is secreted by activated CD4+ T cells in PMR and mediates macrophage activation within the periarticular bursae and tendons","D":"TNF-alpha, which is the dominant proinflammatory cytokine in PMR and explains the dramatic response to tocilizumab in refractory disease","E":"IL-1 beta, which is exclusively elevated in the temporal arteries of PMR patients and primarily drives the systemic fever and acute phase response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary cytokine driving constitutional symptoms in both PMR and GCA. It stimulates hepatocytes to produce acute phase proteins (CRP, fibrinogen, SAA), causes fever through hypothalamic prostaglandin production, and promotes fatigue and anorexia. Elevated serum IL-6 is a consistent finding in active PMR and GCA. IL-6 receptor blockade with tocilizumab is effective in GCA and is being studied in PMR, providing therapeutic confirmation of its central role. Th17 cells are increased but IL-17 is not the primary mediator of constitutional symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 drives the constitutional manifestations of PMR and GCA (fever, fatigue, elevated CRP/ESR). Tocilizumab (IL-6 receptor blocker) is FDA-approved for GCA. COMLEX: The Th17/Treg imbalance is shared between PMR and GCA; Th17 cells are increased and Tregs are decreased in both conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which T cell subset imbalance is found in both polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis, suggesting a shared underlying immunopathological mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Increased CD8+ cytotoxic T cells with decreased CD4+ helper T cells, reflecting a shift toward direct cytolytic tissue destruction mechanisms","B":"Decreased Th1 cells with increased Th2 cells, resulting in an exaggerated humoral antibody response and elevated autoantibody production","C":"Increased Th17 cells and decreased regulatory T cells, which promotes uncontrolled pro-inflammatory signaling and impairs peripheral immune tolerance","D":"Increased memory B cells with decreased naive B cells, reflecting chronic antigen stimulation and class-switched autoantibody production","E":"Decreased natural killer T cells with increased double-negative T cells, disrupting lipid antigen presentation in inflamed vessel walls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both PMR and GCA share a characteristic T cell imbalance: Th17 cells are increased (promoting IL-17-driven inflammation) and regulatory T cells (Tregs) are decreased (impairing peripheral tolerance). This shared immunopathology supports the concept that PMR and GCA may represent a disease spectrum rather than completely distinct conditions. IL-6 itself suppresses Treg differentiation while promoting Th17 development, creating a self-amplifying inflammatory loop in both conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR and GCA = Th17 increased + Treg decreased (shared immune abnormality). The key distinguishing feature of GCA over PMR is the additional presence of IFN-gamma producing T cells driving granulomatous arteritis. COMLEX: This Th17/Treg imbalance is a recurrent theme in autoimmune diseases including RA and SLE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibromyalgia is best classified as which type of pain condition, and what is the primary neurological mechanism responsible for its characteristic symptomatology?","choices":{"A":"A nociceptive pain condition driven by ongoing peripheral tissue damage in muscles and tendons that produces referred pain to distant body regions","B":"A neuropathic pain condition caused by small fiber polyneuropathy affecting intraepidermal nerve fibers throughout affected body regions","C":"An inflammatory pain condition mediated by synovial macrophage activation and elevated TNF-alpha in the periarticular soft tissue compartments","D":"A psychosomatic pain condition without any demonstrable neurobiological substrate, explained entirely by psychiatric comorbidity and somatization","E":"A nociplastic pain condition characterized by central sensitization, with amplified CNS pain signaling in the absence of peripheral tissue damage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibromyalgia is the prototypic nociplastic pain disorder. Central sensitization — amplification of neural signaling within the CNS — results in pain hypersensitivity, temporal summation, and cross-modal sensory sensitization (light, sound, smell also become aversive). There is no peripheral tissue inflammation or structural damage. Muscle biopsies are histopathologically normal. Brain imaging studies using fMRI and PET demonstrate abnormal pain processing and altered neurotransmitter function (reduced serotonin and dopamine, elevated substance P in CSF). The disorder is neurophysiologic, not inflammatory or structural.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = nociplastic pain (central sensitization). NOT inflammatory — normal ESR, CRP, muscle biopsy. NOT neuropathic — no nerve conduction abnormalities. COMLEX: Central sensitization explains why fibromyalgia patients are sensitive to multiple stimuli including light, sound, and temperature, not just mechanical pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with fibromyalgia asks why her muscle biopsy was normal despite severe diffuse muscle pain. Which statement most accurately explains the histopathological findings expected in fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia causes subtle perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates in muscle tissue that are too focal for routine biopsy sampling to reliably detect","B":"Muscle in fibromyalgia is histologically normal because the disorder reflects central pain sensitization, not peripheral tissue inflammation","C":"Mild type II muscle fiber atrophy from deconditioning is the expected biopsy finding, reflecting disuse effects rather than inflammatory damage","D":"Scattered ragged red fibers visible on Gomori trichrome stain indicate underlying mitochondrial dysfunction as the primary pathogenic mechanism","E":"Patchy endomysial fibrosis is the characteristic biopsy finding that distinguishes fibromyalgia from inflammatory myopathies on histology"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Controlled studies have found no evidence of significant pathologic or biochemical muscle abnormalities in fibromyalgia. The muscle is histopathologically normal. The term \"fibrositis\" used historically implied soft-tissue inflammation, but this was disproven by controlled comparisons. The severe pain in fibromyalgia is a central sensitization phenomenon — the peripheral tissues are normal, but the CNS processing of pain signals is dysregulated. This is why anti-inflammatory treatments do not effectively address the underlying mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = normal muscle biopsy. This distinguishes it from inflammatory myopathies (elevated CK, abnormal biopsy) and from PMR (periarticular bursitis on imaging). COMLEX: Normal muscle histology combined with normal CK and normal inflammatory markers is the classic laboratory and pathology profile of fibromyalgia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following has been most strongly associated as a potential environmental trigger or aggravating factor for fibromyalgia symptom onset?","choices":{"A":"Acute sun exposure triggering mast cell degranulation in skin, releasing histamine and substance P into peripheral sensory nerve endings","B":"High-sodium dietary intake causing fluid retention that increases interstitial pressure on peripheral pain receptors in muscle fasciae","C":"Estrogen fluctuations during the menstrual cycle that alter serotonin receptor density in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord","D":"Physical or emotional trauma including PTSD, certain viral infections, and Lyme disease can trigger or worsen fibromyalgia symptoms","E":"Occupational repetitive strain creating microtrauma at entheses that overwhelms local tissue repair mechanisms in susceptible individuals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There is no single causative event in fibromyalgia. However, physical and emotional trauma (including PTSD), infections (particularly certain viruses and Lyme disease), and psychosocial stressors are recognized as potential triggers or aggravating factors. \"Long COVID\" and post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome both produce symptoms strikingly similar to fibromyalgia, suggesting that post-infectious central sensitization may be a shared mechanism. Sleep disturbances and pre-existing mood disorders also independently increase fibromyalgia risk. Menstrual estrogen swings are not among the triggers the reading lists, which are infections (viral illness, Lyme disease), physical or emotional trauma, and sleep and mood disturbance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia triggers include trauma (physical/emotional), PTSD, viral infections, and Lyme disease. \"Long COVID\" symptoms overlap heavily with fibromyalgia. COMLEX: There is no single cause; fibromyalgia is a multifactorial condition with genetic predisposition and environmental triggers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with diffuse musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, disturbed sleep, and cognitive difficulties for 18 months. Physical examination reveals widespread soft tissue tenderness. Which laboratory finding is most consistent with a diagnosis of fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Mildly elevated ESR of 28 mm per hour with a normal CRP, suggesting a low-grade inflammatory process contributing to her diffuse pain","B":"Normal CBC, normal ESR and CRP, and negative autoantibodies, consistent with a nociplastic pain condition without peripheral inflammation","C":"Slightly elevated ANA at a titer of 1 to 40 with a homogeneous pattern, which is commonly found in fibromyalgia patients without lupus","D":"Elevated CK of 350 units per liter suggesting underlying mild inflammatory myopathy as the primary cause of her diffuse muscle pain","E":"Decreased serum serotonin level measurable on routine chemistry panels providing objective biochemical confirmation of central sensitization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hallmark laboratory feature of fibromyalgia is that ALL routine laboratory tests are normal: CBC, ESR, CRP, CMP, CK, ANA, RF, and anti-CCP. This completely normal laboratory profile is both characteristic of fibromyalgia and diagnostically important because it helps exclude inflammatory conditions. There is no commercially available laboratory test that confirms fibromyalgia. Serum serotonin is not measured on routine panels and its utility in diagnosis is unproven. A low ANA titer can be found in healthy older adults and does not indicate fibromyalgia or SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = normal labs across the board (ESR, CRP, CK, ANA, CBC all normal). The diagnosis is entirely clinical. COMLEX: Normal labs in a patient with diffuse pain, fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive symptoms points strongly to fibromyalgia over inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 2016 American College of Rheumatology diagnostic criteria for fibromyalgia incorporate which two scoring components to quantify symptom burden and establish the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Tender point count above the standardized threshold on palpation and a pain severity score based on visual analog scale ratings over the past week","B":"Duration of symptoms exceeding 6 months and a positive response to low-dose naltrexone as a confirmatory therapeutic diagnostic trial","C":"Sleep study confirmation of non-restorative sleep pattern and a documented anxiety or depression comorbidity score above threshold","D":"Exclusion of inflammatory arthritis by negative RF and ANA combined with a positive fibromyalgia symptom checklist screening score","E":"Widespread Pain Index score and Symptom Severity Scale score, which together must meet threshold criteria to diagnose fibromyalgia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 2010/2016 ACR criteria replaced the older 1990 tender point count criteria. The updated criteria use: (1) Widespread Pain Index (WPI), which counts painful body areas, and (2) Symptom Severity Scale (SSS), which scores fatigue, waking unrefreshed, and cognitive symptoms. The older 1990 criteria required a minimum tender point count — this is no longer required for diagnosis. The updated criteria allow diagnosis without physical examination tender point testing and better capture the full symptom complex of fibromyalgia including cognitive and sleep symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 2016 ACR fibromyalgia criteria = WPI (Widespread Pain Index) + SSS (Symptom Severity Scale). The old 1990 tender point criteria are no longer required. COMLEX: Fibromyalgia diagnosis is clinical — no laboratory confirmation required or available.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has had aching and stiffness of the shoulder and hip girdles for the past 2 years, along with widespread pain elsewhere, unrefreshing sleep, and fatigue. ESR is 8 mm per hour, CRP is within normal limits, TSH is normal, and rheumatoid factor is negative. Which diagnosis is best supported by the completely normal inflammatory markers in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"Polymyalgia rheumatica with atypically normal labs, which occurs in approximately 5 to 20 percent of confirmed PMR patients at diagnosis","B":"Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis, because late-onset RA presenting with proximal stiffness can occasionally have entirely normal acute phase reactants","C":"Giant cell arteritis restricted to the aorta, where temporal artery inflammation may be absent and peripheral inflammatory markers can be normal","D":"Fibromyalgia, because a nociplastic pain condition produces widespread aching and stiffness without elevating ESR, CRP, or hematocrit","E":"Hypothyroid myopathy, as thyroid dysfunction commonly produces proximal aching and stiffness with normal inflammatory markers and negative RF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A completely normal ESR and CRP effectively excludes active PMR: in two studies, 99% of patients with PMR at diagnosis had an elevated CRP, and PMR with normal markers occurred in only 1.2% of clinically diagnosed cases in one study. The 5 to 20 percent figure applies to PMR with an ESR below 40 mm per hour, not to completely normal labs. Years of widespread pain with unrefreshing sleep, fatigue, and normal inflammatory markers is the picture of fibromyalgia, a chronic nociplastic pain condition. Hypothyroid myopathy can mimic this, but her TSH is normal. Seronegative RA is a synovitis of the hands, wrists, and feet that usually raises ESR and CRP, and GCA, even when restricted to the aorta, is an inflammatory vasculitis that does not explain years of widespread pain with normal markers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal CRP effectively excludes PMR; abnormal CRP is nearly universal in active PMR. Normal labs with proximal aching = think fibromyalgia first, then hypothyroid. COMLEX: This is the classic PMR vs fibromyalgia discriminator on exams — the ESR and CRP result is the key differentiating laboratory finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which single laboratory finding most reliably distinguishes polymyalgia rheumatica from fibromyalgia in a patient presenting with bilateral proximal aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Positive anti-CCP antibodies, which are elevated in PMR but absent in fibromyalgia due to a lack of citrullinated protein immune reactivity","B":"Elevated serum substance P, which is measurable as a marker of central sensitization in fibromyalgia but is consistently normal in PMR","C":"Elevated CRP or ESR, which is nearly universally present in active PMR but is consistently normal in fibromyalgia across all disease stages","D":"Low serum IL-6 in fibromyalgia, because nociplastic pain dysregulates cytokine production downward rather than causing elevation","E":"Elevated serum creatine kinase in PMR, which reflects periarticular muscle injury from adjacent bursitis and tenosynovitis activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The acute phase reactants — ESR and CRP — are the single most important distinguishing laboratory tests. PMR produces markedly elevated ESR and CRP (99% of PMR patients have elevated CRP at diagnosis), while fibromyalgia produces completely normal ESR and CRP. Anti-CCP and RF are negative in BOTH conditions (PMR is seronegative). CK is normal in both. Substance P is elevated in fibromyalgia but is not routinely measured. IL-6 is actually elevated in PMR, not low. The ESR/CRP is the practical, clinically available discriminating test.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = elevated ESR/CRP. Fibromyalgia = normal ESR/CRP. Both = negative RF and ANA. COMLEX: This is the most commonly tested distinction between the two conditions; always check acute phase reactants in proximal aching patients over 50.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patient 1, a 68-year-old woman, reports bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness that developed almost overnight 3 weeks ago and prevents her from dressing herself. Patient 2, a 55-year-old woman, reports diffuse aching and stiffness that has been present for 4 years and gradually worsened following a stressful divorce. Which feature of the symptom timeline most reliably distinguishes PMR from fibromyalgia in these two presentations?","choices":{"A":"PMR typically presents with acute or subacute onset of recent, discrete symptoms, whereas fibromyalgia symptoms are longstanding with insidious onset","B":"The severity of morning stiffness distinguishes the conditions, with fibromyalgia consistently causing more severe and prolonged morning stiffness than PMR","C":"Geographic distribution of stiffness distinguishes the conditions, with fibromyalgia restricted to the shoulder girdle while PMR causes truly widespread diffuse pain","D":"Response to acetaminophen within 24 hours distinguishes PMR from fibromyalgia, as PMR responds to analgesics while fibromyalgia is analgesic-resistant","E":"Age of onset reliably distinguishes the conditions since fibromyalgia never occurs in patients over 60 and PMR never presents before the age of 70"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The onset pattern is a key clinical discriminator. PMR is characterized by recent, acute to subacute onset — often startlingly abrupt, seeming to occur almost overnight. A story of longstanding aching does not suggest PMR. Fibromyalgia, by contrast, typically involves symptoms of longstanding duration that develop gradually, often in association with psychosocial stressors or trigger events. Morning stiffness in PMR is invariable and severe (lasting into late morning), while in fibromyalgia it is more variable. Age of onset is helpful (PMR requires >50) but fibromyalgia can also occur in patients over 50.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR onset = acute/subacute (sometimes overnight). Fibromyalgia onset = chronic, gradual, longstanding. COMLEX: The history \"started 3 weeks ago suddenly\" favors PMR; \"has been going on for years\" favors fibromyalgia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which characteristic of the response to treatment most sharply distinguishes polymyalgia rheumatica from fibromyalgia, and is itself used as a diagnostic criterion in some PMR classification systems?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia responds dramatically to low-dose prednisone at 15 to 25 mg daily, while PMR requires high-dose steroid therapy at 40 to 60 mg daily","B":"PMR responds completely to hydroxychloroquine within 4 to 6 weeks, while fibromyalgia is entirely unresponsive to any antimalarial therapy","C":"Both conditions respond similarly to duloxetine and pregabalin, but only fibromyalgia responds additionally to serotonin reuptake inhibitors","D":"Fibromyalgia responds to intra-articular corticosteroid shoulder injections, while PMR requires systemic anti-inflammatory therapy for control","E":"PMR responds dramatically to low-dose oral prednisone at 15 to 25 mg daily within days to a week, while fibromyalgia does not respond to corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A dramatic, rapid response to low-dose corticosteroids — typically prednisone 15-25 mg/day (course SDL 39) with rapid, near-complete symptom relief — is so characteristic of PMR that it is incorporated as a supportive criterion in some classification systems. Fibromyalgia does not respond to corticosteroids because the underlying mechanism is central sensitization rather than peripheral inflammation. Fibromyalgia responds better to SNRIs (duloxetine), alpha-2-delta ligands (pregabalin, gabapentin), TCAs, aerobic exercise, and CBT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR + rapid dramatic response to prednisone 15-25 mg/day = confirmatory. Fibromyalgia does NOT respond to prednisone. COMLEX: OMM, aerobic exercise, and SNRIs are more appropriate for fibromyalgia than corticosteroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman of Swedish descent presents with a 3-week history of bilateral shoulder aching and stiffness worst in the morning that prevents her from dressing herself. She denies headache, jaw pain, or visual symptoms. ESR is 87 mm/hour and CRP is 42 mg/L. She has no prior joint disease. Which epidemiologic feature of this presentation is most characteristic of the expected population affected by polymyalgia rheumatica?","choices":{"A":"Age over 50 with northern European ancestry, consistent with the demographic group in which PMR incidence is highest globally","B":"ESR greater than 80 mm per hour combined with northern European descent, which together constitute the specific combination required for PMR diagnosis","C":"Acute onset of bilateral shoulder pain as the presenting manifestation, which is pathognomonic for PMR and cannot occur in fibromyalgia","D":"Female sex over 70 with northern European ancestry, representing the peak reproductive-to-aging transition window for autoimmune onset","E":"Absence of prior joint disease with markedly elevated acute phase reactants, representing the classic pre-PMR laboratory baseline state"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The most characteristic epidemiologic features of this presentation are age over 50 (she is 76) and northern European (Swedish) ancestry, the two most strongly associated demographic risk factors for PMR. Peak incidence is between 70 and 80 years. PMR incidence is highest in Scandinavian countries and in people of Scandinavian descent living elsewhere (e.g., Olmsted County, MN). The elevated ESR/CRP supports the diagnosis but is not specific to any demographic. The acute onset and bilateral proximal pattern are clinical rather than epidemiologic features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = age >50 + northern European descent + acute proximal girdle stiffness + elevated ESR/CRP. COMLEX: The demographic triad (older adult, female, northern European) combined with acute proximal stiffness and elevated acute phase reactants is the classic PMR presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Epidemiologic studies of polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis have followed how their incidence changes over time in the same regions. Which pattern has been reported, and what does it suggest?","choices":{"A":"A steady decline over several decades, suggesting that better hygiene removed the cause","B":"A sharp peak in early adulthood, suggesting a childhood viral exposure with long latency","C":"Identical incidence in every ethnic group, suggesting a purely age-related wear process","D":"Clustering within families in most cases, suggesting a single dominant gene as the cause","E":"A cyclical pattern with seasonal variation, suggesting environmental infectious triggers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Some studies of PMR and GCA have found a cyclical pattern of incidence with seasonal variation, implying possible environmental infectious triggers, although studies of specific viruses have been inconclusive. PMR is almost exclusively a disease of adults over 50, with peak incidence between 70 and 80, so an early-adult peak does not fit. Incidence is highest in Scandinavia and in people of northern European descent and lower in Asian, African American, and Latin American populations. Familial aggregation is rare. A steady multi-decade decline is not described. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, epidemiology and pathogenesis, p2–3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = adults over 50, peak 70 to 80, female predominance 2 to 3:1, highest in northern Europeans (UpToDate, SDL 39). COMLEX: PMR incidence in adults 50 and older is about 113 per 100,000 per year in Norway versus 13 per 100,000 in Italy (UpToDate, SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — populations most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A population study at Olmsted County, Minnesota documents an unusually high PMR incidence of 63.9 per 100,000 per year compared with southern European populations. The researchers attribute this to the ethnic composition of the Olmsted County population. Which ancestry predominates in this population and confers the elevated disease risk?","choices":{"A":"Celtic Irish ancestry, due to disproportionate carriage of HLA-DRB1 alleles associated with inflammatory rheumatic conditions in the Irish population","B":"German ancestry, which is overrepresented in the upper Midwest of the United States and is associated with the globally highest rates of PMR","C":"Finnish ancestry, because the unique Finnish population bottleneck enriched for rare HLA haplotypes specifically predisposing to PMR onset","D":"Scandinavian ancestry, since PMR incidence is highest in Scandinavia and in northern European descendants, who predominate in Olmsted County","E":"British ancestry, because England has the highest documented population-based PMR incidence per 100,000 in all published epidemiologic studies"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Olmsted County, Minnesota is frequently cited in PMR epidemiology because its population is predominantly of Scandinavian descent. Norway has the highest reported PMR incidence in Europe at 113 per 100,000 per year for the over-50 population, while Italy reports only 13 per 100,000. Olmsted County (63.9 per 100,000) falls between these values, consistent with its partial Scandinavian genetic background. This real-world data underscores the strong genetic component of PMR risk linked to HLA-DRB1 alleles prevalent in Scandinavian populations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olmsted County = high PMR incidence due to Scandinavian-descent population. Norway (113/100,000) vs Italy (13/100,000) illustrates the 9-fold geographic variation. COMLEX: Geographic clustering confirms genetic rather than purely environmental causation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman is diagnosed with PMR. She has no headache, jaw claudication, or visual changes. A research PET scan shows increased FDG uptake at the subclavian arteries bilaterally. Which statement best characterizes the significance of this finding in the context of PMR management?","choices":{"A":"The PET scan confirms active temporal artery involvement requiring immediate escalation to high-dose prednisone to prevent irreversible vision loss","B":"Positive PET scan indicates imminent conversion to clinical GCA within 3 months, warranting prophylactic temporal artery biopsy immediately","C":"FDG uptake in the subclavian arteries is specific for Takayasu arteritis and changes the diagnosis from PMR to Takayasu disease","D":"The finding rules out PMR because subclavian artery FDG uptake is exclusively observed in giant cell arteritis with large-vessel predominance","E":"Subclinical vascular inflammation appears on PET in about one-third of PMR patients, and its long-term prognostic significance is still unclear"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Approximately one-third of consecutive PMR patients show increased FDG vascular uptake on PET scan, predominantly in the subclavian arteries. The intensity is less than that observed in clinical GCA. The clinical significance of this subclinical vascular inflammation is still being defined — patients with \"pure\" PMR rarely develop severe ischemic complications (blindness, stroke) despite this finding. However, close monitoring throughout the disease course is recommended since clinical GCA features can develop during follow-up. This finding does not change the initial treatment strategy for PMR without clinical GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ~1/3 of PMR patients have subclinical vascular inflammation on PET scan. This does NOT require high-dose steroids unless clinical GCA features are present. COMLEX: PET for subclinical GCA in PMR is a research tool; the clinical management threshold is the presence of GCA symptoms (jaw claudication, headache, visual changes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A health system is planning rheumatology services for adults over 50 and asks how often polymyalgia rheumatica occurs compared with giant cell arteritis. Which statement best describes their relative frequency?","choices":{"A":"GCA is more common than PMR, because most PMR resolves before anyone diagnoses it","B":"PMR is about two to three times more common than GCA within the same population","C":"The two are equally common, since every patient with PMR has subclinical GCA","D":"PMR is about ten times rarer than GCA, because it is confined to northern Europe","E":"GCA is common but PMR is rare, affecting fewer than 1 in 100,000 older adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PMR is two to three times more common than GCA; it occurs in about half of patients with GCA, while about 5 to 30 percent of patients with PMR develop GCA at some point. Subclinical GCA is found in a minority of PMR patients, about 23 percent in a meta-analysis and 17 percent in a fast-track clinic study, so not every PMR patient has it. PMR is relatively common, with a lifetime risk second only to RA among systemic rheumatic diseases, and it occurs worldwide though it is less common outside northern European populations. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, epidemiology and association with GCA, p2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR is 2 to 3 times more common than GCA; about half of GCA patients have PMR (UpToDate, SDL 39). COMLEX: GCA is the most common vasculitis of older adults in the United States and Europe (Robbins Ch 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — populations most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman with PMR diagnosed 8 months ago is maintained on prednisone 8 mg daily with good symptom control. She now presents with new-onset temporal headaches and jaw pain with chewing. Which statement most accurately describes the documented risk of GCA development in PMR patients during their disease course?","choices":{"A":"Clinical GCA develops in fewer than 1 percent of PMR patients, and new temporal headache on prednisone is most likely tension-type or steroid-related","B":"Clinical GCA develops in approximately 10 percent of PMR patients within the first year, primarily those with very high initial ESR above 100 mm per hour","C":"Clinical GCA develops in approximately 5 to 30 percent of PMR patients during the disease course, warranting prompt temporal artery biopsy","D":"Clinical GCA develops in all PMR patients who are undertreated with prednisone doses below 20 mg daily for longer than 6 months duration","E":"Clinical GCA develops preferentially in HLA-DR4 positive PMR patients with the shared epitope at positions 70 to 74 of the DRbeta chain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The development of clinical GCA in PMR patients ranges from 5-30% across reported series. New-onset jaw claudication (jaw pain with chewing) and temporal headache in a PMR patient are classic warning signs of GCA and require urgent evaluation — temporal artery biopsy, ophthalmology referral, and possible escalation to high-dose prednisone to prevent irreversible visual loss. Late-onset GCA can occur even in patients already on low-dose prednisone for PMR if the dose is insufficient to suppress vascular inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR patient develops jaw claudication + headache = URGENT evaluation for GCA. Jaw claudication (jaw pain with chewing/talking that resolves with rest) is the most specific GCA symptom. COMLEX: Temporal artery biopsy should be performed promptly; high-dose steroids should not be delayed while awaiting biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with polymyalgia rheumatica has a glenohumeral effusion, and synovial tissue from the joint is examined. Which inflammatory infiltrate has been described in synovial membranes from joints involved by PMR?","choices":{"A":"Sheets of neutrophils with intracellular needle-shaped crystals","B":"Eosinophil-rich infiltrates with surrounding fibrinoid necrosis","C":"Plasma cells in germinal centers secreting anti-citrullinated antibodies","D":"Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells along the elastic lamina","E":"A synovitis composed of macrophages and CD4+ helper T lymphocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A synovitis characterized by macrophages and CD4+ T lymphocytes has been described in synovial membranes from involved joints in PMR, although glenohumeral and hip effusions are less frequent than bursitis and tenosynovitis. Neutrophils with needle-shaped crystals are the aspirate of acute gout. Germinal centers with plasma cells making ACPA are a feature of rheumatoid synovium, and ACPA is usually negative in PMR. Granulomas with giant cells along the elastic lamina are the arterial lesion of GCA. Eosinophil-rich infiltrates with fibrinoid necrosis are not described in PMR. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, pathogenesis and pathophysiology, p3–4; autoantibodies, p7).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR and GCA share increased Th17 cells, decreased Tregs, and raised IL-6, but IFN-gamma producing T cells are not prominent in PMR (UpToDate, SDL 39). COMLEX: the muscle in PMR is histologically normal; proximal symptoms come from bursae, tendons, and joints (UpToDate, SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — T-cell response in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of immunologic findings in peripheral blood would be most consistent with a diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica in a patient presenting with proximal aching and stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Elevated IgG anti-CCP antibodies, increased CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, and decreased NK cells consistent with an autoantibody-driven arthritis","B":"Positive ANA with anti-dsDNA, decreased complement C3 and C4, and peripheral B cell lymphopenia consistent with immune complex consumption","C":"Increased circulating plasmablasts, elevated IgG4 levels, and IL-10 overproduction consistent with IgG4-related inflammatory disease","D":"Increased circulating Th17 cells, decreased regulatory T cells, and elevated serum IL-6 without prominent IFN-gamma predominance","E":"Elevated serum IL-4 and IL-13 with increased eosinophil count and total IgE levels reflecting a Th2-skewed inflammatory response pattern"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The immunologic profile of PMR includes: elevated IL-6 (driving constitutional symptoms and acute phase response), increased Th17 cells (promoting pro-inflammatory IL-17 signaling), and decreased regulatory T cells (impairing peripheral tolerance). Importantly, IFN-gamma-producing T cells are NOT prominent in PMR (unlike GCA). Anti-CCP and ANA are negative. There is no complement consumption. IgG4-related disease has a different cytokine profile (IL-10, IL-4, IL-13) and involves IgG4 deposition in tissues. The absence of IFN-gamma prominence helps distinguish PMR immunopathology from GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR immune profile = elevated IL-6 + Th17 up + Treg down, no IFN-gamma. GCA adds IFN-gamma driving granulomas. Anti-CCP and ANA are negative in both. COMLEX: The shared Th17/Treg imbalance between PMR and GCA provides a mechanistic rationale for why they frequently co-occur.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen in polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with PMR reports severe bilateral hip and thigh aching. His rheumatologist explains that despite the name, the muscles themselves are not the primary target of inflammation. Which pathologic finding would most accurately characterize the tissue responsible for the proximal pain in PMR?","choices":{"A":"Endomysial inflammation with CD4+ T cell infiltrates surrounding necrotic muscle fibers in the proximal hip musculature on muscle biopsy","B":"Periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis of the subdeltoid, subacromial, iliopsoas, and trochanteric bursae, as shown by ultrasound and MRI","C":"Synovial proliferation with pannus formation eroding the glenohumeral joint cartilage in a pattern identical to rheumatoid arthritis","D":"Myofascial trigger points with taut bands and local twitch response in the proximal muscle groups consistent with fibromyalgia overlap syndrome","E":"Necrotizing granulomas within arteriolar walls of proximal muscles, explaining an ischemic mechanism of pain in untreated PMR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Despite the name \"polymyalgia\" (many muscle pains), the muscles in PMR are histopathologically normal. Modern imaging (ultrasound, MRI, PET-CT) has demonstrated that the proximal pain arises from periarticular structures — specifically bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis and biceps tenosynovitis at the shoulders, and iliopsoas bursitis, trochanteric bursitis, and hamstring tendinitis at the hips. Bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis is an imaging hallmark of PMR. The proximal aching represents referred pain from the inflamed bursae and tendons, not from the muscles themselves. Pannus eroding cartilage is the lesion of rheumatoid arthritis, not of PMR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = periarticular bursitis/tenosynovitis (NOT muscle inflammation). Hallmark imaging finding = bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis on ultrasound. COMLEX: This explains why PMR pain is described as \"aching around the shoulders and hips\" rather than true muscle weakness — there is no myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen in polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In a classic 1975 experiment, healthy volunteers had their stage 4 sleep repeatedly interrupted by noise. What did they develop, and what does this finding support about fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Widespread pain and fatigue, supporting sleep disturbance as a risk factor for the disorder","B":"Joint swelling with a raised CRP, supporting fibromyalgia as a form of inflammatory arthritis","C":"Proximal muscle weakness with a raised CK, supporting fibromyalgia as a primary myopathy","D":"Focal neuropathic pain in one limb, supporting fibromyalgia as a result of peripheral nerve injury","E":"No new symptoms, supporting sleep problems as a consequence rather than a risk factor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Stage 4 sleep interruption in healthy participants produced widespread pain and fatigue, the first evidence that sleep disturbance is a risk factor for fibromyalgia; later prospective studies found a dose-dependent rise in risk with sleep problems, and nonrestorative sleep was the strongest predictor of widespread pain in older adults. The relationship is bidirectional, so sleep problems are both a risk factor and a consequence. Fibromyalgia has no peripheral musculoskeletal inflammation, no significant muscle abnormality, and is a widespread, not focal, nociplastic pain disorder. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Fibromyalgia: Pathogenesis, sleep and mood disturbances, p3; types of pain, p1–2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibromyalgia is the prototypic nociplastic pain disorder of central sensitization (UpToDate, SDL 39). COMLEX: evaluate patients with possible fibromyalgia for comorbid sleep and mood disorders (UpToDate, SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 5 — normal laboratory findings in fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with fibromyalgia reports that her mother and sister also have the diagnosis. She asks about the genetic component. Which statement most accurately reflects current understanding of the genetic contribution to fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"A single high-penetrance gene mutation in the sodium channel gene SCN9A is responsible for all familial fibromyalgia in affected family members","B":"Multiple polymorphisms in COMT, serotonin transporter, and opioid receptor genes add risk, with a sibling recurrence risk ratio of about 13.6","C":"Fibromyalgia is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with 90 percent penetrance, explaining the high familial clustering observed","D":"A single HLA allele confers 80 percent of genetic risk, identical to the Class II MHC mechanism in other chronic pain autoimmune conditions","E":"Fibromyalgia has no genetic component, and familial clustering reflects purely shared environmental stressors and learned pain behaviors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibromyalgia has a significant genetic component without a single causative gene. Key genetic associations include: COMT gene polymorphisms (catecholamine metabolism, pain sensitivity), serotonin transporter (5-HTT) polymorphisms (comorbid pain and depression), and opioid/dopamine receptor polymorphisms. The sibling recurrence risk ratio is approximately 13.6, comparable to many recognized genetic diseases. A large GWAS identified 26 risk loci with heritability confined to brain and neural cell types. However, no single gene is responsible — fibromyalgia is polygenic with environmental gene-environment interactions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia is polygenic — COMT, 5-HTT, opioid receptor polymorphisms. Sibling RR = 13.6 (significant but not Mendelian). COMLEX: The genetic overlap with IBS and chronic fatigue syndrome supports a shared neurobiological substrate for nociplastic conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with fibromyalgia also has irritable bowel syndrome and a long history of migraines and chronic fatigue. She asks if these conditions are related to her fibromyalgia. Which statement most accurately characterizes the relationship between fibromyalgia and these co-occurring conditions?","choices":{"A":"The co-occurrence reflects a shared autoimmune mechanism in which ANA-positive patients develop multi-organ nociplastic inflammation simultaneously","B":"Fibromyalgia causes these conditions secondarily through dysautonomia-mediated visceral hypersensitivity and reduced cortisol reactivity","C":"This cluster represents a distinct autoimmune syndrome driven by anti-neuronal antibodies that target dorsal root ganglion neurons","D":"The IBS and migraines are medication side effects from serotonergic drugs commonly used to treat fibromyalgia-associated depression","E":"Fibromyalgia commonly co-occurs with IBS, chronic fatigue syndrome, and headache disorders within a spectrum of central sensitization conditions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibromyalgia frequently co-occurs with IBS, myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS), headache disorders, and other chronic overlapping conditions. These represent chronic primary pain and nociplastic conditions that share the underlying mechanism of central sensitization. Swedish national registry data showed fibromyalgia genetic risk overlaps with IBS and CFS but differs from classic autoimmune diseases. Notably, comorbid fibromyalgia persists in up to 40% of patients with rheumatic disease despite effective immunosuppression, confirming the distinct central neurologic mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia comorbidities include IBS, ME/CFS, migraine, and PTSD — all nociplastic/central sensitization disorders. Immunosuppression does not treat fibromyalgia even when comorbid with RA. COMLEX: Recognizing fibromyalgia overlap with inflammatory disease prevents over-treatment with immunosuppressants for symptoms that are nociplastic in origin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with fibromyalgia asks why her laboratory tests keep coming back normal despite her debilitating pain. Which explanation most accurately describes why laboratory tests are normal in fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia is a disorder of central pain processing without peripheral inflammation, so ESR, CRP, and CBC are expected to be entirely normal","B":"Fibromyalgia is associated with low-grade inflammation that falls below the detection threshold of standard clinical laboratory assay sensitivity","C":"Standard ESR and CRP tests fail to detect the microglial activation and neuroinflammation that is specific to nociplastic pain conditions","D":"Fibromyalgia suppresses the acute phase response via hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis dysregulation, actively lowering inflammatory markers","E":"Patients with fibromyalgia have peripheral immune tolerance that prevents upregulation of inflammatory cytokines even during symptomatic flares"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Laboratory tests are normal in fibromyalgia because there is no peripheral inflammation, tissue damage, autoantibody production, or immune activation in the musculoskeletal system. The pathology is entirely within the CNS (altered pain processing, neurotransmitter dysregulation). ESR, CRP, CBC, CK, ANA, RF, and metabolic panel are all expected to be normal. This is not a limitation of test sensitivity — there genuinely is nothing peripheral to detect. This fundamental difference from inflammatory conditions is both diagnostically useful and explanatory for why anti-inflammatory treatments do not address the underlying mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal labs in fibromyalgia = expected finding, not a missed diagnosis. The pathology is central, not peripheral. COMLEX: An extensive workup chasing abnormal labs in fibromyalgia is unnecessary and can reinforce illness behavior; a confident clinical diagnosis based on criteria is appropriate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with chronic widespread pain, fatigue, and sleep disturbance has a positive ANA at 1:80 with a speckled pattern. RF and anti-CCP are negative. ESR and CRP are normal. Her physician is considering fibromyalgia. Which statement most accurately reflects the significance of the positive ANA in this context?","choices":{"A":"A positive ANA at any titer rules out fibromyalgia, as autoantibody production requires an inflammatory milieu incompatible with nociplastic pain","B":"A low-titer positive ANA occurs in healthy individuals and does not confirm autoimmune disease without supporting clinical or laboratory features","C":"The speckled ANA pattern is the specific pattern associated with fibromyalgia, distinguishing it from the homogeneous pattern seen in SLE","D":"Any positive ANA requires immunosuppressive therapy before attributing symptoms to fibromyalgia, to avoid missing subclinical lupus nephritis","E":"A positive ANA combined with normal ESR and CRP confirms mixed connective tissue disease as the underlying diagnosis in this patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A weakly positive ANA is found in about 1 in 20 healthy individuals (course SDL 44). A positive ANA requires clinical context: lupus criteria, complement levels, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith, anti-Sm/RNP, and organ manifestations. In this patient with normal ESR, CRP, and no other features of autoimmune disease, the positive ANA is an incidental finding and does not diagnose SLE, MCTD, or any other autoimmune disease. Fibromyalgia is characterized by negative autoantibodies, but the occasional low-titer ANA in a fibromyalgia patient does not change the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low titer ANA = not diagnostic of autoimmune disease; requires clinical correlation. Fibromyalgia = negative ANA (expected), but a low-titer incidental ANA does not exclude fibromyalgia. COMLEX: Avoid over-interpreting low ANA titers; the clinical picture drives the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman with diffuse muscle pain and perceived weakness receives laboratory testing: CK 38 units/L (normal), ESR 11 mm/hour, CRP 2.1 mg/L, CBC within normal limits, ANA negative. Which interpretation of this result pattern is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The normal CK excludes statin-induced myopathy but the normal ESR and CRP are insufficient to distinguish fibromyalgia from early PMR in this age group","B":"The pattern is nonspecific and cannot differentiate fibromyalgia from seronegative RA without dedicated imaging of the peripheral joints","C":"The combination of normal labs with diffuse pain requires muscle biopsy to exclude inclusion body myositis before confirming fibromyalgia","D":"Normal CK with normal inflammatory markers strongly points toward hypothyroidism as the most likely etiology requiring urgent thyroid function tests","E":"The completely normal laboratory profile is consistent with fibromyalgia, which does not cause elevations in CK, inflammatory markers, or any CBC abnormalities"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A completely normal CK, ESR, CRP, CBC, and negative ANA are the expected and characteristic laboratory findings in fibromyalgia. Normal CK excludes inflammatory myopathies (dermatomyositis, polymyositis) and statin-induced myopathy. Normal ESR and CRP exclude PMR and other inflammatory conditions. Normal CBC excludes anemia of chronic disease. Negative ANA makes lupus and MCTD unlikely. At age 39, PMR is essentially excluded (requires >50). This pattern supports fibromyalgia — the diagnosis is clinical and laboratory testing serves primarily to exclude other conditions. Muscle biopsy is not warranted with a normal CK.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia workup strategy = rule out inflammatory/metabolic/autoimmune causes with labs (all normal). Normal CK + normal ESR/CRP + negative ANA = fibromyalgia profile. COMLEX: Muscle biopsy is only warranted if CK is elevated suggesting inflammatory myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two women are referred for diffuse aching and stiffness. One has polymyalgia rheumatica and the other has fibromyalgia. On physical examination, which pair of findings best separates the two conditions?","choices":{"A":"PMR shows symmetric small-joint synovitis of the hands, while fibromyalgia shows marked proximal muscle wasting","B":"PMR shows tender, thickened temporal arteries, while fibromyalgia shows absent deep tendon reflexes","C":"PMR shows marked proximal muscle weakness on testing, while fibromyalgia shows large joint effusions","D":"PMR limits active shoulder abduction, while fibromyalgia shows diffuse tenderness on an otherwise normal exam","E":"PMR shows painless stiffness with full motion, while fibromyalgia shows warm, swollen tender joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In PMR, bilateral upper-arm aching with limited active shoulder abduction is particularly common, reflecting subdeltoid and subacromial bursitis and biceps tenosynovitis. In fibromyalgia the examination shows no abnormality apart from characteristic diffuse soft tissue tenderness. Distal symptoms in PMR are mild and mainly at the wrists and MCP joints, and fibromyalgia does not cause muscle wasting. Tender temporal arteries suggest GCA rather than PMR itself. The muscle in PMR is histologically normal, so stiffness, not true weakness, limits movement, and fibromyalgia does not produce joint effusions or warm swollen joints. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, clinical features, p4–5; pathogenesis, p3; differential diagnosis, fibromyalgia, p16).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = recent onset after 50, raised ESR and CRP, girdle stiffness; fibromyalgia = longstanding widespread pain, often under 50, normal ESR, CRP, and hematocrit (UpToDate, SDL 39). COMLEX: PMR stiffness can make dressing, hooking a bra, or rising from a chair difficult (UpToDate, SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 4 — characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patient 1 is a 72-year-old woman whose bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness began acutely 4 weeks ago and prevents her from raising her arms above her head. Patient 2 is a 46-year-old woman whose diffuse aching and fatigue have been present for 5 years following a stressful divorce. Which pairing of diagnosis with supporting features is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Patient 1 likely has PMR (acute onset after 50, girdle limitation); Patient 2 likely has fibromyalgia (chronic onset, stress trigger, under 50)","B":"Patient 1 is most likely fibromyalgia because severe functional limitation preventing shoulder abduction is characteristic of central sensitization","C":"Patient 2 is most likely PMR because female sex and emotional stress are dominant risk factors for new-onset polymyalgia rheumatica","D":"Both patients most likely have fibromyalgia because morning stiffness is a universal feature of central sensitization disorders at any age","E":"Both patients require identical workup including temporal artery biopsy before differentiating between PMR and fibromyalgia diagnoses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patient 1: age over 50 + acute onset (4 weeks) + proximal girdle involvement with functional limitation = PMR. Elevated acute phase reactants would confirm. Patient 2: age under 50 + chronic onset (5 years) + psychosocial trigger (divorce) + widespread symptoms = fibromyalgia. PMR requires age >50 by definition. Fibromyalgia can occur at any age and frequently follows stressful events. Temporal artery biopsy is only indicated when clinical GCA features (jaw claudication, headache, visual symptoms) are present — it is not part of the standard PMR or fibromyalgia workup.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age >50 + acute proximal stiffness + elevated markers = PMR. Age variable + chronic widespread symptoms + normal labs + psychosocial trigger = fibromyalgia. COMLEX: Temporal artery biopsy is NOT indicated in uncomplicated PMR without clinical GCA features.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which comparison between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia is most accurate when evaluating a patient with bilateral proximal aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Both conditions produce elevated ESR and CRP, but PMR additionally causes positive ANA and RF while fibromyalgia lacks any autoantibodies","B":"Fibromyalgia is associated with bilateral subacromial bursitis on ultrasound, while PMR produces diffuse soft tissue tenderness without any bursitis","C":"PMR produces elevated acute phase reactants and bursitis on imaging, while fibromyalgia produces normal labs, imaging, and muscle histology","D":"PMR occurs predominantly in patients under 50 with a chronic indolent course, while fibromyalgia predominantly affects older patients with acute onset","E":"Both conditions respond equally to the same treatment regimen, with low-dose prednisone 10 mg daily producing equivalent symptom relief in both"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key distinguishing features: PMR = elevated ESR/CRP (nearly universal), negative RF/ANA, periarticular bursitis/tenosynovitis on imaging, normal muscle biopsy, dramatic prednisone response, age >50, acute onset. Fibromyalgia = normal ESR/CRP, normal imaging, normal muscle biopsy, no prednisone response, any age (often <50), chronic onset. Both have negative RF and ANA — this is a trap in answer choices. PMR does NOT cause positive ANA or RF. Bilateral subacromial bursitis is pathognomonic for PMR on imaging, not for fibromyalgia. Prednisone works dramatically in PMR and not at all in fibromyalgia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Master the PMR vs fibromyalgia comparison table: age, onset, labs (ESR/CRP), imaging (bursitis), muscle histology, prednisone response. Both: negative RF, negative ANA. Key difference: elevated vs normal ESR/CRP. COMLEX: This is high-yield for COMLEX clinical presentations; the complete picture of both conditions is frequently tested.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia"}],"40":[{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with new-onset temporal headache undergoes temporal artery biopsy. Pathology shows granulomatous inflammation centered on the internal elastic membrane with multinucleated giant cells and elastic lamina fragmentation. Which of the following best describes the predominant inflammatory cell infiltrate in this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophils and eosinophils forming microabscesses within the media, with sparse macrophage involvement at the elastic lamina","B":"B lymphocytes and plasma cells arranged in a follicular pattern within the adventitia, with rare macrophage infiltration","C":"CD4+ T cells and macrophages, with CD4+ cells outnumbering CD8+ T cells throughout the granulomatous infiltrate","D":"Natural killer cells and mast cells releasing histamine at the internal elastic lamina, with minimal T cell contribution","E":"Neutrophils forming palisading rows around a central zone of necrosis, with eosinophils at the periphery of the lesion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GCA is a T-cell-mediated granulomatous vasculitis. The predominant infiltrate is CD4+ T cells and macrophages, with CD4 outnumbering CD8 cells. Macrophages fuse to form the characteristic multinucleated giant cells. Neutrophils and eosinophils are not the dominant cell type; those patterns are more typical of ANCA-associated or eosinophilic vasculitides. B cells in a follicular pattern are characteristic of lymphomatoid or rheumatoid vessel disease, not GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic histology = granulomatous inflammation centered on internal elastic membrane; CD4 > CD8 T cells; giant cells in ~75% of adequate biopsies. COMLEX: GCA is a Type IV hypersensitivity reaction; contrast with Type III (immune complex) seen in PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the spatial distribution of inflammatory lesions along the temporal artery in giant cell arteritis and its implication for diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Lesions are patchy and skip along the vessel, so biopsies can be falsely negative; diagnosis requires a segment longer than 1 cm","B":"Lesions are continuous and circumferential, uniformly thickening the vessel from lumen to adventitia throughout its entire length","C":"Lesions are concentrated exclusively at vessel branch points and spare the straight segments of the superficial temporal artery","D":"Lesions begin only in the adventitia and spread inward to the media over months, so early biopsies show only adventitial changes","E":"Lesions selectively target the vasa vasorum while sparing the main vessel wall, producing a pattern of outer wall ischemia only"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pathologic changes of GCA are notoriously patchy along the length of any affected vessel. This discontinuous distribution — known as skip lesions — means that even an adequate biopsy can miss active disease. For this reason, a biopsy longer than 1 cm is recommended. A negative biopsy does not exclude the diagnosis. The other descriptions would imply uniform or anatomically predictable disease, which does not characterize GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA biopsy must be >1 cm due to skip lesions; negative biopsy does not exclude diagnosis. COMLEX: Do not delay steroids for biopsy if ocular symptoms are present — vision loss can be sudden and permanent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A temporal artery biopsy shows medial attenuation with scarring, adventitial fibrosis, intimal thickening, and fragmentation of greater than 30% of the internal elastic lamina circumference. Active granulomatous inflammation and giant cells are absent. Which of the following best identifies this histologic phase of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Active acute phase of GCA, in which neutrophilic infiltration of the internal elastic lamina is the dominant finding","B":"Active granulomatous phase of GCA, in which dense CD4+ T cell infiltration and giant cell formation are expected","C":"Pre-disease susceptibility state in which the vessel wall expresses the target antigen without triggering immune activation","D":"Concurrent superinfection phase, in which neutrophils have replaced the granulomatous response following bacterial invasion","E":"Healed phase of GCA, in which granulomatous inflammation has been replaced by fibrotic scarring and elastic lamina loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The description matches healed GCA. Active disease shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells, CD4+ T cells, and macrophages centered on the internal elastic membrane. Healed lesions show medial attenuation and scarring, intimal thickening, fragmentation of greater than 30% of the internal elastic lamina circumference, and adventitial fibrosis — but no active granulomatous infiltrate. Recognizing the healed phase is important because it can be mistaken for other causes of fibrous arterial thickening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Healed GCA = medial scarring + intimal thickening + elastic lamina fragmentation >30% circumference; no active granulomas. COMLEX: Residual intimal thickening in healed GCA can cause persistent ischemia even after inflammation resolves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following patient profiles most accurately defines the population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Children under age 10 with recurrent fever and cervical lymphadenopathy of East Asian descent, in whom Kawasaki disease is the predominant vasculitis","B":"Women aged 15 to 30 with Asian or South American ancestry and diminished upper extremity pulses, in whom Takayasu arteritis is the predominant diagnosis","C":"Men over age 40 with HBsAg seropositivity and a history of necrotizing skin ulcers, in whom PAN from hepatitis B is the predominant vasculitis","D":"Adults over age 50, predominantly female, with northern European or Scandinavian ancestry, in whom GCA is the most common form of vasculitis","E":"Young adults of any sex with HLA-B27 positivity and inflammatory back pain, in whom spondyloarthritis-associated vasculitis is the predominant diagnosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GCA is rare before age 50 and peaks in incidence in the 70s. It is the most common form of vasculitis in elderly adults in the United States and Europe. Female sex and northern European or Scandinavian ancestry are well-established risk factors, with Norway having among the highest reported incidences. The other profiles describe the demographics of Kawasaki disease, Takayasu arteritis, PAN, and spondyloarthritis-associated vasculitis, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = age >50, female, Scandinavian/northern European; most common vasculitis in the elderly. COMLEX: GCA is associated with PMR; assess for shoulder/hip girdle aching in any GCA patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman develops sudden monocular vision loss. Her ESR is 112 mm/hr and she reports weeks of jaw pain with chewing and scalp tenderness. Which vessel is most directly responsible for the vision loss in giant cell arteritis, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The subclavian artery, in which upper extremity ischemia causes a subclavian steal syndrome that reflexively reduces retinal perfusion","B":"The posterior communicating artery, in which granulomatous compression of the third cranial nerve produces visual field defects","C":"The ophthalmic artery, in which granulomatous vasculitis causes ischemia of the optic nerve head, producing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","D":"The central retinal vein, in which venous stasis from markedly elevated ESR causes venous occlusion retinopathy","E":"The basilar artery, in which posterior circulation ischemia affects the occipital cortex and produces cortical blindness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When giant cell arteritis involves the temporal arteries, the ophthalmic and vertebral arteries are also likely involved. Granulomatous vasculitis of the ophthalmic artery reduces perfusion to the optic nerve head, causing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden, often permanent monocular vision loss. This is the most feared complication of GCA and mandates urgent corticosteroid treatment. The other vessels do not explain monocular vision loss in this context. Central retinal vein occlusion comes from venous thrombosis, not from a high ESR, and GCA is an arteritis whose blindness comes from ophthalmic artery involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA causes anterior ischemic optic neuropathy; start steroids IMMEDIATELY to prevent blindness. COMLEX: Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness are pathognomonic for GCA; do not wait for biopsy before treating if ocular symptoms are present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the most common form of vasculitis among elderly adults in the United States and Europe?","choices":{"A":"Polyarteritis nodosa, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults due to its predilection for renal and visceral vessel involvement","B":"Takayasu arteritis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults because of its global distribution and age-independent incidence","C":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults due to its association with ANCA and renal failure","D":"Microscopic polyangiitis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults because ANCA-associated disease peaks in the sixth and seventh decades","E":"Giant cell arteritis, which is the most common large vessel vasculitis in individuals over age 50 and is rare before that age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giant cell arteritis is the most common form of vasculitis among elderly adults in the United States and Europe. It is rare before age 50 and its incidence increases with age. The other vasculitides — PAN, Takayasu, GPA, and MPA — each have different demographic peaks and are not the most common vasculitis in elderly populations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = most common vasculitis in elderly adults in the US and Europe; Takayasu = most common large vessel vasculitis in patients under 50. COMLEX: PMR is associated with GCA in up to 50% of GCA patients; bilateral shoulder girdle aching in an elderly woman warrants screening for GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the initiating step in the immune pathogenesis of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"B cells in the media produce anti-endothelial cell antibodies that activate complement, causing fibrinoid necrosis and secondary T cell recruitment","B":"CD4+ T cells are activated in the adventitia by dendritic cells presenting an unknown vessel wall antigen, starting the granulomatous cascade","C":"Neutrophils degranulate within the vasa vasorum, releasing elastase that fragments the internal elastic lamina and attracts CD4+ T cells secondarily","D":"Mast cells in the adventitia release histamine that increases vascular permeability and drives the initial recruitment of Th1 and Th17 cells","E":"Eosinophils infiltrate the media in response to IL-5 released by Th2 cells activated by vessel wall antigens presented by adventitial macrophages"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GCA is initiated in the adventitia. Dendritic cells residing in the adventitia present an uncharacterized vessel wall antigen to CD4+ T cells entering via the vasa vasorum. This activates CD4+ T cells and initiates the granulomatous cascade. Anti-endothelial antibodies play a contributing but not initiating role. Neutrophil elastase, mast cell histamine, and eosinophil-IL-5 pathways are not the primary drivers of GCA pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA immune cascade initiates in the adventitia via CD4+ T cells activated by adventitial dendritic cells; vessel wall antigen is uncharacterized. COMLEX: T-cell-mediated (Type IV) hypersensitivity; excellent steroid response confirms immune etiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In giant cell arteritis, Th1 CD4+ cells produce IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages within the vessel media. Which of the following best describes the downstream consequences of macrophage activation in this setting?","choices":{"A":"Macrophage-derived IL-4 suppresses local inflammation and shifts the immune response toward Th2-mediated tissue healing and fibroblast activation","B":"Macrophages release IL-10 to downregulate Th17 cells and prevent progressive granuloma formation at the internal elastic lamina","C":"Activated macrophages fuse into multinucleated giant cells and secrete reactive oxygen species and MMPs that destroy the internal elastic lamina","D":"Macrophages present antigen to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, which directly kill smooth muscle cells lining the media of the temporal artery","E":"Macrophage-derived TGF-beta drives fibrous scarring of the adventitia while simultaneously inhibiting further CD4+ T cell entry via the vasa vasorum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which are the central effector cells in GCA. Activated macrophages fuse to form multinucleated giant cells, produce reactive oxygen species that cause oxidative tissue damage, and secrete matrix metalloproteinases that degrade the internal elastic lamina. This leads to the characteristic elastic lamina fragmentation seen on biopsy. IL-4, IL-10, and TGF-beta would suppress rather than drive the active inflammatory phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma from Th1 activates macrophages that form giant cells and destroy elastic lamina via reactive oxygen species and MMPs. COMLEX: Macrophage-mediated elastic lamina destruction explains the intimal thickening and luminal narrowing that can cause ischemic complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Giant cell arteritis shows an excellent therapeutic response to corticosteroids. Which of the following cytokine profiles most accurately represents the immune pathways that corticosteroids target in GCA?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 and IL-13 from Th2 cells, which drive eosinophil recruitment and mast cell degranulation at the vessel wall in an allergic-type response","B":"IL-5 and IL-9 from innate lymphoid cells, which drive eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation of the aorta and branch vessels","C":"TGF-beta and IL-10 from regulatory T cells, which drive autoantibody production against the internal elastic lamina and mediate fibrosis","D":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells and IL-17 from Th17 cells, which together drive granulomatous macrophage activation and progressive vessel wall destruction","E":"IL-2 from CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and perforin-mediated killing of endothelial cells lining the temporal and ophthalmic arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GCA involves two parallel pathways: Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma activate macrophages and drive granuloma formation, while Th17 cells producing IL-17 amplify vessel wall inflammation. Corticosteroids suppress both cytokine pathways and the downstream macrophage activation, explaining their dramatic efficacy. The other cytokine profiles describe Th2, innate lymphoid cell, regulatory T cell, and CD8 cytotoxic mechanisms, none of which are the primary drivers of GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = Th1 IFN-gamma + Th17 IL-17 as key cytokines; corticosteroids target both pathways. COMLEX: Anti-TNF therapies and IL-6 receptor antagonists (tocilizumab) are used as steroid-sparing agents in GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the key demographic distinction used to differentiate Takayasu arteritis from giant cell arteritis when their histology is identical?","choices":{"A":"Takayasu arteritis is diagnosed in patients under age 50, predominantly young women, while giant cell arteritis is diagnosed in adults over age 50","B":"Takayasu arteritis is ANCA-positive and targets medium vessels exclusively, while giant cell arteritis is ANCA-negative and targets only large vessels","C":"Takayasu arteritis is confined to Japanese and East Asian populations by definition, while giant cell arteritis is an exclusively northern European disease","D":"Takayasu arteritis targets the pulmonary circulation exclusively, while giant cell arteritis targets only the temporal and ophthalmic arteries","E":"Takayasu arteritis is caused by HBsAg immune complex deposition in vessel walls, while giant cell arteritis is a T-cell-mediated granulomatous process"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Takayasu arteritis and GCA share the same granulomatous histology and are distinguished primarily by patient age: Takayasu is diagnosed in patients under 50, while GCA is diagnosed in those over 50. Both are T-cell-mediated and ANCA-negative. Takayasu has a global distribution, not confined to Japan. Takayasu targets the aortic arch and great vessels, not the pulmonary circulation, and is not caused by HBsAg immune complexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The only distinguishing factor between GCA and Takayasu is age: under 50 = Takayasu, over 50 = GCA; histology is identical in both. COMLEX: Takayasu = pulseless disease; absent upper extremity pulses in a young woman is the classic presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman presents with reduced blood pressure and diminished pulses in both upper extremities, fatigue, and visual disturbances. Angiography shows narrowing of the aortic arch and brachiocephalic arteries. Biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells histologically indistinguishable from giant cell arteritis. Which of the following best describes the pathologic mechanism responsible for her weakened pulses?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the vasa vasorum causes intramural hematoma that compresses the vessel lumen from the outside","B":"ANCA-mediated neutrophil activation destroys the media of the subclavian arteries, causing aneurysm formation and reduced distal flow","C":"Immune complex deposition in the intima activates complement and causes segmental thrombotic occlusion of the subclavian arteries","D":"Transmural fibrous thickening with intimal hyperplasia narrows the great vessels of the aortic arch, reducing upper limb perfusion","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration of the adventitia triggers recurrent vasospasm of the subclavian arteries in cold environments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Takayasu arteritis, granulomatous inflammation causes transmural fibrous thickening with intimal hyperplasia, leading to marked narrowing or obliteration of the great vessel lumens. This structural stenosis — not vasospasm, thrombosis, or aneurysm — explains the reduced blood pressure and absent pulses in the upper extremities. This is why Takayasu is also called pulseless disease. ANCA is not present, and immune complex deposition characterizes PAN, not Takayasu.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu = intimal hyperplasia and fibrous thickening narrows aortic arch branch vessels, causing absent pulses in upper extremities. COMLEX: Claudication in legs if distal aorta involved; pulmonary hypertension if pulmonary artery involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with known Takayasu arteritis develops new-onset hypertension with a blood pressure of 162/96 mmHg. Imaging confirms bilateral renal artery stenosis. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of her hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in the glomerular mesangium activates complement, causing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis and secondary volume-dependent hypertension","B":"Granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia narrow the renal artery lumen, reducing renal perfusion and activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","C":"ANCA-mediated necrotizing glomerulonephritis destroys renal parenchyma, reducing GFR and causing hypertension through fluid retention","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the afferent arterioles mimicking PAN causes microinfarcts that reduce functional renal mass and increase renin secretion","E":"Aortic root dilation from Takayasu causes aortic valve regurgitation with volume overload, producing high-output systolic hypertension"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal arteries can be involved in Takayasu arteritis (Robbins Ch 11). Granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia narrow the renal artery lumen, causing renovascular ischemia. Reduced renal perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, leading to systemic hypertension. This is distinct from glomerulonephritis (ANCA-associated diseases) or immune complex nephritis, neither of which is the mechanism in Takayasu.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal artery involvement in Takayasu causes renovascular hypertension via RAAS activation. COMLEX: Blood pressure measurement in both arms is important in Takayasu; asymmetric readings indicate subclavian stenosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis of small to medium muscular arteries. Which of the following vascular beds is characteristically spared in PAN, regardless of disease severity?","choices":{"A":"The renal arteries, which are classically spared in PAN, with hypertension attributed solely to mesenteric inflammation in this disease","B":"The mesenteric arteries, which are not involved in PAN because small muscular arteries in the GI tract are protected from immune complex deposition","C":"The pulmonary circulation, which is characteristically spared by PAN regardless of disease severity or extent of systemic involvement","D":"The coronary arteries, which are never affected in PAN despite involvement of other medium muscular arteries throughout the body","E":"The hepatic arteries, which are spared in all forms of PAN including hepatitis B-associated disease due to portal circulation protection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hallmark feature of PAN is that it spares the pulmonary circulation. In contrast, it typically affects renal and visceral vessels — kidney, heart, liver, and gastrointestinal tract, in descending order of frequency. The sparing of pulmonary vessels distinguishes PAN from microscopic polyangiitis, which does involve pulmonary capillaries. Renal involvement is actually the most common and most morbid manifestation of PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN spares the pulmonary circulation; this is the classic board distinction from MPA and GPA. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflexes from renal and mesenteric ischemia may be an osteopathic examination topic in PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pathologic hallmark of polyarteritis nodosa on biopsy or autopsy?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse, global necrotizing inflammation involving all vessel segments uniformly, with sparing of the adventitia and internal elastic lamina","B":"Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells centered on the internal elastic membrane with elastic lamina fragmentation","C":"ANCA-mediated leukocytoclastic vasculitis involving capillaries and venules, with sparse immune deposits on immunofluorescence","D":"Segmental transmural necrotizing inflammation with fibrinoid necrosis of small to medium muscular arteries, with aneurysms or thrombosis","E":"Eosinophil-rich necrotizing vasculitis of small to medium vessels associated with asthma and marked peripheral blood eosinophilia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PAN is characterized by segmental transmural necrotizing inflammation of small to medium muscular arteries, with fibrinoid necrosis, often accompanied by aneurysm formation and thrombosis. Lesions are typically segmental and show a predilection for branch points. The granulomatous giant cell pattern describes GCA/Takayasu. ANCA-associated leukocytoclastic vasculitis describes MPA or GPA. Eosinophil-rich inflammation with asthma describes eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN = segmental transmural fibrinoid necrosis + aneurysm at branch points; all lesion stages (acute and chronic) coexist simultaneously. COMLEX: Microaneurysms on mesenteric or renal angiography are diagnostic in the appropriate clinical context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"One-third of patients with polyarteritis nodosa have an associated viral infection that contributes to pathogenesis through immune complex deposition in vessel walls. Which of the following correctly identifies this virus and the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis C virus, which triggers cryoglobulin-mediated mixed cryoglobulinemic vasculitis through immune complex deposition in small vessels","B":"Hepatitis B virus, whose HBsAg-antibody immune complexes deposit in vessel walls and trigger complement-mediated fibrinoid necrosis","C":"HIV, which causes immune complex-mediated vasculitis via gp41 antigen-antibody complex deposition in medium-sized muscular arteries","D":"Cytomegalovirus, which activates endothelial TLR2 signaling and drives neutrophilic vessel wall destruction independent of immune complex deposition","E":"Epstein-Barr virus, which promotes molecular mimicry against medium vessel smooth muscle antigens through latent LMP1 protein expression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Approximately one-third of PAN patients have chronic hepatitis B infection. HBsAg-anti-HBsAg immune complexes deposit in affected vessel walls, triggering complement activation and the inflammatory cascade that leads to necrotizing vasculitis. This is a classic example of Type III immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity. Hepatitis C is associated with cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, not classic PAN. The other viruses are not established causes of PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBsAg in ~33% of PAN patients; immune complex (Type III) mechanism; no ANCAs in PAN. COMLEX: Test for HBsAg in any PAN patient; antiviral therapy reduces disease burden in hepatitis B-associated PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with new right temporal headache, jaw claudication and an ESR of 96 mm/hr has a 2-cm temporal artery biopsy. It shows a medial infiltrate of CD4+ T cells and macrophages centered on the internal elastic lamina, with fragmentation of the lamina and intimal thickening. No multinucleated giant cells are found in any section. Which statement best interprets this biopsy?","choices":{"A":"It excludes giant cell arteritis, because multinucleated giant cells are present in every adequately sampled biopsy of the disease","B":"It shows the healed phase of giant cell arteritis, in which scarring has replaced the granulomas and active T cells have cleared","C":"It is consistent with giant cell arteritis, because giant cells appear in about 75% of adequate biopsies and can be rare or absent","D":"It shows polyarteritis nodosa, because transmural inflammation without giant cells is the defining lesion of medium-vessel vasculitis","E":"It is a false-negative skip area, because an arterial segment that lacks giant cells cannot contain active giant cell arteritis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Classic giant cell arteritis lesions show medial granulomatous inflammation centered on the internal elastic membrane, with fragmentation of the elastic lamina and an infiltrate of T cells (CD4+ more than CD8+) and macrophages. Involved segments develop intimal thickening. Multinucleated giant cells are seen in about 75% of adequately biopsied specimens, but granulomas and giant cells can be rare or absent, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis. This biopsy has the active infiltrate and lamina damage, so it supports GCA. Healed lesions show medial scarring, intimal thickening, fragmentation of more than 30% of the elastic lamina and adventitial fibrosis, without an active infiltrate. Polyarteritis nodosa is a necrotizing arteritis with fibrinoid necrosis, not a CD4+ T-cell and macrophage infiltrate centered on the elastic lamina. Skip lesions explain a normal segment; this segment is inflamed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA histology: granulomatous medial inflammation centered on the internal elastic lamina, CD4+ > CD8+ T cells and macrophages, elastic lamina fragmentation; giant cells in about 75% of adequate biopsies. COMLEX: Take a biopsy longer than 1 cm because lesions are patchy, and start glucocorticoids before the biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with biopsy-proven giant cell arteritis reports aching in his jaw muscles that starts after a minute of chewing and eases when he stops. Which structural change in the affected arteries best explains this symptom?","choices":{"A":"Aneurysmal dilation of the temporal artery diverts flow away from the masseter branches into low-resistance collateral channels","B":"Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and capillaries in the chewing muscles causes patchy infarcts that tear during mastication","C":"Eosinophil-rich necrotizing granulomas in small muscle vessels, driven by an ANCA response, obstruct flow to the masseters","D":"Immune complexes containing hepatitis B antigen inflame the masseteric arteries, causing segmental necrosis at branch points","E":"Intimal thickening in the inflamed arterial segments, at times with thrombosis, narrows the lumen and limits muscle blood flow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In giant cell arteritis the involved arterial segments develop intimal thickening, with occasional thromboses, that reduces the luminal diameter. A narrowed artery can supply the jaw muscles at rest but not during sustained chewing, so ischemic pain appears with use and fades with rest; jaw claudication is a classic GCA symptom. GCA is a granulomatous arteritis of large and medium arteries, especially those of the head. It does not cause aneurysmal steal at the temporal artery; aortic involvement (giant cell aortitis) is a separate issue. Fibrinoid necrosis of small vessels describes necrotizing small-vessel vasculitis. Eosinophil-rich necrotizing granulomas with ANCA describe eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Hepatitis B immune complexes are linked to polyarteritis nodosa, which mainly involves the vessels of the kidney, heart, liver and gastrointestinal tract.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA (females > 50 years): unilateral headache, jaw claudication, temporal artery tenderness, risk of blindness from anterior ischemic optic neuropathy; very high ESR and CRP; associated with polymyalgia rheumatica. COMLEX: Give high-dose glucocorticoids before the biopsy when GCA is suspected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed GCA has an ESR of 115 mm/hr and CRP of 42 mg/L. She asks whether her disease involves only the temporal arteries or whether other vessels are at risk. Which of the following most accurately describes the vascular distribution of GCA beyond the temporal arteries?","choices":{"A":"The vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are often involved along with the temporal artery, and the aorta can be affected as giant cell aortitis","B":"GCA is anatomically confined to the external carotid territory and does not involve the ophthalmic, vertebral, or internal carotid arteries","C":"GCA spreads in a predictable rostrocaudal pattern from the temporal artery to the subclavian artery and then the aorta over months to years","D":"Only vessels expressing a specific surface glycoprotein unique to scalp and facial arteries are susceptible to GCA, limiting spread beyond the head","E":"The internal carotid artery is always involved in GCA while the vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are characteristically protected from granulomatous inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GCA is classified as a large vessel vasculitis and can involve vessels beyond the temporal artery. When GCA is present in the temporal arteries, the vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are likely also involved. The aorta can also be affected, a variant termed giant cell aortitis. Medium and even small vessels may be affected as well. The temporal arteries are biopsied because of accessibility, not because they are uniquely vulnerable. Spread does not follow a predictable anatomical sequence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA is not limited to temporal arteries; vertebral, ophthalmic, and aorta all at risk. Giant cell aortitis = aortic involvement by GCA. COMLEX: Screen for aortic aneurysm on long-term follow-up in GCA patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman of Norwegian descent presents with 6 weeks of bilateral hip and shoulder girdle aching, over 1 hour of morning stiffness, scalp pain when brushing her hair, and new temporal headache. Her ESR is 88 mm/hr. An urgent ophthalmology consult is arranged while high-dose prednisone is started. Which of the following best identifies the rationale for the urgent ophthalmic assessment?","choices":{"A":"Prednisone raises intraocular pressure and can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma, requiring prophylactic laser iridotomy before treatment","B":"The combination of elevated ESR and Scandinavian ancestry increases the likelihood of ANCA-positive microscopic polyangiitis with glomerulonephritis affecting the retinal vessels","C":"Corticosteroids are contraindicated in GCA and the consult is needed to explore alternative anti-cytokine biologic therapies before committing to treatment","D":"Fundoscopic examination is required to confirm the GCA diagnosis by identifying granulomatous inflammation in the retinal arterioles before steroid initiation","E":"Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA can lead to sudden and permanent vision loss, and treatment with steroids must begin before this complication occurs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA can cause sudden and permanent blindness. This is the most feared complication of GCA, and affected persons must be diagnosed and treated promptly. Ophthalmology is consulted to assess for early ophthalmic involvement and to ensure prompt treatment is initiated. Steroids are not contraindicated in GCA; they are the treatment. Prednisone-induced glaucoma is a long-term concern, not an acute pre-treatment barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA + ocular symptoms = ophthalmologic emergency; start high-dose steroids immediately, do not wait for biopsy. COMLEX: Giant cell arteritis is a preventable cause of permanent vision loss in patients over age 50.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of Polymyalgia Rheumatica with Giant Cell Arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man presents with a 4-week history of headache and jaw pain when chewing. Physical examination reveals a tender, indurated left temporal artery and an ESR of 105 mm/hr. Temporal artery biopsy confirms GCA. His physician wants to counsel him about the incidence of this disease in different populations. Which population carries the highest background incidence of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Young women of East Asian descent living in Japan, where Takayasu arteritis and GCA share an overlapping peak incidence in the fifth decade","B":"Elderly adults of northern European or Scandinavian ancestry, in whom GCA incidence is markedly higher than in other ethnic populations","C":"Middle-aged African American men with HLA-B27 positivity and a prior history of uveitis and inflammatory back pain","D":"Young adult men of any ancestry with HBsAg seropositivity and prior hepatitis B virus infection in childhood","E":"Postmenopausal women with HLA-DR4 positivity of any ethnic ancestry, where HLA allele alone predicts GCA incidence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GCA shows a strong geographic and ethnic predilection for northern European and Scandinavian populations. Norway has among the highest reported incidences worldwide. Although HLA class II haplotypes are associated with GCA susceptibility, ethnicity remains the most clinically useful epidemiologic predictor. East Asian populations are more affected by Takayasu arteritis; African Americans have lower GCA incidence; HBsAg seropositivity is associated with PAN, not GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA highest incidence in Scandinavian/northern European populations; Norway has highest documented rates. COMLEX: Ethnic predilection for GCA reflects both genetic and environmental factors; Olmsted County MN (Scandinavian descent) data support this.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of Polymyalgia Rheumatica with Giant Cell Arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman with polymyalgia rheumatica, treated for 4 months, develops a temperature of 39.2°C with sweats. She has no headache, jaw pain, or visual change. Which statement best describes the significance of this fever?","choices":{"A":"Low-grade and high spiking fevers are both typical of PMR and need no further evaluation","B":"High spiking fever is rare in PMR without GCA, so it should prompt a search for GCA or infection","C":"Fever in PMR reflects tapering of glucocorticoids and is managed by raising the steroid dose","D":"Fever in PMR indicates an overlapping fibromyalgia, which commonly causes high, spiking evening fevers","E":"Fever in PMR points to late-onset rheumatoid arthritis, which RF and anti-CCP testing confirm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PMR can cause low-grade fever, but a high spiking fever is rare without coexisting GCA, so any fever in a patient with PMR should prompt a search for underlying GCA or another cause, especially infection such as endocarditis. GCA can present with only vague constitutional symptoms such as fever, fatigue, and weight loss, before headache or visual symptoms. Steroid tapering is not described as a cause of high fever. Fibromyalgia does not cause fever, and its laboratory tests are normal. Positive RF or anti-CCP would suggest late-onset RA, but RA does not explain a high spiking fever. Sources: course SDL 39 reading (UpToDate, Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, systemic signs and laboratory findings, p5–6; differential diagnosis, p16); course SDL 40 reading (Robbins Ch 11, Blood vessels, giant cell arteritis clinical features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA is rare before 50; ocular symptoms appear abruptly in about half of patients and can progress to permanent blindness (Robbins Ch 11). COMLEX: patients with pure PMR rarely have cranial ischemic events, but they need follow-up because GCA features can appear later (UpToDate, SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — overlap of PMR with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed GCA is started on prednisone. Her physician explains that the T-cell-mediated immune response in GCA involves two distinct CD4+ T cell cytokine pathways operating in parallel. Which of the following best describes both pathways and their effector mechanisms in GCA pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Th1 cells produce IL-4 to activate eosinophils in the adventitia, while Th2 cells produce IFN-gamma to stimulate endothelial proliferation in the intima","B":"Th2 cells produce IL-13 to drive adventitial fibrosis, while Th17 cells produce IL-22 to stimulate endothelial cell proliferation and intimal thickening","C":"Regulatory T cells produce TGF-beta to drive giant cell formation at the elastic lamina, while Th1 cells produce IL-10 to suppress macrophage activation","D":"Th1 cells produce IL-17 to recruit neutrophils to the elastic lamina, while CD8+ cytotoxic T cells produce IFN-gamma to activate macrophages in the media","E":"Th1 cells produce IFN-gamma to activate macrophages and form giant cells, while Th17 cells produce IL-17 to amplify local inflammation and vessel injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GCA involves two parallel T helper cell pathways. Th1 cells produce IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages, drives granuloma formation, and leads to multinucleated giant cell formation and elastic lamina destruction. Th17 cells produce IL-17, which amplifies local vascular inflammation. Both pathways contribute to progressive vessel wall injury. IL-4 and IL-13 are Th2 cytokines not central to GCA; TGF-beta drives fibrosis rather than giant cell formation; IL-10 is anti-inflammatory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA Th1/Th17 dual pathway: IFN-gamma from Th1 drives macrophage activation; IL-17 from Th17 amplifies inflammation. COMLEX: Corticosteroids suppress both pathways; tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor) is an approved steroid-sparing agent for GCA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune Cells Producing Cytokines and Specific Cytokines Released"},{"id":"sdl40_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Although the target antigen in giant cell arteritis has never been identified, pathologists classify it as an immune-mediated disease. Which set of observations most directly supports an immune cause?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatous vessel wall inflammation, a link to certain MHC class II haplotypes, and an excellent response to steroids","B":"Myeloperoxidase ANCA in most patients, few or no immune deposits in vessel walls, and frequent necrotizing glomerulonephritis","C":"Chronic hepatitis B infection in a third of patients, immune complexes in vessel walls, and lesions of different ages side by side","D":"Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome in young children, coronary artery aneurysms, and a good response to intravenous immunoglobulin","E":"Necrotizing granulomas of the respiratory tract, proteinase 3 ANCA in about 95% of cases, and necrotizing glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Giant cell arteritis probably results from a T-cell-mediated immune response to an uncharacterized vessel wall antigen, with contributions from IFN-gamma, IL-17 and anti-endothelial cell antibodies. Robbins cites three observations that strongly support an immune etiology: the characteristic granulomatous inflammation, an association with certain MHC class II haplotypes, and the excellent therapeutic response to steroids. The other sets describe different vasculitides. MPO-ANCA with few immune deposits and frequent glomerulonephritis is microscopic polyangiitis. Hepatitis B in about one-third of cases, immune complexes and lesions at different stages describe polyarteritis nodosa. Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome with coronary aneurysms in young children is Kawasaki disease. Respiratory-tract necrotizing granulomas with PR3-ANCA in 95% describe granulomatosis with polyangiitis. GCA is not ANCA-associated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = T-cell-mediated granulomatous vasculitis: unknown vessel wall antigen, MHC class II association, Th1 (IFN-gamma) and Th17 (IL-17) cytokines, steroid-responsive. COMLEX: Contrast immune-complex vasculitis (hepatitis B-associated PAN) and pauci-immune ANCA vasculitis (MPA, GPA, EGPA).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl40_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with GCA is being treated with high-dose prednisone. Her rheumatologist considers adding an IL-6 receptor antagonist (tocilizumab) to enable steroid dose reduction. Which of the following best explains the rationale for targeting the IL-6 pathway in the immunopathogenesis of GCA?","choices":{"A":"IL-6 produced exclusively by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells is the primary cytokine responsible for elastic lamina fragmentation in active GCA","B":"IL-6 directly activates mast cells in the adventitia to release histamine, causing vasodilation that promotes macrophage and T cell entry into vessel walls","C":"IL-6 is uniquely produced by multinucleated giant cells themselves and is solely responsible for the granulomatous morphology seen in GCA lesions","D":"IL-6 is a key proinflammatory cytokine in GCA that drives the acute phase response, promotes Th17 differentiation, and sustains vascular inflammation","E":"IL-6 acts as the sole activation signal for all macrophages in GCA; blocking it completely abrogates the granulomatous response in all patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-6 is a pleiotropic cytokine that plays an important role in GCA. It drives the systemic acute phase response, which explains the markedly elevated ESR and CRP seen in active GCA. IL-6 also promotes Th17 cell differentiation, linking it to the IL-17-mediated pathway of vascular inflammation. Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor) is approved for GCA as a steroid-sparing agent. IL-6 is not produced exclusively by giant cells or CD8 T cells, and it does not act as the sole macrophage activator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 in GCA drives ESR/CRP elevation and Th17 differentiation; tocilizumab is FDA-approved as steroid-sparing in GCA. COMLEX: Markedly elevated ESR in GCA reflects IL-6-driven acute phase response; ESR can be used to monitor disease activity and treatment response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune Cells Producing Cytokines and Specific Cytokines Released"},{"id":"sdl40_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman from South Korea presents with a 3-month history of fatigue, fever, and reduced blood pressure in both arms. Angiography reveals severe narrowing of the brachiocephalic, carotid, and subclavian arteries arising from the aortic arch. Biopsy of the carotid artery shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells histologically indistinguishable from giant cell arteritis. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Giant cell arteritis presenting atypically in a young Asian woman due to rare early-onset disease associated with HLA-DR4 susceptibility alleles","B":"Takayasu arteritis, a granulomatous large vessel vasculitis of patients under 50 that targets the aortic arch and branches, weakening arm pulses","C":"Polyarteritis nodosa causing necrotizing vasculitis of medium-sized arteries including the carotid and subclavian vessels with fibrinoid necrosis","D":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis causing upper respiratory granulomas and medium vessel vasculitis with ANCA positivity and glomerulonephritis","E":"Kawasaki disease presenting with coronary artery aneurysms and great vessel involvement in a young adult of East Asian descent with fever"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation is classic for Takayasu arteritis: a young woman of Asian descent with reduced upper extremity blood pressure and pulses, aortic arch and great vessel involvement on angiography, and granulomatous histology. Age under 50 with aortic arch involvement distinguishes Takayasu from GCA. PAN causes segmental fibrinoid necrosis without granulomas, is ANCA-negative, and does not involve large vessels. GPA causes upper respiratory granulomas and is ANCA-positive. Kawasaki typically affects young children (Robbins Ch 11: 80% are younger than 5 years; FA p478: Asian children under 4 years).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu = young woman + pulseless + aortic arch stenosis + granulomatous histology identical to GCA. COMLEX: Pulseless disease; absent radial pulse in a woman under 40 is the classic board scenario for Takayasu.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with Takayasu arteritis diagnosed 3 years ago has weak radial pulses and a 30 mm Hg difference in blood pressure between her arms. She now reports progressive dyspnea on exertion. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle and raised pulmonary artery systolic pressure, with normal left ventricular function and normal mitral and aortic valves. Which complication best explains her dyspnea?","choices":{"A":"Arteritis of the pulmonary arteries, a common site of Takayasu involvement that can cause pulmonary hypertension","B":"Narrowing of the coronary ostia, causing silent myocardial infarction and ischemic failure of the left ventricle","C":"Dilation of the aortic root with aortic valve insufficiency, causing chronic volume overload of the left ventricle","D":"Bilateral renal artery stenosis, causing renin-driven salt retention and a high-output failure of the left heart","E":"Granulomatous interstitial lung disease from extension of the arteritis into the alveolar walls and the septa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Takayasu arteritis classically involves the aortic arch and its great vessels, which explains the weak upper-extremity pulses. In a third of patients it also affects the rest of the aorta and its branches, and Robbins reports pulmonary artery involvement in half of cases; pulmonary artery disease can cause pulmonary hypertension, which fits a dilated right ventricle with high pulmonary pressure. Narrowing of the coronary ostia can cause myocardial infarction, and aortic root involvement occasionally causes aortic valve insufficiency, but both would affect the left ventricle or the aortic valve, which are normal here. Renal artery involvement causes systemic hypertension in about half of patients, not right-heart failure. Takayasu arteritis is a disease of medium and large arteries; the sources describe arterial narrowing, not interstitial lung disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu arteritis: granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis in patients < 50 years (First Aid: Asian females < 40); aortic arch and great vessels give pulseless disease; also pulmonary arteries (pulmonary hypertension), coronary ostia (MI) and renal arteries (hypertension). COMLEX: Compare blood pressure in both arms; a large difference and weak radial pulses are clues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist describes the histologic progression of Takayasu arteritis from earliest to most advanced in the same vessel wall over time. Which of the following correctly sequences the histologic progression of Takayasu arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the media → intimal hyperplasia → adventitial mononuclear infiltration → granuloma formation at the elastic lamina","B":"Intimal hyperplasia → medial granuloma formation → adventitial fibrosis → complete luminal obliteration without prior mononuclear infiltration","C":"Giant cell formation at the intima-media junction → medial necrosis → spread to adventitial mononuclear infiltrates → collagen deposition","D":"Luminal thrombosis → subendothelial fibrin deposition → medial inflammation → periadventitial granulomas as the final stage","E":"Adventitial cuffing of vasa vasorum → medial mononuclear inflammation → giant cell granulomas with medial necrosis → scarring of all layers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Takayasu arteritis progresses histologically in a characteristic outside-in pattern. The earliest changes are adventitial mononuclear infiltrates with perivascular cuffing of the vasa vasorum. This progresses to intense mononuclear inflammation in the media, followed by granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and patchy medial necrosis. The end-stage is collagenous scarring of all three vessel layers. This outside-in progression parallels the pathogenesis of GCA, where the adventitia is also the initiating site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu histology progresses from adventitial infiltrates to medial granulomas to collagenous scarring; histologically identical to GCA at any stage. COMLEX: End-stage fibrotic scarring of all three layers causes the rigid, narrowed vessel responsible for pulseless disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents with a 2-month history of new-onset hypertension, diffuse abdominal pain with bloody stools, diffuse myalgias, and foot drop. Laboratory studies show neutrophilia, elevated ESR, and positive HBsAg serology. ANCA testing is negative. Mesenteric angiography demonstrates multiple microaneurysms. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with systemic vasculitis, GI involvement, and ANCA-positive glomerulonephritis affecting small and medium vessels","B":"Hepatitis B-associated polyarteritis nodosa, in which HBsAg immune complexes in medium muscular arteries cause necrotizing vasculitis and microaneurysms","C":"Microscopic polyangiitis with pANCA positivity causing small vessel necrotizing glomerulonephritis and pulmonary capillaritis with GI involvement","D":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with GI vasculitis, peripheral eosinophilia, and asthma as the primary triad of findings","E":"Takayasu arteritis involving the mesenteric and renal arteries in a young adult with co-existent hepatitis B-associated aortitis and peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation is classic for PAN: a young adult man with hypertension, GI ischemia, myalgias, mononeuritis multiplex, positive HBsAg, ANCA negativity, and mesenteric microaneurysms on angiography. One-third of PAN patients have chronic hepatitis B, with HBsAg immune complexes driving the vasculitis. Microaneurysms on angiography are a classic diagnostic finding. GPA and MPA are ANCA-positive. Eosinophilic GPA requires asthma and eosinophilia. Takayasu affects large vessels and does not cause microaneurysms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN = HBsAg in 33% + ANCA negative + microaneurysms on angiography + spares lungs + renal/GI/peripheral nerve involvement. COMLEX: Mononeuritis multiplex in PAN reflects ischemic injury to peripheral motor nerves from necrotizing vasculitis of vasa nervorum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist reviews a renal biopsy from a patient with suspected PAN. Multiple sections reveal vessels at different stages of activity simultaneously: some arteries show active fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophilic infiltrate, others show only fibrotic wall thickening with chronic mononuclear cells, and still others appear unaffected. Which of the following correctly interprets this mixed-stage histologic pattern?","choices":{"A":"The coexistence of active and healed lesions indicates that the patient has two simultaneous vascular diseases operating at different rates in the same kidney","B":"The uninvolved vessels confirm that only the main renal artery trunk is affected in PAN; the interlobular arteries are always spared in this disease","C":"The active fibrinoid necrosis in some vessels and fibrosis in others represents alternating cycles of bacterial infection and host-mediated healing responses","D":"PAN characteristically shows lesions at all stages in the same tissue, reflecting continuous, recurrent vascular injury rather than one synchronous episode","E":"The mixed-stage pattern is found exclusively in hepatitis B-associated PAN and does not occur in ANCA-negative idiopathic forms of polyarteritis nodosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A hallmark of PAN is that all stages of vascular lesion activity — from active fibrinoid necrosis and neutrophilic infiltration to healed fibrous thickening — coexist simultaneously in different vessels or even within the same vessel. This mixed-stage pattern reflects ongoing and recurrent injury rather than a single synchronized acute event. It is characteristic of PAN in both HBsAg-associated and idiopathic forms, and it helps distinguish PAN from conditions that cause uniform acute injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN hallmark = simultaneous coexistence of active, healing, and healed lesions in the same tissue; indicates ongoing injury. COMLEX: Mixed-stage PAN lesions are best identified on renal or nerve biopsy; mesenteric angiography showing microaneurysms is an alternative diagnostic approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with polyarteritis nodosa. Her physician discusses prognosis and initiates immunosuppressive therapy. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary organ system responsible for mortality in PAN and the approximate 5-year survival achieved with immunosuppression?","choices":{"A":"Renal artery involvement leading to rapidly progressive hypertension and renal failure is the most common cause of mortality; immunosuppression provides up to 80% 5-year survival","B":"Pulmonary artery involvement causing pulmonary hemorrhage is the leading cause of death in PAN; oxygen therapy and anticoagulation provide approximately 60% 5-year survival","C":"Coronary artery involvement alone is responsible for all PAN-related mortality; stenting of affected coronary segments achieves 95% 5-year survival in treated patients","D":"ANCA-mediated crescentic glomerulonephritis is the dominant cause of renal death in PAN; plasma exchange combined with steroids provides 90% 5-year survival","E":"Mesenteric artery involvement causing bowel infarction accounts for all PAN mortality; surgical resection of affected bowel segments achieves 70% 5-year survival"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal involvement is a major cause of mortality in PAN, with rapidly accelerating hypertension from renal artery disease being a defining clinical feature. The GI, peripheral nervous system, and coronary vasculature are also affected but renal disease drives most mortality. Untreated, PAN is typically fatal. With immunosuppression, 5-year survival reaches approximately 80%. PAN characteristically spares the pulmonary circulation, making pulmonary hemorrhage an unlikely direct cause of death. ANCA is not associated with PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN prognosis: untreated = typically fatal; immunosuppression achieves ~80% 5-year survival; renal involvement is major mortality driver. COMLEX: PAN requires both steroids and cyclophosphamide in severe cases; screen and treat underlying hepatitis B to reduce disease burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"}]};
